00:00:55.610 --> 00:00:57.560
an author and vegan advocate.

00:00:57.560 --> 00:01:01.070
Most known as Earthling Ed on the internet.

00:01:01.070 --> 00:01:02.760
And although Ed is only 27,

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:04.980
he's quickly become one of the most compelling

00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:07.970
and powerful voices in the vegan movement.

00:01:07.970 --> 00:01:10.260
A millennial Peter singer, if you will,

00:01:10.260 --> 00:01:13.950
who, in addition to accumulating bazillions of views

00:01:13.950 --> 00:01:17.960
here on YouTube, has lectured at many UK universities,

00:01:17.960 --> 00:01:19.500
including Cambridge.

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:22.040
He served as a guest lecturer at Harvard.

00:01:22.040 --> 00:01:24.670
He spoken at several Ivy league institutions

00:01:24.670 --> 00:01:25.780
and major corporations

00:01:25.780 --> 00:01:28.630
like Google, Facebook, and The Economist,

00:01:28.630 --> 00:01:31.850
and has appeared in many mainstream media outlets

00:01:31.850 --> 00:01:33.268
like the BBC.

00:01:33.268 --> 00:01:35.920
The occasion for today's conversation

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:40.174
is Ed's tremendous new, all things vegan book entitled,

00:01:40.174 --> 00:01:42.310
"This Is Vegan Propaganda:

00:01:42.310 --> 00:01:44.530
And Other Lies the Meat Industry Tells You"

00:01:44.530 --> 00:01:48.103
which is, let's face it just an incredible title

00:01:48.103 --> 00:01:51.400
vested only by the content there in.

00:01:51.400 --> 00:01:55.910
So today we talk, obviously all things V-E-G-A-N,

00:01:55.910 --> 00:02:00.020
including the moral philosophy that underpins it.

00:02:00.020 --> 00:02:02.150
Ed's rebuttals to the many common arguments

00:02:02.150 --> 00:02:03.930
against veganism,

00:02:03.930 --> 00:02:05.930
the powerful ways in which cultural forces

00:02:05.930 --> 00:02:07.630
drive cognitive dissonance

00:02:07.630 --> 00:02:10.320
around our food and consumer choices,

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:12.670
the ethical and environmental implications

00:02:12.670 --> 00:02:14.430
of those decisions,

00:02:14.430 --> 00:02:16.880
how to be an effective advocate, very important,

00:02:16.880 --> 00:02:18.140
and many other topics.

00:02:18.140 --> 00:02:21.210
So if you find yourself vegan curious

00:02:21.210 --> 00:02:24.460
but haven't yet found the will to commit,

00:02:24.460 --> 00:02:26.990
I suspect Ed just might be the catalyst

00:02:26.990 --> 00:02:28.540
you've been waiting for.

00:02:28.540 --> 00:02:29.620
We cover a lot today,

00:02:29.620 --> 00:02:32.950
but there's so much more to Ed and his message.

00:02:32.950 --> 00:02:34.700
So if you find yourself eager

00:02:34.700 --> 00:02:36.630
to continue your investigation,

00:02:36.630 --> 00:02:39.490
I encourage you to not only pick up Ed's new book,

00:02:39.490 --> 00:02:41.140
this is vegan propaganda,

00:02:41.140 --> 00:02:43.870
but to also spend time on his YouTube channel,

00:02:43.870 --> 00:02:45.630
which is linked in the description below,

00:02:45.630 --> 00:02:49.890
because it's all their people, all of it is there.

00:02:49.890 --> 00:02:51.360
I think that's enough for me,

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:53.210
so please hit that subscribe button

00:02:53.210 --> 00:02:56.610
and without further ado, I give you Ed Winters,

00:02:56.610 --> 00:02:58.773
AKA Earthling Ed.

00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:01.760
Its so nice to meet you.

00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:03.680
I've been wanting to meet you for a very long time.

00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:05.150
It's somewhat surprising

00:03:05.150 --> 00:03:07.340
that we haven't crossed paths in person.

00:03:07.340 --> 00:03:11.280
But I've been following your work for quite some time

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:13.760
and always knew that I wanted to have you here.

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:15.600
And we're in the perfect moment to have you here

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:18.637
because you have this amazing new book coming out,

00:03:18.637 --> 00:03:20.080
"This Is Vegan Propaganda"

00:03:20.080 --> 00:03:22.280
which is a title for the ages.

00:03:22.280 --> 00:03:24.470
Like this is made for the internet.

00:03:24.470 --> 00:03:28.530
Like it's so cheeky and obviously contemplates

00:03:28.530 --> 00:03:33.400
the fact that the first thing that a naysayer might say

00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:35.280
is that this book is vegan propaganda.

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:36.113
Yeah.
Right.

00:03:36.113 --> 00:03:37.773
Exactly.

00:03:37.773 --> 00:03:39.173
That was the idea behind it.

00:03:40.860 --> 00:03:43.250
When we were kind of coming up with the title for the book,

00:03:43.250 --> 00:03:46.230
we went through so many different iterations for it

00:03:46.230 --> 00:03:48.790
and we kind of landed on this idea of propaganda.

00:03:48.790 --> 00:03:49.770
'Cause I'd made a video

00:03:49.770 --> 00:03:51.910
and I'd used the word vegan propaganda

00:03:51.910 --> 00:03:53.730
as kind of like a thing that people say

00:03:53.730 --> 00:03:55.600
about the information.

00:03:55.600 --> 00:03:56.910
And we were like, let's run with that.

00:03:56.910 --> 00:03:57.743
There's something there,

00:03:57.743 --> 00:03:59.370
so like we messed around of it a little bit.

00:03:59.370 --> 00:04:02.110
And we are like this vegan propaganda's a good

00:04:02.110 --> 00:04:04.430
kind of tongue in cheek, disarming of that phrase

00:04:04.430 --> 00:04:06.820
that we kind of hear often as vegans.

00:04:06.820 --> 00:04:08.130
I think it's perfect.
Oh good.

00:04:08.130 --> 00:04:09.680
I love it.
Oh good.

00:04:09.680 --> 00:04:12.540
In thinking about you and your advocacy

00:04:12.540 --> 00:04:15.470
and the way you sort of comport yourself in the world

00:04:15.470 --> 00:04:18.948
and carry this message that you care about so deeply,

00:04:18.948 --> 00:04:23.560
I think that what is so indelibly powerful

00:04:23.560 --> 00:04:27.240
about you specifically, is this very intentional,

00:04:27.240 --> 00:04:30.253
elegant style of communication that you have,

00:04:33.470 --> 00:04:38.470
and this level of like patience and unflappable, equanimity,

00:04:38.560 --> 00:04:41.040
like equanimity really is like the touchdown

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:43.090
and how you kind of interact with people.

00:04:45.130 --> 00:04:47.280
When you talk about veganism

00:04:47.280 --> 00:04:50.460
and address the Pantheon of arguments

00:04:50.460 --> 00:04:52.480
that get hurled in your direction

00:04:52.480 --> 00:04:55.473
as a defender of this way of life.

00:04:57.700 --> 00:05:02.700
And in that there are so many different aspects

00:05:02.910 --> 00:05:04.490
of the vegan lifestyle.

00:05:04.490 --> 00:05:07.250
There's health, there's in the environment sustainability,

00:05:07.250 --> 00:05:09.580
there's compassion, all of that.

00:05:09.580 --> 00:05:11.450
But your primary animating force

00:05:11.450 --> 00:05:14.880
is really rooted in ethics and moral responsibility.

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:16.250
So I think a good place to start

00:05:16.250 --> 00:05:19.810
is just to unpack that a little bit

00:05:19.810 --> 00:05:22.563
and kind of how you got into this to begin with.

00:05:23.540 --> 00:05:27.550
The way that I see veganism is that it is a moral issue.

00:05:27.550 --> 00:05:28.710
I often say to people like,

00:05:28.710 --> 00:05:29.960
even if eating animal products

00:05:29.960 --> 00:05:31.990
was just as bad for the environment,

00:05:31.990 --> 00:05:33.900
or the exact same for the environment

00:05:33.900 --> 00:05:36.100
and had no negative repercussions on your health

00:05:36.100 --> 00:05:38.370
and it was exactly the same both ways, even though it isn't,

00:05:38.370 --> 00:05:40.780
but even if it was that case,

00:05:40.780 --> 00:05:44.260
there's still a moral reason to be vegan, the animals.

00:05:44.260 --> 00:05:46.970
And so for me, I've always come at it from that moral angle

00:05:46.970 --> 00:05:49.470
and I think that the other arguments

00:05:49.470 --> 00:05:52.580
really supplement and solidify that reason to be vegan

00:05:52.580 --> 00:05:54.580
in a way that kind of makes it undeniably,

00:05:54.580 --> 00:05:55.420
the right thing to do.

00:05:55.420 --> 00:05:56.970
But that moral framework

00:05:56.970 --> 00:05:58.620
has always been really important to me

00:05:58.620 --> 00:06:00.060
and it was the decision,

00:06:00.060 --> 00:06:02.940
or that was the kind of thing that led me to the decision

00:06:02.940 --> 00:06:04.310
to change myself.

00:06:04.310 --> 00:06:07.002
And I went vegan in 2015

00:06:07.002 --> 00:06:10.000
and had been vegetarian for about eight months before them.

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:11.060
And the reason I went vegetarian

00:06:11.060 --> 00:06:12.460
is because I came across this story

00:06:12.460 --> 00:06:15.220
about a truck carrying around 6 1/2 thousand chickens

00:06:15.220 --> 00:06:17.970
crashing on the way to a slaughterhouse near Manchester,

00:06:17.970 --> 00:06:19.950
which is a city in Northern England.

00:06:19.950 --> 00:06:21.810
And I was reading this story

00:06:21.810 --> 00:06:25.330
and just feeling so dreadfully, sorry for these chickens,

00:06:25.330 --> 00:06:28.140
the journalist was describing how many of them had died,

00:06:28.140 --> 00:06:29.640
there were more who were alive,

00:06:29.640 --> 00:06:31.810
but they were on the side of the road. They were mutilated.

00:06:31.810 --> 00:06:33.873
They had broken bones, broken wings.

00:06:33.873 --> 00:06:37.520
And I realized that they could suffer,

00:06:37.520 --> 00:06:39.600
which is such a strange thing to realize,

00:06:39.600 --> 00:06:41.980
'cause obviously the animals who we farm can suffer,

00:06:41.980 --> 00:06:44.750
but how often do we ever stop and think about that?

00:06:44.750 --> 00:06:46.240
I never had.

00:06:46.240 --> 00:06:47.680
And so I was thinking about these chickens

00:06:47.680 --> 00:06:49.310
who were suffering and then I realized,

00:06:49.310 --> 00:06:50.700
well, hang on a minute.

00:06:50.700 --> 00:06:53.380
It's not this the chickens on the side of the road,

00:06:53.380 --> 00:06:56.480
the destination they were heading to is hardly much better,

00:06:56.480 --> 00:06:58.970
potentially even worse than for many of the chickens

00:06:58.970 --> 00:07:01.050
because of what they endure in these slaughterhouses.

00:07:01.050 --> 00:07:05.420
So I felt like a hypocrite because in my fridge was a KFC

00:07:05.420 --> 00:07:06.820
and I used to love fried chicken.

00:07:06.820 --> 00:07:08.160
It was like my favorite food.

00:07:08.160 --> 00:07:09.470
I had a KFC outlet

00:07:09.470 --> 00:07:11.760
that was about maybe a five, 10 minute walk

00:07:11.760 --> 00:07:13.480
from where I used to live in London.

00:07:13.480 --> 00:07:15.090
And I went there so often

00:07:15.090 --> 00:07:17.200
that I became very accustomed to the workers

00:07:17.200 --> 00:07:18.400
and they knew who I was,

00:07:18.400 --> 00:07:21.130
and we had this kind of like rapport going

00:07:21.130 --> 00:07:25.100
and it was just this kind of biweekly, twice weekly

00:07:25.100 --> 00:07:29.210
kind of pilgrimage that I went on to get my fried chicken.

00:07:29.210 --> 00:07:31.970
But anyway, my fridge was some leftover fried chicken,

00:07:31.970 --> 00:07:33.700
but now I'm empathizing with chickens

00:07:33.700 --> 00:07:35.310
in a way I never have before.

00:07:35.310 --> 00:07:36.590
And I thought, hang on a minute,

00:07:36.590 --> 00:07:38.630
what are my values when it comes to animals?

00:07:38.630 --> 00:07:41.187
And one thing I often ask people is,

00:07:41.187 --> 00:07:42.760
are you against animal cruelty?

00:07:42.760 --> 00:07:44.167
And we all say that we are,

00:07:44.167 --> 00:07:46.760
and people who commit cruel acts to animals

00:07:46.760 --> 00:07:49.060
are seen as some of the worst people that can exist.

00:07:49.060 --> 00:07:52.420
And we really look down upon violence towards pets

00:07:52.420 --> 00:07:54.290
like dogs and cats and, and such

00:07:54.290 --> 00:07:56.140
but we turn a blind eye to the cruelty

00:07:56.140 --> 00:07:57.880
that's inflicted on farm animals

00:07:57.880 --> 00:08:01.440
and indeed animals that are exploited in other ways as well.

00:08:01.440 --> 00:08:03.390
So when I ask people, are you against animal cruelty?

00:08:03.390 --> 00:08:04.600
What I'm trying to do, I suppose,

00:08:04.600 --> 00:08:07.210
is highlight that contradiction our values.

00:08:07.210 --> 00:08:09.840
When we say about cruelty and I say to people,

00:08:09.840 --> 00:08:12.700
can you define what that means to be cruel to someone?

00:08:12.700 --> 00:08:15.260
People will always say that it's about causing physical

00:08:15.260 --> 00:08:17.560
or mental emotional harm.

00:08:17.560 --> 00:08:19.120
It's about doing something that unnecessary,

00:08:19.120 --> 00:08:22.113
that negatively contributes to someone else's wellbeing.

00:08:22.113 --> 00:08:24.870
And then when we think about what we do to animals,

00:08:24.870 --> 00:08:25.970
of course, it's cruel.

00:08:25.970 --> 00:08:28.180
We mutilate them, we exploit them,

00:08:28.180 --> 00:08:29.810
we forcibly impregnate them.

00:08:29.810 --> 00:08:31.320
We take their babies away from them.

00:08:31.320 --> 00:08:33.960
And then we take their life from them prematurely

00:08:33.960 --> 00:08:35.190
for an unnecessary reason.

00:08:35.190 --> 00:08:37.580
I mean, to me, that's like the definition of being cruel

00:08:37.580 --> 00:08:38.670
to an animal.

00:08:38.670 --> 00:08:41.920
So I realize that in that moment, thinking about the KFC,

00:08:41.920 --> 00:08:43.000
thinking about the chickens

00:08:43.000 --> 00:08:47.460
and realizing that there was this kind of disalignment

00:08:47.460 --> 00:08:49.977
in the person I thought I was when it came to animals

00:08:49.977 --> 00:08:50.980
and the person I actually was.

00:08:50.980 --> 00:08:54.040
And so I kind of reached this fork in the road,

00:08:54.040 --> 00:08:56.070
if your part in the (indistinct), but this fork in the road

00:08:56.070 --> 00:08:58.819
where I could choose to kind of bury my head in the sands,

00:08:58.819 --> 00:09:01.380
kind of hopefully repress those feelings

00:09:01.380 --> 00:09:02.430
and just get on with my life

00:09:02.430 --> 00:09:04.002
and not worry about animals anymore,

00:09:04.002 --> 00:09:06.560
or I could accept this something not quite right

00:09:06.560 --> 00:09:07.880
with my values and my actions.

00:09:07.880 --> 00:09:10.750
And I chose the latter and went vegetarian

00:09:10.750 --> 00:09:13.157
'cause I didn't know anything about dairy or eggs.

00:09:13.157 --> 00:09:15.010
And then I saw "Earthlings",

00:09:15.010 --> 00:09:17.530
which is a documentary that exposes

00:09:17.530 --> 00:09:19.930
what happens in Us farms and slaughterhouses.

00:09:19.930 --> 00:09:22.620
The movie we all begrudgingly watch.

00:09:22.620 --> 00:09:23.950
Exactly.
We have to get dragged

00:09:23.950 --> 00:09:26.440
to watching it and then we're never the same.

00:09:26.440 --> 00:09:28.070
It is a hard watch, isn't it?

00:09:28.070 --> 00:09:31.530
it's like an hour and 40 minutes of objective,

00:09:31.530 --> 00:09:33.830
I guess, undercover, hidden camera footage

00:09:33.830 --> 00:09:35.250
that just shows what happens to animal.

00:09:35.250 --> 00:09:37.870
And after that, I went and spent time

00:09:37.870 --> 00:09:41.120
with Ruper, the hamster who was my first real pet.

00:09:41.120 --> 00:09:42.560
And I don't like to use the word pet so much,

00:09:42.560 --> 00:09:44.630
but more like companion animal.

00:09:44.630 --> 00:09:46.090
He was kind of my companion.

00:09:46.090 --> 00:09:48.190
And I had Ruper in my hands

00:09:48.190 --> 00:09:50.400
and I was looking at Ruper, the hamster

00:09:50.400 --> 00:09:51.970
and I gave him some broccoli

00:09:51.970 --> 00:09:53.340
'cause broccoli was his favorite food.

00:09:53.340 --> 00:09:55.230
He loved broccoli, absolutely loved it.

00:09:55.230 --> 00:09:57.490
So I gave him and broccoli and I was looking at Ruper,

00:09:57.490 --> 00:09:59.420
eating this broccoli with his cute little paws.

00:09:59.420 --> 00:10:00.390
He always looked so adorable.

00:10:00.390 --> 00:10:02.000
And I was looking at him thinking

00:10:02.000 --> 00:10:03.070
there's so much about Ruper

00:10:03.070 --> 00:10:05.890
that creates a moral worth for him.

00:10:05.890 --> 00:10:06.800
He's an individual,

00:10:06.800 --> 00:10:09.200
he has likes and dislikes and he loved the broccoli,

00:10:09.200 --> 00:10:11.940
but he didn't like other things like kale, for example.

00:10:11.940 --> 00:10:13.160
He had likes and dislikes

00:10:13.160 --> 00:10:15.730
things that made him Ruper, the hamster

00:10:15.730 --> 00:10:17.690
and I up all the animals who were exploited

00:10:17.690 --> 00:10:20.410
in all the different ways that we exploit them.

00:10:20.410 --> 00:10:21.570
I wasn't eating meat,

00:10:21.570 --> 00:10:23.160
but I was still consuming dairy and eggs.

00:10:23.160 --> 00:10:24.930
I was still perpetuating systems

00:10:24.930 --> 00:10:27.530
that exploits animals in other means.

00:10:27.530 --> 00:10:29.690
And it really dawned on meat in that moment

00:10:29.690 --> 00:10:31.970
that the issue of what we do to animals

00:10:31.970 --> 00:10:33.750
isn't just really about food,

00:10:33.750 --> 00:10:35.660
Food is a symptom of the problem.

00:10:35.660 --> 00:10:39.010
The problem is mentality that creates the justification

00:10:39.010 --> 00:10:41.310
for these systems to exist in the first place.

00:10:41.310 --> 00:10:43.020
The fact that we view non-human animals

00:10:43.020 --> 00:10:46.590
or these non-human animals with such little worth,

00:10:46.590 --> 00:10:47.580
that we can then justify

00:10:47.580 --> 00:10:49.560
doing everything that we do to them.

00:10:49.560 --> 00:10:50.393
That's the problem.

00:10:50.393 --> 00:10:52.190
And I realize that veganism

00:10:52.190 --> 00:10:54.530
isn't just about eating plant-based food,

00:10:54.530 --> 00:10:56.430
it's about challenging that mindset,

00:10:56.430 --> 00:10:59.950
that value use non human animals as having such little worth

00:10:59.950 --> 00:11:02.010
that we can do these unspeakably cruel things to them

00:11:02.010 --> 00:11:05.020
and not bat an eyed about I most of the time.

00:11:05.020 --> 00:11:07.730
A lot of people have an analogous experience

00:11:07.730 --> 00:11:12.578
where they're witness to some type of animal cruelty

00:11:12.578 --> 00:11:14.960
and they're compelled to kind of reckon

00:11:14.960 --> 00:11:17.698
with how that measures up against their value system.

00:11:17.698 --> 00:11:21.860
But not that many people use that as a lever

00:11:21.860 --> 00:11:24.180
or a tipping point to actually make change.

00:11:24.180 --> 00:11:25.510
I mean, there's so much packed into that.

00:11:25.510 --> 00:11:27.900
There's the level of cognitive dissonance,

00:11:27.900 --> 00:11:31.550
this idea like, we all flinch and look away from things

00:11:31.550 --> 00:11:33.770
like "Earthlings" and slaughterhouse videos

00:11:33.770 --> 00:11:35.910
and stuff like that for the very reason.

00:11:35.910 --> 00:11:36.840
And you talk about this,

00:11:36.840 --> 00:11:40.430
like the very reason that it will compel us to confront

00:11:40.430 --> 00:11:41.410
that dissonance.

00:11:41.410 --> 00:11:42.430
And if we do that,

00:11:42.430 --> 00:11:45.080
then perhaps we either need to make that change

00:11:45.080 --> 00:11:46.520
that we don't wanna make,

00:11:46.520 --> 00:11:50.130
or we have to live with that level of disconnect,

00:11:50.130 --> 00:11:51.780
which creates kind of like

00:11:51.780 --> 00:11:55.180
just a lack of integration with just being human.

00:11:55.180 --> 00:11:57.120
So you had this moment.

00:11:57.120 --> 00:12:01.350
What was it about your background or leading up to that,

00:12:01.350 --> 00:12:03.670
that you were kind of primed to make that change

00:12:03.670 --> 00:12:05.920
that so many struggle to make?

00:12:05.920 --> 00:12:08.520
Well, I think that we often as vegans,

00:12:08.520 --> 00:12:10.630
we often think of this one moment,

00:12:10.630 --> 00:12:12.120
I saw "Earthlings" that made me vegan,

00:12:12.120 --> 00:12:14.450
but what we forget or don't often realize

00:12:14.450 --> 00:12:15.750
is that throughout our whole life,

00:12:15.750 --> 00:12:17.050
there are all these little moments

00:12:17.050 --> 00:12:20.450
that are building up to this, this kind of realization.

00:12:20.450 --> 00:12:22.120
And so throughout my whole life,

00:12:22.120 --> 00:12:24.070
I was raised with this mentality

00:12:24.070 --> 00:12:27.660
that animals should be cared for.

00:12:27.660 --> 00:12:29.639
That people that do bad things to animals are wrong.

00:12:29.639 --> 00:12:31.850
That we should try and protect our environment.

00:12:31.850 --> 00:12:33.170
I was raised with these values.

00:12:33.170 --> 00:12:34.460
And I was raised with the,

00:12:34.460 --> 00:12:35.570
I guess the concept

00:12:35.570 --> 00:12:38.270
that a world we've reduced suffering is preferable.

00:12:38.270 --> 00:12:40.230
So this was kinda like the mentality I had,

00:12:40.230 --> 00:12:42.910
but I'd never really connected all of these components

00:12:42.910 --> 00:12:46.385
wit being vegan or with the consequence of not being vegan.

00:12:46.385 --> 00:12:49.300
So for me, it was just like this, I suppose,

00:12:49.300 --> 00:12:51.500
domino effect of thinking about

00:12:51.500 --> 00:12:53.070
these different points in my life.

00:12:53.070 --> 00:12:56.010
For example, I stopped going to zoos

00:12:56.010 --> 00:12:58.910
before I stopped eating meats and dairy and eggs.

00:12:58.910 --> 00:13:00.480
And the reason I stopped going to zoos

00:13:00.480 --> 00:13:03.050
is because I had seen the documentary "Blackfish"

00:13:03.050 --> 00:13:05.170
and the documentary "Blackfish"

00:13:05.170 --> 00:13:08.050
made me not want to go to aquariums.

00:13:08.050 --> 00:13:10.510
But then I went to Barcelona zoo and in Barcelona zoo,

00:13:10.510 --> 00:13:13.980
I saw a bear and this bear just looked so solemn and so sad.

00:13:13.980 --> 00:13:15.619
That it would just sat down

00:13:15.619 --> 00:13:19.000
in this very small enclosure looking around.

00:13:19.000 --> 00:13:20.580
And I kind of followed their eyes,

00:13:20.580 --> 00:13:21.780
they looked around the enclosure

00:13:21.780 --> 00:13:24.260
and looked at the four walls they were trapped in.

00:13:24.260 --> 00:13:26.160
And it dawned on me that these animals

00:13:26.160 --> 00:13:28.040
are also being kept in captivity

00:13:28.040 --> 00:13:30.180
like the Orca at sea world are.

00:13:30.180 --> 00:13:32.030
So I left Barcelona Zoo

00:13:32.030 --> 00:13:34.040
and never went to another zoo after that.

00:13:34.040 --> 00:13:35.500
So I had these kind of moments

00:13:35.500 --> 00:13:38.740
that made me realize that what we're doing to animals,

00:13:38.740 --> 00:13:40.333
isn't something that's good for animals.

00:13:40.333 --> 00:13:41.780
It's definitely not.

00:13:41.780 --> 00:13:44.060
But also is something that contradicts kind of the values

00:13:44.060 --> 00:13:46.350
that I have towards how we should treat them.

00:13:46.350 --> 00:13:48.400
And really just this last piece of the puzzle

00:13:48.400 --> 00:13:51.690
was just being, kind of confronted if you like,

00:13:51.690 --> 00:13:52.960
with the objective reality

00:13:52.960 --> 00:13:54.330
of this is what you are paying for

00:13:54.330 --> 00:13:56.613
when you go into the supermarket and you buy this.

00:13:56.613 --> 00:13:59.110
We may view these decisions as being unconscious

00:13:59.110 --> 00:14:01.090
because they are unconscious,

00:14:01.090 --> 00:14:04.060
but they have this very real, tangible consequence

00:14:04.060 --> 00:14:05.720
that we often don't think about.

00:14:05.720 --> 00:14:06.960
And so I was forced to go,

00:14:06.960 --> 00:14:07.910
when I go into a supermarket

00:14:07.910 --> 00:14:09.907
and I go into a restaurant or wherever it is,

00:14:09.907 --> 00:14:12.180
and I give my money to these industries,

00:14:12.180 --> 00:14:14.840
I am personally funding these very things

00:14:14.840 --> 00:14:18.070
that I'm feeling and very upset about to continue.

00:14:18.070 --> 00:14:20.940
And how do I, as a person who wants to reduce suffering,

00:14:20.940 --> 00:14:22.803
who thinks that a world that,

00:14:23.800 --> 00:14:25.660
obviously we're never gonna create a utopia,

00:14:25.660 --> 00:14:27.600
but a world that is trying to reach that utopia

00:14:27.600 --> 00:14:28.433
as much as we can,

00:14:28.433 --> 00:14:31.280
how does paying for slaughterhouses to exist,

00:14:31.280 --> 00:14:33.440
how does that work and this kind of vision of the future

00:14:33.440 --> 00:14:34.920
that I would like to live in?

00:14:34.920 --> 00:14:35.753
It doesn't.

00:14:35.753 --> 00:14:37.050
So it was all these little moments,

00:14:37.050 --> 00:14:39.552
but then, just this overarching realization

00:14:39.552 --> 00:14:41.450
that I'm not living in alignment,

00:14:41.450 --> 00:14:43.340
that I'm fundamentally a hypocrite

00:14:43.340 --> 00:14:45.090
when it comes to my treatment of animals.

00:14:45.090 --> 00:14:46.840
And that was the final piece to go.

00:14:46.840 --> 00:14:47.840
There is a problem here

00:14:47.840 --> 00:14:51.330
that is bigger than just this food problem.

00:14:51.330 --> 00:14:53.704
It's a mentality issue. And there's a consequence of that.

00:14:53.704 --> 00:14:56.100
I have to be that example of what I want to see.

00:14:58.560 --> 00:15:00.170
So where does the transition

00:15:00.170 --> 00:15:04.653
from simply living this lifestyle turn into,

00:15:04.653 --> 00:15:06.723
this is my life's work.

00:15:07.910 --> 00:15:10.100
That's a big leap, right?
Yeah, it is.

00:15:10.100 --> 00:15:13.617
I had been vegan for a little while,

00:15:13.617 --> 00:15:17.350
and my partner she had been watching some YouTubers

00:15:17.350 --> 00:15:18.500
and we'd been watching,

00:15:19.480 --> 00:15:21.600
absorbing a lot of information about veganism.

00:15:21.600 --> 00:15:23.130
I'd learned about the environmental stuff,

00:15:23.130 --> 00:15:25.100
I'd learned about the health impact,

00:15:25.100 --> 00:15:26.990
about individual chronic health,

00:15:26.990 --> 00:15:28.670
but also kind of a global

00:15:28.670 --> 00:15:30.673
infectious disease risk problem as well.

00:15:30.673 --> 00:15:32.710
Antibiotic resistance, all these things.

00:15:32.710 --> 00:15:35.370
I'd learned about the true scale of the problem

00:15:35.370 --> 00:15:37.570
and how just this simple thing

00:15:37.570 --> 00:15:40.500
that we do every day, consume foods,

00:15:40.500 --> 00:15:42.270
can have such a huge impact

00:15:42.270 --> 00:15:43.830
that we don't even realize.

00:15:43.830 --> 00:15:45.940
Like it's one of the most important decisions

00:15:45.940 --> 00:15:47.430
that we make every day for ourselves,

00:15:47.430 --> 00:15:48.880
but also for the health of the planet.

00:15:48.880 --> 00:15:51.680
And every single being that coexist here with us.

00:15:51.680 --> 00:15:53.270
It can feel, I suppose,

00:15:53.270 --> 00:15:56.510
kind of maybe a little bit of exaggerated

00:15:56.510 --> 00:15:59.960
to make such a claim, but our choices impact the future,

00:15:59.960 --> 00:16:01.613
impacts us in every single way

00:16:01.613 --> 00:16:02.815
that we can possibly think of in that sense.

00:16:02.815 --> 00:16:05.290
So when I started to realize the enormity

00:16:05.290 --> 00:16:06.620
and scale of the problem,

00:16:06.620 --> 00:16:08.970
I kind of felt that I needed to do something about it,

00:16:08.970 --> 00:16:11.940
that simply just living this way, myself, wasn't enough with

00:16:11.940 --> 00:16:14.130
because I'm still kind of allowing

00:16:14.130 --> 00:16:15.960
these actions to continue.

00:16:15.960 --> 00:16:20.960
And before I was vegan, I kind of didn't know.

00:16:21.130 --> 00:16:23.350
And I think about how I've reacted

00:16:23.350 --> 00:16:25.460
if someone had given me this information.

00:16:25.460 --> 00:16:27.114
Would I have changed earlier?

00:16:27.114 --> 00:16:29.360
Would I have had that realization before?

00:16:29.360 --> 00:16:30.830
And I think about all the people in the world

00:16:30.830 --> 00:16:32.960
who align with the same values I have

00:16:32.960 --> 00:16:35.360
and who would probably make that change like I did,

00:16:35.360 --> 00:16:38.680
if they kind of realized the same things that I realized.

00:16:38.680 --> 00:16:41.690
And so being silent in the face of adversity

00:16:41.690 --> 00:16:43.890
is still allowing that adversity to continue.

00:16:43.890 --> 00:16:45.320
Is still a form of complicity

00:16:45.320 --> 00:16:48.260
because you are being a bystander to that exploitation

00:16:48.260 --> 00:16:50.340
and damage continue to happen.

00:16:50.340 --> 00:16:53.960
So I realized I needed to speak up in some way,

00:16:53.960 --> 00:16:55.940
and that started off by just being,

00:16:55.940 --> 00:16:57.010
uploading YouTube videos.

00:16:57.010 --> 00:16:59.190
And the first ones I did were it's very rough

00:16:59.190 --> 00:17:01.160
and I was super nervous,

00:17:01.160 --> 00:17:02.430
but I felt that it was important

00:17:02.430 --> 00:17:04.010
to try and communicate this message

00:17:04.010 --> 00:17:05.210
because so many people

00:17:05.210 --> 00:17:06.580
just have never heard about it before.

00:17:06.580 --> 00:17:08.376
At least have heard about it,

00:17:08.376 --> 00:17:09.210
but have never really sat

00:17:09.210 --> 00:17:11.500
and thought about the reasons to be vegan.

00:17:11.500 --> 00:17:15.159
So it was really YouTube from the get go for you.

00:17:15.159 --> 00:17:17.048
Pretty much.
You are a true millennial.

00:17:17.048 --> 00:17:18.880
Yeah, I guess so, I tick that box.

00:17:18.880 --> 00:17:23.170
Did you study philosophy, or argumentation, or discourse?

00:17:23.170 --> 00:17:25.198
Like you have such a facility with language,

00:17:25.198 --> 00:17:28.220
and like I said earlier,

00:17:28.220 --> 00:17:32.310
just this composure about you that feels very crafted

00:17:32.310 --> 00:17:33.190
and skilled.

00:17:33.190 --> 00:17:36.064
Like where did you learn how to do that?

00:17:36.064 --> 00:17:37.579
I mean, it was really trial and error.

00:17:37.579 --> 00:17:38.758
It's very kind of you to say,

00:17:38.758 --> 00:17:43.590
but there was no real kind of education that I went under.

00:17:43.590 --> 00:17:45.797
I studied film and TV at university

00:17:45.797 --> 00:17:49.460
and I had this aspiration of being some big movie director,

00:17:49.460 --> 00:17:52.310
but quickly realized that that's not what I wanted to do,

00:17:52.310 --> 00:17:54.310
but I could have those transferrable skills

00:17:54.310 --> 00:17:56.462
and apply it to being a YouTuber, of course.

00:17:56.462 --> 00:17:59.150
But for me it was just a process of trial and error,

00:17:59.150 --> 00:18:03.020
I kind of had these conversations with people in the street,

00:18:03.020 --> 00:18:05.130
just very rough conversations at the beginning

00:18:05.130 --> 00:18:07.290
where I'd go out to places like

00:18:07.290 --> 00:18:09.860
Leicester Square or Trafalgar Square in London

00:18:09.860 --> 00:18:11.420
and try to strike up conversations

00:18:11.420 --> 00:18:12.570
with members of the public

00:18:12.570 --> 00:18:15.340
and ask some, hopefully prompting questions.

00:18:15.340 --> 00:18:18.040
But I quickly realized what worked and what didn't work.

00:18:18.040 --> 00:18:20.830
And I quickly realized that having

00:18:20.830 --> 00:18:24.070
an aggressive tone or using certain language,

00:18:24.070 --> 00:18:27.440
or kind of putting someone in a defensive mode,

00:18:27.440 --> 00:18:30.600
doesn't help people reach positive conclusion.

00:18:30.600 --> 00:18:32.220
And I had conversations with people

00:18:32.220 --> 00:18:33.780
where I overstepped the mark a little bit

00:18:33.780 --> 00:18:36.550
where I maybe was a little bit too,

00:18:36.550 --> 00:18:38.880
or at least I came across a bit judgemental,

00:18:38.880 --> 00:18:42.040
I came across, like I had this, air of superiority.

00:18:42.040 --> 00:18:43.850
And I thought, well, who am I doing this for?

00:18:43.850 --> 00:18:46.350
Because being an advocate,

00:18:46.350 --> 00:18:48.300
you are trying to represent something

00:18:48.300 --> 00:18:50.000
and being an advocate for this movement

00:18:50.000 --> 00:18:52.670
means I'm trying to represent something or someone

00:18:52.670 --> 00:18:55.480
who isn't me the animals whose voices we ignore

00:18:55.480 --> 00:18:58.760
because their screams are behind walls of slaughterhouses.

00:18:58.760 --> 00:19:00.850
So if I'm trying to be an advocate for them,

00:19:00.850 --> 00:19:03.640
then I can't allow this kind of self-serving

00:19:05.010 --> 00:19:07.470
philosophy of wanting to make myself feel good

00:19:07.470 --> 00:19:09.640
by allowing these emotions to overtake me,

00:19:09.640 --> 00:19:11.912
this kind anger or this frustration,

00:19:11.912 --> 00:19:13.700
that's self-serving.

00:19:13.700 --> 00:19:15.030
But if I'm trying to be an advocate

00:19:15.030 --> 00:19:16.090
for them or someone else,

00:19:16.090 --> 00:19:17.620
I just think about their best interests

00:19:17.620 --> 00:19:19.308
and I quickly realize that

00:19:19.308 --> 00:19:21.410
allowing these kind of self-serving emotions,

00:19:21.410 --> 00:19:22.710
wasn't productive.

00:19:22.710 --> 00:19:24.400
Trying to be this,

00:19:24.400 --> 00:19:26.980
I suppose, almost voice of someone's conscience.

00:19:26.980 --> 00:19:28.330
when I'm in these conversations,

00:19:28.330 --> 00:19:31.230
I'm trying to not impose my myself,

00:19:31.230 --> 00:19:33.010
but really just allow someone else

00:19:33.010 --> 00:19:35.900
to understand themselves better, themself,

00:19:35.900 --> 00:19:36.980
trying to allow them to understand

00:19:36.980 --> 00:19:38.731
how they view these situations.

00:19:38.731 --> 00:19:40.570
What attitudes do they have?

00:19:40.570 --> 00:19:43.640
And so, I try and revolve these conversations

00:19:43.640 --> 00:19:45.120
around asking questions

00:19:45.120 --> 00:19:47.110
and trying to get people to understand their own beliefs

00:19:47.110 --> 00:19:49.300
rather than telling them what my beliefs are.

00:19:49.300 --> 00:19:51.260
Well, you're very effective in that.

00:19:51.260 --> 00:19:53.090
And I wanna put a pin in that,

00:19:53.090 --> 00:19:55.569
I wanna come back to effective advocacy

00:19:55.569 --> 00:19:58.653
in the later stages of this conversation,

00:19:58.653 --> 00:20:01.388
'cause I think that's super important and interesting.

00:20:01.388 --> 00:20:05.950
But on the thread of sort of advocating

00:20:05.950 --> 00:20:08.429
on behalf of vegan lifestyle,

00:20:08.429 --> 00:20:11.500
I feel like the Overton window has really expanded,

00:20:11.500 --> 00:20:13.360
like even in the last couple years,

00:20:13.360 --> 00:20:17.210
like this is now part of the mainstream discourse,

00:20:17.210 --> 00:20:20.584
people are not confused about what it means so much,

00:20:20.584 --> 00:20:24.340
people are interested in and intrigued by it.

00:20:24.340 --> 00:20:29.170
So I wanna kind of establish the moral landscape here

00:20:29.170 --> 00:20:31.070
and then expand upon that too,

00:20:31.070 --> 00:20:34.670
for you to just kind of advocate on behalf of this,

00:20:34.670 --> 00:20:35.570
in your own words,

00:20:35.570 --> 00:20:37.370
like why should somebody go vegan?

00:20:37.370 --> 00:20:39.280
I mean, you do these, you go around to colleges

00:20:39.280 --> 00:20:42.670
and I wanna talk about how you set up these booths,

00:20:42.670 --> 00:20:44.321
like Lucy in "Peanuts" and say,

00:20:44.321 --> 00:20:48.390
everybody should go vegan, prove me wrong and all of that.

00:20:48.390 --> 00:20:49.270
So you've had hundreds,

00:20:49.270 --> 00:20:52.530
if not thousands of these interactions,

00:20:52.530 --> 00:20:54.637
but if somebody came to you and just said,

00:20:54.637 --> 00:20:56.570
"Ed, tell me why I should be vegan?"

00:20:56.570 --> 00:20:58.563
Like how do you respond to that?

00:20:59.521 --> 00:21:01.830
I think it comes down to a number of factors.

00:21:01.830 --> 00:21:04.560
But the first thing to acknowledge is that we can be vegan.

00:21:04.560 --> 00:21:06.290
What sets apart our current time

00:21:06.290 --> 00:21:07.550
from to times in the past

00:21:07.550 --> 00:21:09.150
is the fact that we have that option

00:21:09.150 --> 00:21:11.070
in a way that previous generations didn't

00:21:11.070 --> 00:21:12.090
throughout history.

00:21:12.090 --> 00:21:14.460
So the first thing to acknowledge is that it's a choice.

00:21:14.460 --> 00:21:15.830
And once we recognize it's a choice,

00:21:15.830 --> 00:21:17.730
it then becomes a moral issue.

00:21:17.730 --> 00:21:19.970
Obviously what we do to animals is a moral issue

00:21:19.970 --> 00:21:21.520
because we all think that animals deserve

00:21:21.520 --> 00:21:23.430
some form of moral consideration.

00:21:23.430 --> 00:21:25.077
That's why we say animal cruelty is wrong.

00:21:25.077 --> 00:21:26.770
And we even talk about animal welfare

00:21:26.770 --> 00:21:27.960
because we have an understanding

00:21:27.960 --> 00:21:29.300
that we want to reduce suffering.

00:21:29.300 --> 00:21:32.210
So we recognize that what we do to animals is a moral issue.

00:21:32.210 --> 00:21:33.870
And then we recognize that what we do to animals

00:21:33.870 --> 00:21:36.290
is a choice and is it something that we have to do?

00:21:36.290 --> 00:21:37.123
So then we have to look

00:21:37.123 --> 00:21:40.200
at why we think animals deserve moral consideration.

00:21:40.200 --> 00:21:41.990
And I always think about it from my own perspective

00:21:41.990 --> 00:21:43.480
as a human first.

00:21:43.480 --> 00:21:45.985
And I recognize it as a human,

00:21:45.985 --> 00:21:49.329
and because I have things, sentience, individuality,

00:21:49.329 --> 00:21:52.900
the capacity to feel whether that's pleasure or pain,

00:21:52.900 --> 00:21:54.440
happiness, or suffering,

00:21:54.440 --> 00:21:57.270
that I have an awareness of my surroundings,

00:21:57.270 --> 00:21:59.070
basically that I have this sentience,

00:21:59.070 --> 00:22:00.980
that, that assigns me moral worth

00:22:00.980 --> 00:22:04.180
because when someone harms me, it's causing me suffering.

00:22:04.180 --> 00:22:06.240
When someone does something to impede on my life,

00:22:06.240 --> 00:22:07.810
that's impeding on my life.

00:22:07.810 --> 00:22:10.700
And that's kind of broadly why we recognize

00:22:10.700 --> 00:22:12.450
that arbitrarily harming other humans

00:22:12.450 --> 00:22:14.130
based on superfluous reasons,

00:22:14.130 --> 00:22:17.210
such as sexuality or ethnicity or whatever it may be,

00:22:17.210 --> 00:22:18.300
sadly enough.

00:22:18.300 --> 00:22:19.750
We recognize that's obviously wrong

00:22:19.750 --> 00:22:21.687
because these people as sentient individuals

00:22:21.687 --> 00:22:22.940
who have these capacities

00:22:22.940 --> 00:22:25.430
in much the same way that we all do, of course.

00:22:25.430 --> 00:22:26.480
So if I'm assigning moral,

00:22:26.480 --> 00:22:29.620
worked myself based on these characteristics and traits,

00:22:29.620 --> 00:22:31.530
I have to work out whether or not the non-human animals

00:22:31.530 --> 00:22:33.370
also tick these boxes.

00:22:33.370 --> 00:22:35.837
Our pigs, chickens, cows, marine animals,

00:22:35.837 --> 00:22:37.210
are they sentient?

00:22:37.210 --> 00:22:38.090
Are they conscious?

00:22:38.090 --> 00:22:39.830
Do they have the capacity to suffer?

00:22:39.830 --> 00:22:43.609
Feel pain, also pleasure, happiness, perhaps as well.

00:22:43.609 --> 00:22:45.150
And we know that they do.

00:22:45.150 --> 00:22:47.730
We recognize that with the animals we have in our homes.

00:22:47.730 --> 00:22:50.840
That dogs and have these personalities,

00:22:50.840 --> 00:22:52.710
things that make them fluffy, the cat

00:22:52.710 --> 00:22:54.460
or in a box or the dog,

00:22:54.460 --> 00:22:56.580
whatever name we might assign to these animals.

00:22:56.580 --> 00:22:58.137
We recognize this about them

00:22:58.137 --> 00:23:01.750
and we know that they have this broad spectrum of emotions.

00:23:01.750 --> 00:23:03.900
And we know that with the animals who we farm

00:23:03.900 --> 00:23:05.610
and who we exploit as well.

00:23:05.610 --> 00:23:08.750
And so when I think about them as individuals,

00:23:08.750 --> 00:23:11.160
I have to think about what moral justification do I have

00:23:11.160 --> 00:23:14.610
to arbitrarily harm them and unnecessarily kill them.

00:23:14.610 --> 00:23:16.700
And you can't morally justify that,

00:23:16.700 --> 00:23:20.630
in the current context of a modern day society.

00:23:20.630 --> 00:23:22.850
So it's about recognizing that these animals

00:23:22.850 --> 00:23:26.380
from a moral perspective should have worth the transcends

00:23:26.380 --> 00:23:28.210
the reasons we use to exploit them,

00:23:28.210 --> 00:23:30.210
taste, food, nutrition.

00:23:30.210 --> 00:23:31.440
We know we can get nutrition

00:23:31.440 --> 00:23:33.280
from plant-based sources instead.

00:23:33.280 --> 00:23:35.100
So when we recognize it's unnecessary

00:23:35.100 --> 00:23:37.943
and we recognize that these animals have these capacities,

00:23:37.943 --> 00:23:40.590
that becomes what I would describe as a,

00:23:40.590 --> 00:23:43.090
it becomes a moral obligation to be vegan

00:23:43.090 --> 00:23:45.050
because it's the best way to reduce suffering

00:23:45.050 --> 00:23:45.970
to its fullest extent

00:23:45.970 --> 00:23:47.647
when it comes to our treatment of animals.

00:23:47.647 --> 00:23:49.540
And we're all against animal suffering,

00:23:49.540 --> 00:23:50.950
all against animal cruelty.

00:23:50.950 --> 00:23:52.790
So it's about working out how we apply that logic

00:23:52.790 --> 00:23:54.060
the most consistently

00:23:54.060 --> 00:23:55.570
and farming them in any way

00:23:55.570 --> 00:23:57.350
or taking them to a slaughterhouse to kill them,

00:23:57.350 --> 00:23:59.470
which is what happens to every farmed animal.

00:23:59.470 --> 00:24:01.120
Even if they're raised for dairy and eggs,

00:24:01.120 --> 00:24:05.070
they still meet the same miserable end prematurely,

00:24:05.070 --> 00:24:07.587
that can't be changed while we still farm them.

00:24:07.587 --> 00:24:09.260
And that causes suffering and cruelties.

00:24:09.260 --> 00:24:11.300
So the best way to extend that, to it's first extent

00:24:11.300 --> 00:24:14.006
is by no longer paying for these things to happen to them

00:24:14.006 --> 00:24:14.960
in the first place.

00:24:14.960 --> 00:24:18.380
There's so much cognitive dissonance that comes into play.

00:24:18.380 --> 00:24:21.580
And there's this leap to really embracing

00:24:21.580 --> 00:24:23.870
the individuation of these animals.

00:24:23.870 --> 00:24:26.608
And I feel like society is structured

00:24:26.608 --> 00:24:30.710
to entrench that cognitive dissonance

00:24:30.710 --> 00:24:33.870
and prevent us from empathizing

00:24:33.870 --> 00:24:37.370
with the individualistic nature of these animals.

00:24:37.370 --> 00:24:38.903
We all know with our pets,

00:24:38.903 --> 00:24:41.380
we can see that and we understand that,

00:24:41.380 --> 00:24:45.010
but then this wall comes down when it comes to other species

00:24:45.010 --> 00:24:49.010
particularly farmed animals for our food

00:24:49.010 --> 00:24:52.763
where we're not so quick to make that leap.

00:24:54.250 --> 00:24:56.910
The psychological aspect of it to me

00:24:56.910 --> 00:25:00.720
is almost more interesting than the intellectual arguments.

00:25:00.720 --> 00:25:02.600
It's like you can make those intellectual arguments

00:25:02.600 --> 00:25:03.790
and we all get it,

00:25:03.790 --> 00:25:06.240
but there's a gap between that understanding

00:25:06.240 --> 00:25:07.400
and behavior change

00:25:07.400 --> 00:25:09.640
that lies in this very murky terrain

00:25:09.640 --> 00:25:12.580
around how the human animal operates.

00:25:12.580 --> 00:25:13.770
It's so important,

00:25:13.770 --> 00:25:16.110
and what you said that I think is really profound

00:25:16.110 --> 00:25:20.310
because it's really easy, I suppose, in principle,

00:25:20.310 --> 00:25:21.270
to get people to understand

00:25:21.270 --> 00:25:23.040
the reasons why change is important.

00:25:23.040 --> 00:25:25.040
But as you say, there are these barriers,

00:25:25.040 --> 00:25:27.510
social, psychological, emotional

00:25:27.510 --> 00:25:30.340
that can restrict us from making those rational choices.

00:25:30.340 --> 00:25:32.050
I mean, the third section of the book

00:25:32.050 --> 00:25:33.577
is really trying to address that.

00:25:33.577 --> 00:25:35.700
And I think we have to understand the mechanisms

00:25:35.700 --> 00:25:37.600
behind why we have this attitude.

00:25:37.600 --> 00:25:39.500
And broadly speaking,

00:25:39.500 --> 00:25:42.080
we categorize animals into free kind of groups.

00:25:42.080 --> 00:25:44.680
We have wild animals, we have pets

00:25:44.680 --> 00:25:46.400
and then we have farm animals.

00:25:46.400 --> 00:25:48.620
And by categorizing animals in that way,

00:25:48.620 --> 00:25:50.300
it allows us to treat them differently

00:25:50.300 --> 00:25:52.330
based on the categorization we've given them.

00:25:52.330 --> 00:25:55.460
So in a sense, what we do is we otherized farm animals

00:25:55.460 --> 00:25:57.750
and we've othrized humans throughout history

00:25:58.868 --> 00:26:00.360
to then provide a justification

00:26:00.360 --> 00:26:02.070
for causing unnecessary harm.

00:26:02.070 --> 00:26:03.280
And that's what we do with farm animals,

00:26:03.280 --> 00:26:04.300
we've otherized them.

00:26:04.300 --> 00:26:07.550
And so we kind of view them as being abstractions.

00:26:07.550 --> 00:26:08.650
we have pets in our home

00:26:08.650 --> 00:26:11.020
and we assign them individual personalities,

00:26:11.020 --> 00:26:12.040
but pigs and chickens,

00:26:12.040 --> 00:26:14.010
while they're just this abstract concept.

00:26:14.010 --> 00:26:16.330
Pigs are dirty, chickens are stupid.

00:26:16.330 --> 00:26:19.760
So by kind of assigning just this one characteristic

00:26:19.760 --> 00:26:22.930
or these broad characteristics to an entire species,

00:26:22.930 --> 00:26:24.650
it makes what we do to the more palatable,

00:26:24.650 --> 00:26:27.420
because we're not viewing them as individuals.

00:26:27.420 --> 00:26:29.780
And I think part of the problem

00:26:29.780 --> 00:26:31.560
with the scale of animal agriculture

00:26:31.560 --> 00:26:34.050
is it allows us to detach

00:26:34.050 --> 00:26:36.810
from that right individuality of each animal.

00:26:36.810 --> 00:26:38.140
Even when I'm advocating,

00:26:38.140 --> 00:26:40.430
I'm quite conscious to make the point of,

00:26:40.430 --> 00:26:43.990
whilst it's terrible that we farm 80 billion land animals

00:26:43.990 --> 00:26:45.080
and we kill somewhere

00:26:45.080 --> 00:26:48.140
between 0.8 and 2.3 trillion marine animals

00:26:48.140 --> 00:26:49.490
every single year,

00:26:49.490 --> 00:26:52.420
whilst that is obviously terrible 'cause of the numbers,

00:26:52.420 --> 00:26:53.750
what's important is that those numbers

00:26:53.750 --> 00:26:57.127
are made up of individuals, solitary individual animals

00:26:57.127 --> 00:26:59.940
who are experiencing everything themselves.

00:26:59.940 --> 00:27:01.767
So I think because we've otherized animals

00:27:01.767 --> 00:27:03.720
and we've created to these abstract concepts

00:27:03.720 --> 00:27:06.460
of what these species that we farm represent.

00:27:06.460 --> 00:27:08.980
It then allows us to not have to really engage

00:27:08.980 --> 00:27:10.870
with the actions that we're committing.

00:27:10.870 --> 00:27:13.520
It's similar to our difficulty

00:27:13.520 --> 00:27:16.300
in empathizing with a genocide

00:27:16.300 --> 00:27:19.480
versus the one kid who's stuck in the well.

00:27:19.480 --> 00:27:21.380
Exactly. That's exactly right, isn't it?

00:27:21.380 --> 00:27:23.510
So I think that part of this problem

00:27:23.510 --> 00:27:25.410
is the detachment from the process.

00:27:25.410 --> 00:27:27.930
Where we live and the cultures where we currently are,

00:27:27.930 --> 00:27:31.530
there is this huge detachment from the slaughterhouse,

00:27:31.530 --> 00:27:34.263
the farm and the supermarkets. And I think in the US,

00:27:34.263 --> 00:27:36.180
there was a study in schools

00:27:36.180 --> 00:27:38.040
that said that a lot of school children think

00:27:38.040 --> 00:27:40.060
that chocolate milk comes from brown cows.

00:27:40.060 --> 00:27:41.950
There's this huge disconnection

00:27:41.950 --> 00:27:44.480
from what we're paying for on what we're consuming.

00:27:44.480 --> 00:27:45.910
And so there is this element

00:27:45.910 --> 00:27:48.770
of having to become more conscious as consumers.

00:27:48.770 --> 00:27:51.951
To intellectually recognize the reasons to make the change,

00:27:51.951 --> 00:27:54.020
but then challenge some of those barriers

00:27:54.020 --> 00:27:56.188
that exist in that cognitive dissonance.

00:27:56.188 --> 00:28:00.570
The confirmation bias that we can all grapple with at times.

00:28:00.570 --> 00:28:02.690
We have to be kind of mindful and conscious

00:28:02.690 --> 00:28:05.640
of these external factors that influence our behavior.

00:28:05.640 --> 00:28:08.340
Because by being mindful of those external factors,

00:28:08.340 --> 00:28:09.964
we can then try and work out

00:28:09.964 --> 00:28:11.210
whether or not our actions are aligning

00:28:11.210 --> 00:28:13.280
with our rational thought processes.

00:28:13.280 --> 00:28:14.810
And I think it's just as simple sometimes

00:28:14.810 --> 00:28:16.800
as going to a supermarket,

00:28:16.800 --> 00:28:18.790
looking at the foods we normally buy

00:28:18.790 --> 00:28:20.748
and just taking a moment to pause.

00:28:20.748 --> 00:28:23.883
Just thinking about who is this from?

00:28:24.987 --> 00:28:27.230
Who did this used to belong to?

00:28:27.230 --> 00:28:28.830
By meat, this is a piece of flesh,

00:28:28.830 --> 00:28:29.790
so where did that come from?

00:28:29.790 --> 00:28:31.790
And just trying to take a moment

00:28:31.790 --> 00:28:34.100
to be more thoughtful and our decision making

00:28:34.100 --> 00:28:36.240
can help us sometimes see through those

00:28:36.240 --> 00:28:39.163
unconscious barriers that can drive our behaviors.

00:28:40.360 --> 00:28:42.760
It's so fascinating.

00:28:42.760 --> 00:28:46.100
I think you say in the book, you cite these to statistics.

00:28:46.100 --> 00:28:49.510
People are poll about their relationship

00:28:49.510 --> 00:28:50.510
with factory farming.

00:28:50.510 --> 00:28:52.550
Most people say it should be abolished

00:28:52.550 --> 00:28:53.510
or we shouldn't have it,

00:28:53.510 --> 00:28:56.530
but 99% of those people the next day

00:28:56.530 --> 00:28:59.360
go to the supermarket and just pick up whatever.

00:28:59.360 --> 00:29:02.110
So, it's so weird that we're that way

00:29:02.110 --> 00:29:06.490
or that we understand that pigs are as smart as dogs,

00:29:06.490 --> 00:29:08.250
if not smarter, probably smarter,

00:29:08.250 --> 00:29:09.780
right?
Sure.

00:29:09.780 --> 00:29:14.690
That they have personalities, et cetera, and yet bacon.

00:29:14.690 --> 00:29:16.840
Like people just can't get around it.

00:29:16.840 --> 00:29:19.430
We're gonna get into all the various arguments

00:29:19.430 --> 00:29:20.660
that you field,

00:29:20.660 --> 00:29:22.840
but on the subject of continuing down this path

00:29:22.840 --> 00:29:25.583
of just making the case for veganism.

00:29:27.780 --> 00:29:31.850
It becomes harder and harder to defend our habits,

00:29:31.850 --> 00:29:33.590
the more educated we are.

00:29:33.590 --> 00:29:35.500
And that's really kind of the tip of your sphere,

00:29:35.500 --> 00:29:38.320
like prioritizing education,

00:29:38.320 --> 00:29:40.770
but being in a place of allowing people

00:29:40.770 --> 00:29:42.780
to make their own decisions around it.

00:29:42.780 --> 00:29:43.613
Absolutely.

00:29:44.780 --> 00:29:48.663
We as a species are of course ignorance about so many things

00:29:48.663 --> 00:29:50.500
and ignorance, isn't a bad thing

00:29:50.500 --> 00:29:52.390
because we can't ever know everything,

00:29:52.390 --> 00:29:53.770
but at the same time,

00:29:53.770 --> 00:29:55.820
the antidote of ignorance is education.

00:29:55.820 --> 00:29:57.600
So there can be, I suppose,

00:29:57.600 --> 00:29:59.783
this belief that non-vegans have

00:29:59.783 --> 00:30:04.486
that vegan can be judgemental have an air of superiority.

00:30:04.486 --> 00:30:06.170
I'm sure some vegans do tick those boxes.

00:30:06.170 --> 00:30:09.330
but by and large, I think that what the role

00:30:09.330 --> 00:30:11.540
of a vegan advocate is really just to try

00:30:11.540 --> 00:30:14.360
and give some to provide some education

00:30:14.360 --> 00:30:17.290
to someone who just has never heard of these arguments.

00:30:17.290 --> 00:30:18.370
I think what we have to understand

00:30:18.370 --> 00:30:20.900
is that good people do bad things.

00:30:20.900 --> 00:30:23.504
We're all guilty of that
We all live in some level

00:30:23.504 --> 00:30:26.270
of moral ambiguity.
Absolutely.

00:30:26.270 --> 00:30:28.660
'Cause we can never be morally puritanical, can we?

00:30:28.660 --> 00:30:30.330
And so I think we have to recognize

00:30:30.330 --> 00:30:31.567
that good people can do bad things

00:30:31.567 --> 00:30:32.750
and that these bad things

00:30:32.750 --> 00:30:37.670
are driven by often external factors, such as advertising,

00:30:37.670 --> 00:30:41.080
such as a detachment from the reality

00:30:41.080 --> 00:30:42.040
of what we're purchasing.

00:30:42.040 --> 00:30:46.530
Such as this proliferation of information

00:30:46.530 --> 00:30:48.420
and industry funded studies

00:30:48.420 --> 00:30:50.530
that creates doubt in the eyes of the consumer.

00:30:50.530 --> 00:30:51.410
There are so many things

00:30:51.410 --> 00:30:54.080
that lead us to making the decisions that we make.

00:30:54.080 --> 00:30:55.790
And I think just having that recognition

00:30:55.790 --> 00:30:57.810
that we are all capable of doing bad things

00:30:57.810 --> 00:30:59.830
but at the same time we're all capable of doing better.

00:30:59.830 --> 00:31:03.800
And education can be an antidote to that ignorance.

00:31:03.800 --> 00:31:05.910
Aristotle, I see in the books, says that,

00:31:05.910 --> 00:31:10.250
the roots of education are bitter, but the fruit is sweet.

00:31:10.250 --> 00:31:11.520
Obviously what we do to animals

00:31:11.520 --> 00:31:13.360
is a deeply uncomfortable topic.

00:31:13.360 --> 00:31:15.160
And I suppose that the broader consequence

00:31:15.160 --> 00:31:16.930
of the environmental damage,

00:31:16.930 --> 00:31:19.060
the personal health consequences,

00:31:19.060 --> 00:31:21.460
these things that are terrifying and frightening,

00:31:21.460 --> 00:31:23.770
and it's often very to learn something

00:31:23.770 --> 00:31:26.420
that contradicts the way that we've lived our entire life.

00:31:26.420 --> 00:31:27.990
But the consequence of that,

00:31:27.990 --> 00:31:31.100
the fruit of that is something so important,

00:31:31.100 --> 00:31:33.530
which is that we're creating a better food system,

00:31:33.530 --> 00:31:35.610
which is a consequence of that improves

00:31:35.610 --> 00:31:36.550
so many of these issues

00:31:36.550 --> 00:31:38.600
that we're currently facing as a species.

00:31:39.610 --> 00:31:43.420
Are you able to maintain hopefulness amid all of this,

00:31:43.420 --> 00:31:47.033
or you find yourself descending into cynicism?

00:31:49.550 --> 00:31:51.160
It can really vary.

00:31:51.160 --> 00:31:52.970
Some days I can feel cynical,

00:31:52.970 --> 00:31:55.420
some days I feel probably overly optimistic.

00:31:55.420 --> 00:31:58.150
But I think by and large, I have a huge amount of faith,

00:31:58.150 --> 00:31:59.850
whether that's misguided, I'm not sure,

00:31:59.850 --> 00:32:02.890
but I think humans are an incredible species

00:32:02.890 --> 00:32:05.810
because we are obviously so intellectual,

00:32:05.810 --> 00:32:07.830
we're obviously capable of doing so much.

00:32:07.830 --> 00:32:10.430
And whilst we are deeply flawed in so many ways,

00:32:10.430 --> 00:32:13.370
we're also capable of achieving something better.

00:32:13.370 --> 00:32:14.203
And I think one of the reasons

00:32:14.203 --> 00:32:15.640
why I enjoy having these conversations

00:32:15.640 --> 00:32:17.170
and going to college campuses and such

00:32:17.170 --> 00:32:19.500
is because it allows me to connect with people

00:32:19.500 --> 00:32:20.820
I would never normally connect with,

00:32:20.820 --> 00:32:22.509
cowboys, farmers.

00:32:22.509 --> 00:32:24.760
People have very different views to me,

00:32:24.760 --> 00:32:26.010
but it allows me to sit down

00:32:26.010 --> 00:32:29.220
and talk with them for 30 minutes, 40 minutes,

00:32:29.220 --> 00:32:31.600
and really get to know them and understand how they feel.

00:32:31.600 --> 00:32:33.290
And I often leave these conversations

00:32:33.290 --> 00:32:34.590
feeling very optimistic

00:32:34.590 --> 00:32:36.770
because I recognize that deep down

00:32:36.770 --> 00:32:38.030
we all want the same thing,

00:32:38.030 --> 00:32:40.390
which is a better world fundamentally.

00:32:40.390 --> 00:32:42.180
We might have differing views on how we get there,

00:32:42.180 --> 00:32:44.330
but we all have this idea that we want,

00:32:44.330 --> 00:32:45.880
when it comes to agriculture, at least,

00:32:45.880 --> 00:32:47.500
that we want a more sustainable system,

00:32:47.500 --> 00:32:50.310
a more ethical system, a more just system,

00:32:50.310 --> 00:32:53.060
a system that reduces disparity

00:32:53.060 --> 00:32:56.300
for people in low economic scenarios.

00:32:56.300 --> 00:32:57.993
We all want to strive for that.

00:32:57.993 --> 00:32:59.310
It's just, we haven't quite reached

00:32:59.310 --> 00:33:00.510
the commonality of how to get there.

00:33:00.510 --> 00:33:02.320
But these conversations I have with people,

00:33:02.320 --> 00:33:04.610
they reassure me that when we sit down

00:33:04.610 --> 00:33:06.270
and talk about these issues,

00:33:06.270 --> 00:33:07.930
and these are big moral concerns,

00:33:07.930 --> 00:33:09.950
and these are big philosophical topics,

00:33:09.950 --> 00:33:12.200
just a conversation can really help us reach

00:33:12.200 --> 00:33:13.360
an era of commonality,

00:33:13.360 --> 00:33:15.870
which leaves me feeling a lot more empowered

00:33:15.870 --> 00:33:18.690
than I probably without that aspect of my advocacy.

00:33:18.690 --> 00:33:22.000
So you've lectured and taught at many colleges,

00:33:22.000 --> 00:33:23.760
you've traveled all over the place,

00:33:23.760 --> 00:33:25.840
you were a guest lecturer at Harvard,

00:33:25.840 --> 00:33:27.530
which is super interesting.

00:33:27.530 --> 00:33:29.710
I think you're doing an online thing with Harvard.

00:33:29.710 --> 00:33:31.060
Yes.
Like next week,

00:33:31.060 --> 00:33:31.913
which is cool.

00:33:33.060 --> 00:33:35.910
But in addition to that, like I mentioned earlier,

00:33:35.910 --> 00:33:38.380
you set up these booths on college campuses

00:33:38.380 --> 00:33:41.410
and you entertain these conversations with people

00:33:41.410 --> 00:33:42.910
about veganism.

00:33:42.910 --> 00:33:46.050
You share those clips on Instagram and on and on YouTube.

00:33:46.050 --> 00:33:48.640
So you've had so many of these conversations

00:33:48.640 --> 00:33:50.910
and every argument conceivable I'm sure

00:33:50.910 --> 00:33:53.983
has been foisted upon you that you've had to field.

00:33:55.180 --> 00:33:58.880
How many of those conversations end up

00:33:58.880 --> 00:34:01.430
'cause you're only choosing which ones to share,

00:34:01.430 --> 00:34:03.680
you're having many more of them.

00:34:03.680 --> 00:34:06.280
Do you get the sense that you're moving the needle

00:34:06.280 --> 00:34:08.740
or at least leaving people with something to think about?

00:34:08.740 --> 00:34:10.020
I mean, that's my sense,

00:34:10.020 --> 00:34:13.230
occasionally you'll have a frustrating one but overall,

00:34:13.230 --> 00:34:15.010
my feeling is that young people

00:34:15.010 --> 00:34:17.850
are engaging you in good faith

00:34:17.850 --> 00:34:20.420
and they may have their head in a different place,

00:34:20.420 --> 00:34:24.380
but there's a lot of kind of mutual respect

00:34:24.380 --> 00:34:26.790
that goes both ways in these conversations.

00:34:26.790 --> 00:34:31.060
And irrespective of the outcome, that gives me hope.

00:34:31.060 --> 00:34:33.623
Like I think that's cool to just witness.

00:34:35.080 --> 00:34:35.930
I mean, exactly right.

00:34:35.930 --> 00:34:39.347
We tend to upload nearly all the conversations

00:34:39.347 --> 00:34:41.200
that take place.

00:34:41.200 --> 00:34:43.940
And I've just been traveling for the past few weeks.

00:34:43.940 --> 00:34:47.031
I've got so much content because the thing about the US

00:34:47.031 --> 00:34:49.653
is people are very open to telling you their views.

00:34:51.820 --> 00:34:53.850
That's a loaded statement in and of itself.

00:34:53.850 --> 00:34:54.890
It's a polite way of saying

00:34:54.890 --> 00:34:58.210
there's a little bit of division that occurs here sometimes.

00:34:58.210 --> 00:34:59.597
But people are very open to that

00:34:59.597 --> 00:35:01.880
and that's part of the reason why I like being here.

00:35:01.880 --> 00:35:03.910
And so I've had monster of these conversations,

00:35:03.910 --> 00:35:07.930
but you are right by and large, which is that there is this,

00:35:07.930 --> 00:35:10.010
I think respectful dialogue that hopefully takes place.

00:35:10.010 --> 00:35:11.920
And I try very hard to facilitate that.

00:35:11.920 --> 00:35:14.700
So when people sit down the camera operator

00:35:14.700 --> 00:35:16.080
will start moving the cameras,

00:35:16.080 --> 00:35:17.870
checking the memory cards, checking the batteries.

00:35:17.870 --> 00:35:19.310
And we do that deliberately,

00:35:19.310 --> 00:35:21.120
so that I have two or three minutes

00:35:21.120 --> 00:35:23.830
just to sit and talk to of someone, not about veganism.

00:35:23.830 --> 00:35:24.663
What do you study?

00:35:24.663 --> 00:35:25.496
Where are you from?

00:35:25.496 --> 00:35:27.400
Do you like it? Just really build up a bit of rapport

00:35:27.400 --> 00:35:29.940
because if we're gonna engage in these conversations

00:35:29.940 --> 00:35:32.450
where I'm gonna be challenging people fundamentally

00:35:32.450 --> 00:35:35.130
on their lifestyle and their belief system,

00:35:35.130 --> 00:35:35.963
we need to make sure

00:35:35.963 --> 00:35:37.400
that we're going into that with this positive mindset.

00:35:37.400 --> 00:35:39.610
So trying to just make someone feel comfortable immediately

00:35:39.610 --> 00:35:40.443
is very important

00:35:40.443 --> 00:35:43.400
because if we want people to be vulnerable,

00:35:43.400 --> 00:35:45.600
we have to express vulnerability in ourselves.

00:35:45.600 --> 00:35:48.120
We have to be able to,

00:35:48.120 --> 00:35:53.120
I suppose, make statements that almost,

00:35:53.200 --> 00:35:54.660
I guess what I'm trying to say is it's important for us

00:35:54.660 --> 00:35:56.050
to be able to own up to things

00:35:56.050 --> 00:35:56.897
that they're right to about as well.

00:35:56.897 --> 00:35:58.530
And I think in a debate,

00:35:58.530 --> 00:36:00.980
what can often happen is people have this reluctance

00:36:00.980 --> 00:36:02.340
to accept that they're wrong,

00:36:02.340 --> 00:36:04.390
or accept that the other person's made a good point.

00:36:04.390 --> 00:36:06.460
It's always, no, I have to be 100% right

00:36:06.460 --> 00:36:08.010
and nothing they say can be valid.

00:36:08.010 --> 00:36:09.530
And I think just taking that moment at the beginning

00:36:09.530 --> 00:36:12.030
to build with a rapport, lowers both of our defenses

00:36:12.030 --> 00:36:13.800
to be open enough to be able to say,

00:36:13.800 --> 00:36:15.210
that's a really good point that you've made,

00:36:15.210 --> 00:36:17.080
or I understand why you're saying,

00:36:17.080 --> 00:36:19.073
or yeah, I've never thought about that before,

00:36:19.073 --> 00:36:22.270
because we have this kind of almost very early friendship

00:36:22.270 --> 00:36:25.070
emerging from just this moment of rapport building.

00:36:25.070 --> 00:36:26.940
And I think having those kind of techniques

00:36:26.940 --> 00:36:30.219
of just taking the time to listen to someone,

00:36:30.219 --> 00:36:33.250
asking them thoughtful questions.

00:36:33.250 --> 00:36:34.990
Trying to understand their argument.

00:36:34.990 --> 00:36:37.810
There is this, I think fracturing

00:36:37.810 --> 00:36:39.960
that's occurring within society

00:36:39.960 --> 00:36:42.800
where we're kind of caricaturing people

00:36:42.800 --> 00:36:43.633
and their beliefs

00:36:43.633 --> 00:36:47.280
based on certain principles of their identity.

00:36:47.280 --> 00:36:48.230
And we don't actually take the time

00:36:48.230 --> 00:36:50.570
to understand people's beliefs anymore.

00:36:50.570 --> 00:36:53.540
And I think why I like having these conversations

00:36:53.540 --> 00:36:55.360
is I'm interested to understand

00:36:55.360 --> 00:36:57.270
why people think the way they do,

00:36:57.270 --> 00:36:58.700
how did they reach the, that conclusion?

00:36:58.700 --> 00:37:00.423
Why do you think this is the way that it is?

00:37:00.423 --> 00:37:02.760
Because it makes me better as an advocate as well?

00:37:02.760 --> 00:37:04.280
How am I supposed to be,

00:37:04.280 --> 00:37:06.070
I suppose, competent at debating people

00:37:06.070 --> 00:37:08.250
if I don't understand what their point of view is.

00:37:08.250 --> 00:37:10.730
So I think that builds respect as well.

00:37:10.730 --> 00:37:13.620
Just listening and being genuinely interested

00:37:13.620 --> 00:37:17.798
and sincere in your, I guess, your care of their beliefs.

00:37:17.798 --> 00:37:18.830
And to that point point,

00:37:18.830 --> 00:37:21.840
I assume it's intentional on your part

00:37:21.840 --> 00:37:26.620
to purposefully attend colleges with animal husbandry

00:37:26.620 --> 00:37:28.320
and animal agriculture departments

00:37:28.320 --> 00:37:30.880
like Cornell and Texas A&amp;M like those are,

00:37:30.880 --> 00:37:32.270
like there's a lot of students

00:37:32.270 --> 00:37:33.470
that I'm sure you interacted with

00:37:33.470 --> 00:37:38.470
who are actually studying how to be animal agriculturalists

00:37:38.710 --> 00:37:40.070
on some level.

00:37:40.070 --> 00:37:42.210
And that I'm sure you're confronting

00:37:42.210 --> 00:37:43.460
a lot of cognitive dissonance.

00:37:43.460 --> 00:37:46.280
I mean, they're being, indoctrinated is the wrong word,

00:37:46.280 --> 00:37:48.540
but they're being kind of trained and educated

00:37:48.540 --> 00:37:53.120
in a particular way of life and profession

00:37:53.120 --> 00:37:56.730
that is at odds with your core moral philosophy.

00:37:56.730 --> 00:37:58.880
Yeah, that exact scenario played out

00:37:58.880 --> 00:38:00.315
at Texas A&amp;M recently,

00:38:00.315 --> 00:38:05.315
this guy sat down this really proud Texan, from rural Texas,

00:38:05.960 --> 00:38:07.110
told me he didn't like the cities,

00:38:07.110 --> 00:38:09.569
he just liked being out in the middle of the wild west.

00:38:09.569 --> 00:38:14.140
And he was studying animal ag, animal ag science.

00:38:14.140 --> 00:38:16.907
And so he sat down very respectfully and was just like,

00:38:16.907 --> 00:38:19.650
"I'm really interested to hear your point of view."

00:38:19.650 --> 00:38:20.720
And I was like, "Well, that's great.

00:38:20.720 --> 00:38:21.800
Now I'm interested to hear yours."

00:38:21.800 --> 00:38:23.387
And so we started this conversation and he said,

00:38:23.387 --> 00:38:28.130
"Well, I was told today in my class that dairy cows

00:38:29.100 --> 00:38:32.040
like to be farmed, they queue up to be milked

00:38:32.040 --> 00:38:34.277
and so it's a consensual thing."

00:38:34.277 --> 00:38:37.000
And I said, well, that's a very interesting statement,

00:38:37.000 --> 00:38:39.280
but it kind of misses the finer details,

00:38:39.280 --> 00:38:41.470
which is that, we've selectively bred dairy cows

00:38:41.470 --> 00:38:43.630
to produce excessive amounts of milk.

00:38:43.630 --> 00:38:45.980
Dairy cows need to be milked because they're producing milk

00:38:45.980 --> 00:38:48.100
and it's uncomfortable for them not to be milked.

00:38:48.100 --> 00:38:49.659
So there's a reason why dairy cows

00:38:49.659 --> 00:38:51.900
will walk themselves into the milking parlor,

00:38:51.900 --> 00:38:54.110
it's because they have to be milked.

00:38:54.110 --> 00:38:56.827
why is it causes them pain, mastitis, all of these things,

00:38:56.827 --> 00:38:58.150
but it doesn't make them

00:38:58.150 --> 00:39:00.350
willing participants in their exploitation,

00:39:00.350 --> 00:39:01.830
just because they go through this

00:39:01.830 --> 00:39:03.500
predetermined biological process

00:39:03.500 --> 00:39:05.580
where they have to have their milk released

00:39:05.580 --> 00:39:06.890
should be done by their child, of course,

00:39:06.890 --> 00:39:08.040
but in the absence of their child

00:39:08.040 --> 00:39:09.570
must be done through the machinery.

00:39:09.570 --> 00:39:11.940
Just because they willfully walk into that environment,

00:39:11.940 --> 00:39:14.340
doesn't make everything else we do to them consensual.

00:39:14.340 --> 00:39:16.480
But so there is that indoctrination

00:39:16.480 --> 00:39:18.330
that exists within these courses

00:39:18.330 --> 00:39:21.930
because farmers by and large

00:39:21.930 --> 00:39:23.750
are raised in farming communities.

00:39:23.750 --> 00:39:26.543
And this is a really important thing to recognize.

00:39:26.543 --> 00:39:28.700
And they're raised with a belief system

00:39:28.700 --> 00:39:29.533
that's given to them

00:39:29.533 --> 00:39:31.650
by their parents and their grandparents.

00:39:31.650 --> 00:39:35.080
These are family farms passed down generation to generation.

00:39:35.080 --> 00:39:37.470
And as a child growing up in that environment,

00:39:37.470 --> 00:39:40.150
seeing what the farm means to your family

00:39:40.150 --> 00:39:41.430
and feeling the expectation

00:39:41.430 --> 00:39:44.210
that you have to go into that farming scenario yourself,

00:39:44.210 --> 00:39:45.220
plus you're in a community

00:39:45.220 --> 00:39:47.280
that's just farmers and farming families,

00:39:47.280 --> 00:39:48.447
so your friends are farmers

00:39:48.447 --> 00:39:50.780
and you grew up with farming children,

00:39:50.780 --> 00:39:52.430
it's all you've ever known.

00:39:52.430 --> 00:39:54.830
So what choice do you have but to pursue that?

00:39:54.830 --> 00:39:56.890
I think it requires an exceptional amount

00:39:56.890 --> 00:40:01.580
of humility and dedication for a child to break out of that.

00:40:01.580 --> 00:40:03.840
So then they go to universities, colleges

00:40:03.840 --> 00:40:06.310
that are studying teaching animal ag science.

00:40:06.310 --> 00:40:08.910
So they come from farming communities with farming families,

00:40:08.910 --> 00:40:11.670
go learn about farming and then become farmers.

00:40:11.670 --> 00:40:13.150
And it's all they've ever known.

00:40:13.150 --> 00:40:16.230
I think it's easy to understand why many farmers

00:40:16.230 --> 00:40:18.571
may not sit down like that Texan did

00:40:18.571 --> 00:40:19.600
and have a conversation with me,

00:40:19.600 --> 00:40:21.890
why they may feel threatened by the presence of someone

00:40:21.890 --> 00:40:24.207
who's out outspoken about their veganism,

00:40:24.207 --> 00:40:26.060
because it's not just challenging their livelihood,

00:40:26.060 --> 00:40:29.089
it's challenging their entire existence, their identity.

00:40:29.089 --> 00:40:30.360
Yeah, completely.

00:40:30.360 --> 00:40:34.330
Like I can easily empathize with the level of defensiveness

00:40:34.330 --> 00:40:35.163
or resistance

00:40:35.163 --> 00:40:37.950
that somebody in that position have towards you.

00:40:37.950 --> 00:40:41.140
Absolutely.
With your long hair,

00:40:41.140 --> 00:40:42.870
it's like, come on, dude.

00:40:42.870 --> 00:40:44.300
The opposite of a Texan cowboy.

00:40:44.300 --> 00:40:46.230
What do you know about my life?

00:40:46.230 --> 00:40:48.700
Yeah, exactly.
So how does that go?

00:40:48.700 --> 00:40:50.680
Like how did it play out with that guy?

00:40:50.680 --> 00:40:51.996
Really well.

00:40:51.996 --> 00:40:53.710
I was lucky in the sense

00:40:53.710 --> 00:40:56.604
that he was very receptive and open minded

00:40:56.604 --> 00:41:00.040
and he didn't leave with this revelation

00:41:00.040 --> 00:41:02.170
that he was gonna stop studying animal science

00:41:02.170 --> 00:41:03.030
and become vegan.

00:41:03.030 --> 00:41:04.290
That didn't happen.

00:41:04.290 --> 00:41:07.530
But at the same time he left, I would hope,

00:41:07.530 --> 00:41:11.420
with an understanding about veganism,

00:41:11.420 --> 00:41:12.950
but also an understanding about vegans

00:41:12.950 --> 00:41:14.120
that he didn't have before.

00:41:14.120 --> 00:41:17.350
I think that vegans often have an optics problem,

00:41:17.350 --> 00:41:20.080
that we have this perception of us

00:41:20.080 --> 00:41:21.589
which is really damaging,

00:41:21.589 --> 00:41:22.860
and a lot of the time

00:41:22.860 --> 00:41:24.860
we don't necessarily help ourselves with that perception,

00:41:24.860 --> 00:41:27.210
I can be the first one to (indistinct) that.

00:41:27.210 --> 00:41:31.390
It's incredibly emotive and divisive, as you have said,

00:41:31.390 --> 00:41:33.410
regardless of whether you're pro or con.

00:41:33.410 --> 00:41:34.823
Just saying the word,

00:41:38.093 --> 00:41:40.346
people get agitated no matter what.

00:41:40.346 --> 00:41:43.630
So first of all, what does it even mean?

00:41:43.630 --> 00:41:48.000
Why does that word tend to provoke people

00:41:48.000 --> 00:41:49.630
one way or the other.

00:41:49.630 --> 00:41:50.930
And you're correct,

00:41:50.930 --> 00:41:52.890
vegans can be their own worst enemies

00:41:52.890 --> 00:41:55.240
and there's a lot of infighting and confusion

00:41:55.240 --> 00:41:57.600
amidst the community that is not helpful.

00:41:57.600 --> 00:41:58.433
No.

00:41:58.433 --> 00:42:00.370
So I think optics are a massive part of that problem

00:42:00.370 --> 00:42:05.370
and I think very quickly I realized that being a messenger

00:42:05.400 --> 00:42:07.957
isn't necessarily so much about what you say

00:42:07.957 --> 00:42:10.090
but how you say it, how you come across.

00:42:10.090 --> 00:42:11.840
And with that cowboy,

00:42:11.840 --> 00:42:14.200
and hopefully with most people that sit down

00:42:14.200 --> 00:42:16.410
and talk to me or engage with me in one way or another,

00:42:16.410 --> 00:42:18.047
I just want them to leave with the feeling that,

00:42:18.047 --> 00:42:20.380
oh, okay vegans can be this way.

00:42:20.380 --> 00:42:22.420
Vegans can listen, vegans can be respectful,

00:42:22.420 --> 00:42:24.210
vegans can engage in a thoughtful manner.

00:42:24.210 --> 00:42:26.110
They're gonna call me an animal murder

00:42:26.110 --> 00:42:27.120
as soon as I sit down.

00:42:27.120 --> 00:42:28.580
And I think that that's an important aspect

00:42:28.580 --> 00:42:30.016
of what I try and do,

00:42:30.016 --> 00:42:32.220
is just trying to, I suppose, reframe people's perceptions

00:42:32.220 --> 00:42:33.900
of what bean vegan means.

00:42:33.900 --> 00:42:35.910
'Cause it's easy for us to distance ourself

00:42:35.910 --> 00:42:36.870
from doing something

00:42:36.870 --> 00:42:38.870
if we don't like the person who's doing it.

00:42:38.870 --> 00:42:42.250
So, if we can't disagree with the message

00:42:42.250 --> 00:42:45.016
that causing unnecessary suffering to animals is wrong,

00:42:45.016 --> 00:42:46.030
the next best thing

00:42:46.030 --> 00:42:47.640
is to disagree with the person saying it.

00:42:47.640 --> 00:42:49.530
And so people are looking for any excuse

00:42:49.530 --> 00:42:52.740
to I suppose disbar from these conversations,

00:42:52.740 --> 00:42:54.180
to not have to engage.

00:42:54.180 --> 00:42:57.910
And if we, as vegans perpetuating a stereotype

00:42:57.910 --> 00:43:00.610
that people don't want to engage with,

00:43:00.610 --> 00:43:01.930
then that's how they distance themselves

00:43:01.930 --> 00:43:03.410
from these really important issues.

00:43:03.410 --> 00:43:05.150
So the first thing we have to do is be,

00:43:05.150 --> 00:43:06.870
I suppose, personable and approachable

00:43:06.870 --> 00:43:09.390
as that people will then want to listen.

00:43:09.390 --> 00:43:11.880
And if we fail that first hurdle and we don't come across-

00:43:11.880 --> 00:43:13.509
The game's over.

00:43:13.509 --> 00:43:14.760
Yeah, it's over just like that.

00:43:14.760 --> 00:43:15.610
And not only that,

00:43:15.610 --> 00:43:18.620
you have to overcome all of these other barriers and hurdles

00:43:18.620 --> 00:43:22.950
because the media is hard at work at characterizing,

00:43:22.950 --> 00:43:24.380
there's a whole thing in the book about this,

00:43:24.380 --> 00:43:26.230
and you've made videos about this,

00:43:26.230 --> 00:43:30.180
the mischaracterization on behalf of the media

00:43:30.180 --> 00:43:34.030
to paint a broad picture of vegans and veganism,

00:43:34.030 --> 00:43:38.940
as insane militants and violent people

00:43:38.940 --> 00:43:42.190
who wanna kill babies, et cetera, all of that kind of stuff.

00:43:42.190 --> 00:43:45.100
So people come in with their preconceived idea

00:43:45.100 --> 00:43:47.091
that you have to rebut.

00:43:47.091 --> 00:43:50.910
So you almost have to be all the much more

00:43:50.910 --> 00:43:53.410
sort of patient and conciliatory.

00:43:53.410 --> 00:43:55.550
And it's hard at times,

00:43:55.550 --> 00:43:57.913
but you're right there is often,

00:44:01.236 --> 00:44:02.990
maybe a concerted effort is a little bit too dramatic,

00:44:02.990 --> 00:44:07.220
but there is often an abundance of media articles

00:44:07.220 --> 00:44:10.558
and press releases that are staunchly against veganism.

00:44:10.558 --> 00:44:13.430
Whether that's veganism makes you ill,

00:44:13.430 --> 00:44:14.520
young girls are going vegan

00:44:14.520 --> 00:44:17.683
'cause they're trying to mask their anorexia or orthorexia,

00:44:18.540 --> 00:44:20.400
that vegans kill more animals

00:44:20.400 --> 00:44:22.110
that were destroying the Amazon rainforest.

00:44:22.110 --> 00:44:24.810
There are these narratives that are consistently perpetuated

00:44:24.810 --> 00:44:26.890
even though they're consistently debunked.

00:44:26.890 --> 00:44:28.690
And there is this, I think,

00:44:28.690 --> 00:44:30.640
issue with people's perceptions about veganism,

00:44:30.640 --> 00:44:32.560
not coming from any, I suppose,

00:44:32.560 --> 00:44:34.230
detailed research on their part,

00:44:34.230 --> 00:44:35.440
but merely just because they've seen

00:44:35.440 --> 00:44:37.752
sensationalist headlines about vegan activists.

00:44:37.752 --> 00:44:39.180
It's this passive media consumption.

00:44:39.180 --> 00:44:43.520
that is buttressed by the advertising industry

00:44:43.520 --> 00:44:45.530
and dairy checkoff programs, et cetera,

00:44:45.530 --> 00:44:48.830
that are feeding us this narrative

00:44:48.830 --> 00:44:52.010
about how to eat made is to be masculine,

00:44:52.010 --> 00:44:53.440
and if you're gonna be vegan,

00:44:53.440 --> 00:44:56.623
you're a soy boy and that's for sissies and all of that.

00:44:57.690 --> 00:45:00.700
So there's that kind of piece as well,

00:45:00.700 --> 00:45:03.170
which for young men can be very powerful

00:45:03.170 --> 00:45:06.190
as a lever in thinking about these issues.

00:45:06.190 --> 00:45:07.150
It is so important.

00:45:07.150 --> 00:45:09.920
And I think the meat and masculinity link

00:45:09.920 --> 00:45:13.283
is a really important one for us to consider.

00:45:14.860 --> 00:45:15.900
I suppose it's easy for us

00:45:15.900 --> 00:45:19.510
to look at men who consume meat for that reason

00:45:19.510 --> 00:45:22.063
and view them as being these really terrible people

00:45:22.063 --> 00:45:24.270
who are just all about their ego

00:45:24.270 --> 00:45:26.480
and are fragile in all of this.

00:45:26.480 --> 00:45:27.980
But it kind of does a disservice

00:45:27.980 --> 00:45:29.900
to the incredible amount of pressure

00:45:29.900 --> 00:45:31.120
that comes from advertising,

00:45:31.120 --> 00:45:34.150
that come from public perception and peer pressure.

00:45:34.150 --> 00:45:36.350
And there are studies that show that

00:45:36.350 --> 00:45:40.320
men can feel very disenfranchised in social environments

00:45:40.320 --> 00:45:41.760
where they feel that their masculinity

00:45:41.760 --> 00:45:43.320
is being questioned by their friends

00:45:43.320 --> 00:45:44.880
and that they're less likely

00:45:44.880 --> 00:45:47.560
to choose plan-based options in these social situations,

00:45:47.560 --> 00:45:50.160
even if they rationally understand

00:45:50.160 --> 00:45:52.430
the reasons to do so that, that peer pressure

00:45:52.430 --> 00:45:54.790
and that societal pressure of these expectations

00:45:54.790 --> 00:45:55.940
about what being a man means

00:45:55.940 --> 00:45:57.900
and how that equates to diets

00:45:57.900 --> 00:45:59.940
really puts a lot of burden on young men,

00:45:59.940 --> 00:46:01.730
especially to fulfill that.

00:46:01.730 --> 00:46:03.880
And of course there are lots of issues

00:46:03.880 --> 00:46:08.330
with this kind of hyper masculine attitudes

00:46:08.330 --> 00:46:09.400
that's perpetuated,

00:46:09.400 --> 00:46:12.110
especially now I feel maybe more than has been in the past,

00:46:12.110 --> 00:46:13.690
but I think overlooks

00:46:13.690 --> 00:46:15.960
the fact that there is this concerted effort,

00:46:15.960 --> 00:46:18.690
probably from many areas from industry

00:46:18.690 --> 00:46:21.870
and also from the media to perpetuate that narrative.

00:46:21.870 --> 00:46:24.870
You turn on the TV and these fast food chains

00:46:24.870 --> 00:46:27.460
are using images of sexualized women

00:46:27.460 --> 00:46:31.970
or images of men talking about eating meat like a man,

00:46:31.970 --> 00:46:34.810
that's a message that's perpetuated a lot,

00:46:34.810 --> 00:46:35.910
and it weighs heavy

00:46:35.910 --> 00:46:38.110
on the psychology of young people growing up

00:46:38.110 --> 00:46:39.190
and who they want to be

00:46:39.190 --> 00:46:41.090
and how they feel they should conform.

00:46:42.320 --> 00:46:43.560
Well, let's go through

00:46:43.560 --> 00:46:46.870
some of the main arguments against being vegan

00:46:46.870 --> 00:46:50.620
since you have fielded all of them.

00:46:50.620 --> 00:46:52.750
At the top of that list really is this argument

00:46:52.750 --> 00:46:53.820
around personal choice,

00:46:53.820 --> 00:46:55.690
like get outta my business,

00:46:55.690 --> 00:46:58.360
why are vegans always trying to make me go vegan,

00:46:58.360 --> 00:46:59.623
live and let live.

00:47:00.820 --> 00:47:02.200
Live and let live.
To let's say you,

00:47:02.200 --> 00:47:03.893
Earthling Ed.
Let's say me.

00:47:05.220 --> 00:47:07.160
Sometimes I think vegans can try and make the point

00:47:07.160 --> 00:47:09.420
that it's not a personal choice if it affects someone else.

00:47:09.420 --> 00:47:10.253
I hear that light.

00:47:10.253 --> 00:47:11.900
It's not a personal choice because there's a victim,

00:47:11.900 --> 00:47:14.200
but fundamentally it is a choice.

00:47:14.200 --> 00:47:16.070
When we go into a supermarket or a restaurant,

00:47:16.070 --> 00:47:19.190
what we choose to buy is up to us to decide,

00:47:19.190 --> 00:47:21.170
no one else can make that decision for us.

00:47:21.170 --> 00:47:23.960
So the first thing to recognize is it is a choice

00:47:23.960 --> 00:47:25.383
but then by the recognition that it's a choice,

00:47:25.383 --> 00:47:28.810
it means that we don't have to do that.

00:47:28.810 --> 00:47:30.280
And when we talk about moral issues,

00:47:30.280 --> 00:47:32.480
well, everything is a choice.

00:47:32.480 --> 00:47:37.160
I could leave today, find someone and mug them, rob them.

00:47:37.160 --> 00:47:40.770
I could find a dog and kick them and stomp them to death.

00:47:40.770 --> 00:47:42.800
I can choose to do all of these things,

00:47:42.800 --> 00:47:44.140
but they're not morally justified

00:47:44.140 --> 00:47:46.090
as a consequence of being a choice.

00:47:46.090 --> 00:47:48.500
So whilst we do to animals and what we choose to buy

00:47:48.500 --> 00:47:49.550
when it comes to animal products,

00:47:49.550 --> 00:47:51.990
isn't undeniably up to us to choose,

00:47:51.990 --> 00:47:52.823
that doesn't mean

00:47:52.823 --> 00:47:54.100
that it's justified as a consequence of that,

00:47:54.100 --> 00:47:58.190
because every action moral or immoral is a choice.

00:47:58.190 --> 00:47:59.680
And I suppose I always say,

00:47:59.680 --> 00:48:01.507
what about the choice of the animals?

00:48:01.507 --> 00:48:03.800
When we say about personal choice,

00:48:03.800 --> 00:48:04.820
often people make the argument

00:48:04.820 --> 00:48:07.210
that they're trying to protect people's sovereignty

00:48:07.210 --> 00:48:09.450
and people's ability to make individual decisions

00:48:09.450 --> 00:48:12.610
but what overlooks is the fact that our personal choice

00:48:12.610 --> 00:48:15.978
is impacting the the desire of trillions,

00:48:15.978 --> 00:48:17.830
of others around the world.

00:48:17.830 --> 00:48:20.150
And so what about their choice, if they had that choice,

00:48:20.150 --> 00:48:22.230
they wouldn't walk into a slaughterhouse in-

00:48:22.230 --> 00:48:24.643
We're so detached from that equation, though.

00:48:24.643 --> 00:48:27.220
It's very difficult for us to do that math

00:48:27.220 --> 00:48:30.660
because of the distance and the barriers that are put up

00:48:30.660 --> 00:48:32.840
to prevent us from really understanding that.

00:48:32.840 --> 00:48:36.130
Plus the fact that it feels like my personal choice

00:48:36.130 --> 00:48:40.060
has so little impact on this broader problem at large,

00:48:40.060 --> 00:48:41.820
me ordering a cheeseburger

00:48:41.820 --> 00:48:45.150
or picking up some ground beef at the supermarket,

00:48:45.150 --> 00:48:49.013
isn't gonna really impact the state of factory farming.

00:48:49.013 --> 00:48:52.430
But at the same I'm every mass movement for our history

00:48:52.430 --> 00:48:53.770
has been formative individuals.

00:48:53.770 --> 00:48:57.360
And so whilst as an individual making personal decisions,

00:48:57.360 --> 00:49:00.370
we may feel like the impact that we have is small

00:49:00.370 --> 00:49:02.970
and as an individual choice, it is.

00:49:02.970 --> 00:49:04.716
But an individual choice

00:49:04.716 --> 00:49:07.340
combined with millions of other individual choices

00:49:07.340 --> 00:49:08.700
starts to have a huge impact.

00:49:08.700 --> 00:49:11.320
And so it's about being empowered to recognize

00:49:11.320 --> 00:49:12.217
what we can achieve.

00:49:12.217 --> 00:49:15.430
And we look across the world and we look at so many problems

00:49:15.430 --> 00:49:16.723
and as individuals,

00:49:16.723 --> 00:49:19.340
there's very little we can do about that.

00:49:19.340 --> 00:49:21.790
We can donate to charities, but can we stop war?

00:49:21.790 --> 00:49:23.320
Not as an individual necessarily,

00:49:23.320 --> 00:49:25.200
we can go protests and marches.

00:49:25.200 --> 00:49:27.430
Sometimes that doesn't make a difference either, of course,

00:49:27.430 --> 00:49:29.640
but when it comes to the agricultural system

00:49:29.640 --> 00:49:32.930
and the way the food that we consumers produced

00:49:32.930 --> 00:49:34.720
and the consequences of that,

00:49:34.720 --> 00:49:36.620
is entirely something that we as individuals

00:49:36.620 --> 00:49:38.850
and consumers have the power to change.

00:49:38.850 --> 00:49:41.340
There are subsidy systems and there are legislative problems

00:49:41.340 --> 00:49:43.689
from governments and politicians.

00:49:43.689 --> 00:49:46.180
But fundamentally, if we, as consumers come together

00:49:46.180 --> 00:49:47.770
and vote our wallets for a different world,

00:49:47.770 --> 00:49:49.547
we can create a massive amount of change.

00:49:49.547 --> 00:49:52.834
And so it's one of those rare issues, potentially,

00:49:52.834 --> 00:49:54.730
where we as individuals have the power

00:49:54.730 --> 00:49:56.460
to completely change that system.

00:49:56.460 --> 00:49:58.610
So I think it's about being in power to recognize

00:49:58.610 --> 00:50:00.070
the responsibility,

00:50:00.070 --> 00:50:02.760
but also the ability that we have to bring about change.

00:50:02.760 --> 00:50:06.210
And recognizing that capitalism, fundamentally

00:50:06.210 --> 00:50:07.660
responds to shifts in demand.

00:50:08.620 --> 00:50:12.200
So if the demand shifts and people are desirous

00:50:12.200 --> 00:50:14.030
of plant-based milk options,

00:50:14.030 --> 00:50:16.170
that's what you're gonna see in the supermarket

00:50:16.170 --> 00:50:18.200
and of course, that is what we're seeing,

00:50:18.200 --> 00:50:20.872
we're seeing the proliferation of plant-based options,

00:50:20.872 --> 00:50:23.670
at not only restaurants across the world,

00:50:23.670 --> 00:50:24.950
but the fast food outlets,

00:50:24.950 --> 00:50:27.460
like they are responsive to consumer demand.

00:50:27.460 --> 00:50:30.630
And to the extent that we can consolidate

00:50:30.630 --> 00:50:33.860
and marshal the messaging around that demand

00:50:33.860 --> 00:50:37.033
and get more people enthusiastic about this.

00:50:37.033 --> 00:50:41.640
These companies, it's not mustache Sterling people

00:50:41.640 --> 00:50:42.790
sitting around a boardroom

00:50:42.790 --> 00:50:45.650
conspiring to make people unhealthy

00:50:45.650 --> 00:50:47.540
and kill as many animals as possible.

00:50:47.540 --> 00:50:49.830
They're trying to meet their quarterly earnings.

00:50:49.830 --> 00:50:51.883
And if they figure out a way to do that,

00:50:51.883 --> 00:50:55.170
by dent of exploring the plant-based world,

00:50:55.170 --> 00:50:57.030
then that's exactly what they're going to do.

00:50:57.030 --> 00:50:59.240
So I think it really is powerful,

00:50:59.240 --> 00:51:02.880
and it's not dissimilar from this discussion

00:51:02.880 --> 00:51:04.210
around individuation,

00:51:04.210 --> 00:51:05.070
in the same sense

00:51:05.070 --> 00:51:08.893
that we struggle to individuate these animals.

00:51:09.750 --> 00:51:13.670
We have to also be able to understand

00:51:13.670 --> 00:51:15.530
that the individuals amongst us

00:51:15.530 --> 00:51:19.980
comprise a greater hole that is indeed very powerful.

00:51:19.980 --> 00:51:23.760
And YouTube and podcasts and things like that,

00:51:23.760 --> 00:51:27.170
I think have played a huge part in,

00:51:27.170 --> 00:51:30.540
at a minimum, shifting the consciousness

00:51:30.540 --> 00:51:34.230
of the younger generation to really care about these things.

00:51:34.230 --> 00:51:35.063
Definitely.

00:51:36.090 --> 00:51:39.275
What's unique about this issue in a sense is,

00:51:39.275 --> 00:51:42.380
throughout history, we've never had the opportunity

00:51:42.380 --> 00:51:44.490
to hear the other side of the story.

00:51:44.490 --> 00:51:46.040
Parents, grandparents,

00:51:46.040 --> 00:51:49.314
they were raised in until you the past 10 years or so.

00:51:49.314 --> 00:51:51.640
What we knew about animal agriculture

00:51:51.640 --> 00:51:53.540
came from animal agriculture.

00:51:53.540 --> 00:51:55.140
We were told the animals are treated well,

00:51:55.140 --> 00:51:56.560
why would we have question that?

00:51:56.560 --> 00:51:57.490
We go into a soup market

00:51:57.490 --> 00:52:00.572
and we see these promotional posters telling us one thing,

00:52:00.572 --> 00:52:02.910
this is all we're feds, and this is all we hear.

00:52:02.910 --> 00:52:05.150
So what's unique about now

00:52:05.150 --> 00:52:07.380
is that we have this opportunity to go,

00:52:07.380 --> 00:52:09.440
well, actually, is there some thing else to this is,

00:52:09.440 --> 00:52:10.390
is there more to this,

00:52:10.390 --> 00:52:13.110
is there an another side to this coin?

00:52:13.110 --> 00:52:16.670
And when we can do that, look into that, see what happens,

00:52:16.670 --> 00:52:19.050
there is this now growing awareness

00:52:19.050 --> 00:52:21.270
about the impact that animal agriculture

00:52:21.270 --> 00:52:22.930
has on the environment, on our health.

00:52:22.930 --> 00:52:25.640
These things are stemming from a public awareness,

00:52:25.640 --> 00:52:27.130
a shift in public consciousness

00:52:27.130 --> 00:52:30.400
that is also stemming from a proliferation of information

00:52:30.400 --> 00:52:33.230
through podcasts and YouTube that hasn't existed before.

00:52:33.230 --> 00:52:35.410
So it's creating a unique situation

00:52:35.410 --> 00:52:37.490
for us to engage with ideas

00:52:37.490 --> 00:52:39.340
that potentially older generations

00:52:39.340 --> 00:52:41.340
didn't have the luxury of engaging with.

00:52:42.510 --> 00:52:43.800
I hear you Ed,

00:52:43.800 --> 00:52:48.777
but I procure all of my meat from regenerative farms.

00:52:50.370 --> 00:52:54.290
It's all locally raised, hormone free, the whole thing.

00:52:54.290 --> 00:52:56.433
So I'm doing my part.

00:52:57.540 --> 00:52:58.950
It's a common argument now, isn't it?

00:52:58.950 --> 00:53:00.300
Regenerative.
And those animals

00:53:00.300 --> 00:53:03.100
are important in regenerating the soil

00:53:03.100 --> 00:53:06.630
which is a key to solving the climate crisis.

00:53:06.630 --> 00:53:09.650
And regenerative is like the new sustainable word,

00:53:09.650 --> 00:53:11.960
is the new the buzzword that these industries

00:53:11.960 --> 00:53:13.413
are using a lot.

00:53:14.340 --> 00:53:15.630
I think the first thing to recognize

00:53:15.630 --> 00:53:19.100
is this idea that people are supporting these systems

00:53:19.100 --> 00:53:21.320
comes from a good feeling.

00:53:21.320 --> 00:53:22.153
It comes from the sense

00:53:22.153 --> 00:53:24.160
that we need to do something differently,

00:53:24.160 --> 00:53:25.650
but we're kind of still stuck in this paradigm

00:53:25.650 --> 00:53:27.340
of wanting to continue consuming animals,

00:53:27.340 --> 00:53:29.327
so we're trying to find rationalizations to continue that,

00:53:29.327 --> 00:53:32.370
and this idea of regenerative beef farming

00:53:32.370 --> 00:53:33.710
or lamb farming comes along

00:53:33.710 --> 00:53:34.640
and it ticks the boxes

00:53:34.640 --> 00:53:36.510
of allowing us to continue eating animals,

00:53:36.510 --> 00:53:38.440
but also means we can regenerate the soils

00:53:38.440 --> 00:53:39.337
and it gets rid of all the problems.

00:53:39.337 --> 00:53:42.440
And it alleviates all of that cognitive dissonance.

00:53:42.440 --> 00:53:45.940
It takes everything that we want, but is it true?

00:53:45.940 --> 00:53:47.490
That's when it becomes a problem.

00:53:47.490 --> 00:53:49.910
So the idea of regenerative animal farming is,

00:53:49.910 --> 00:53:50.950
as you rightfully say,

00:53:50.950 --> 00:53:52.610
it creates a system

00:53:52.610 --> 00:53:54.740
which can put nutrients back in the soil.

00:53:54.740 --> 00:53:57.380
It reduces the use of kind of artificial fertilizers

00:53:57.380 --> 00:54:00.732
and crop production, can reduce the strain on places like,

00:54:00.732 --> 00:54:02.070
the Amazon rain forest and such,

00:54:02.070 --> 00:54:04.860
which is currently being decimated for feed production,

00:54:04.860 --> 00:54:08.080
and cattle grazing, and all these terrible things.

00:54:08.080 --> 00:54:11.210
But when we think about regenerative farming,

00:54:11.210 --> 00:54:12.170
what we have to recognize

00:54:12.170 --> 00:54:14.950
is that it uses up an incredible amount of land.

00:54:14.950 --> 00:54:16.520
So if we think about the us, for example,

00:54:16.520 --> 00:54:17.540
so the USDA states

00:54:17.540 --> 00:54:20.570
that currently about 41% of the land mass of the US

00:54:20.570 --> 00:54:22.260
is used for animal farming,

00:54:22.260 --> 00:54:23.930
when it comes to plant farming

00:54:23.930 --> 00:54:25.940
just for human consumptions it's only 4%.

00:54:25.940 --> 00:54:28.540
So in the us 10 times more land

00:54:28.540 --> 00:54:30.170
is used to produce animal products

00:54:30.170 --> 00:54:32.830
than plant products for humans directly.

00:54:32.830 --> 00:54:34.620
And when we look at beef farming in particular,

00:54:34.620 --> 00:54:37.250
well, it's about 20% of the entire land mass

00:54:37.250 --> 00:54:41.420
of the 48 contiguous US states is used to produce beef,

00:54:41.420 --> 00:54:43.990
which makes about 3% of our caloric needs.

00:54:43.990 --> 00:54:46.433
So this is with a system that uses feed lots.

00:54:46.433 --> 00:54:49.510
this is with an efficient system when it comes to land use.

00:54:49.510 --> 00:54:53.790
So simply put, even if we believed in regenerative farming,

00:54:53.790 --> 00:54:55.750
we cannot continue to consume as much meat,

00:54:55.750 --> 00:54:57.370
dairy, and eggs as we currently do.

00:54:57.370 --> 00:54:59.300
It'll be a fraction of that anyway,

00:54:59.300 --> 00:55:01.180
because there isn't the land or the resources

00:55:01.180 --> 00:55:03.090
to produce the animal products or the quantity

00:55:03.090 --> 00:55:04.810
that we currently consume use in these systems.

00:55:04.810 --> 00:55:06.980
So even if it was a viable alternative,

00:55:06.980 --> 00:55:08.210
we'd still be dramatically

00:55:08.210 --> 00:55:10.120
reduce the amount that we consume.

00:55:10.120 --> 00:55:12.170
But when we look at regenerative farming,

00:55:12.170 --> 00:55:14.010
there's a couple of aspects to look at,

00:55:14.010 --> 00:55:16.842
first is biodiversity and second is emissions.

00:55:16.842 --> 00:55:17.890
Those are like the two main things

00:55:17.890 --> 00:55:21.150
that proponents of this system of farming will talk about.

00:55:21.150 --> 00:55:23.410
So when we look at biodiversity to begin with,

00:55:23.410 --> 00:55:25.020
the idea is that grazing animals

00:55:25.020 --> 00:55:28.310
increases biodiversity because of monocropping.

00:55:28.310 --> 00:55:30.170
And of course in indoor factory farming

00:55:30.170 --> 00:55:33.360
is terrible for biodiversity which it is,

00:55:33.360 --> 00:55:35.500
but at the same time is grazing animals

00:55:35.500 --> 00:55:38.910
the best thing for biodiversity, or is there an alternative.

00:55:38.910 --> 00:55:40.240
And when we look at biodiversity,

00:55:40.240 --> 00:55:43.290
it's very simple to recognize that forests, woodlands,

00:55:43.290 --> 00:55:46.366
long grass Meadows, and wildflower Meadows

00:55:46.366 --> 00:55:50.080
increase by more than grazing pasture lands do.

00:55:50.080 --> 00:55:51.140
So what we need to do

00:55:51.140 --> 00:55:54.420
is work out how we can maximize biodiversity gains.

00:55:54.420 --> 00:55:56.007
So when we look at animal agriculture

00:55:56.007 --> 00:55:57.560
and we look at it globally,

00:55:57.560 --> 00:56:02.000
it uses 76%, sorry, it uses 83% of all agricultural land.

00:56:02.000 --> 00:56:05.350
So over 4/5 of agricultural land is used to graze animals.

00:56:05.350 --> 00:56:07.160
Now, if we switched to a plant-based diets,

00:56:07.160 --> 00:56:09.810
we could reduce the amount of agricultural land that we need

00:56:09.810 --> 00:56:11.940
by about 75%

00:56:11.940 --> 00:56:14.340
and that's feeding everyone on a plant-based diet.

00:56:14.340 --> 00:56:16.980
We can still reduce that amount of land by 75%.

00:56:16.980 --> 00:56:18.710
So that means that we have all this land now

00:56:18.710 --> 00:56:21.153
that we no longer need to use for agriculture,

00:56:21.153 --> 00:56:23.037
that we can do something else with.

00:56:23.037 --> 00:56:26.530
And the primary thing we can do with that land is rewild it.

00:56:26.530 --> 00:56:28.490
And that just basically means returning the land

00:56:28.490 --> 00:56:29.740
that we currently use for agriculture

00:56:29.740 --> 00:56:31.630
back to some form of natural state,

00:56:31.630 --> 00:56:34.800
whether that swamps, peatlands, forests, Woodlands,

00:56:34.800 --> 00:56:36.720
wild flower Meadows, whatever it may be,

00:56:36.720 --> 00:56:38.650
we can return that land back to nature

00:56:38.650 --> 00:56:40.070
and as a consequence increase

00:56:40.070 --> 00:56:42.400
the fauna of flora of the world,

00:56:42.400 --> 00:56:44.600
increase the biodiversity that exists.

00:56:44.600 --> 00:56:45.433
So that's what we need to do

00:56:45.433 --> 00:56:47.990
if we want to prioritize biodiversity gains.

00:56:47.990 --> 00:56:50.640
When it comes to emissions,

00:56:50.640 --> 00:56:54.766
there is an idea that grazing animals, ruminant animals,

00:56:54.766 --> 00:56:58.250
sequesters carbon into the soil, which it does,

00:56:58.250 --> 00:57:00.480
and the reason it does that is through photosynthesis.

00:57:00.480 --> 00:57:03.980
So when an animal grazes there to turning up the roots,

00:57:03.980 --> 00:57:04.930
they're consuming the grass,

00:57:04.930 --> 00:57:06.430
which is encouraging and stimulating

00:57:06.430 --> 00:57:07.830
the regrowth of these plants,

00:57:07.830 --> 00:57:09.750
which the sequesters carbon through photosynthesis

00:57:09.750 --> 00:57:11.240
and stores carbon in the soil.

00:57:11.240 --> 00:57:12.073
And something about

00:57:12.073 --> 00:57:15.210
the way that they're walking on the land and seeding it

00:57:15.210 --> 00:57:19.170
in some respects has some sort of beneficial impact.

00:57:19.170 --> 00:57:21.330
Yeah, and then the manure

00:57:21.330 --> 00:57:24.136
replaces some of the lost nutrients in the soil.

00:57:24.136 --> 00:57:26.159
But there's a couple of ways looking at it,

00:57:26.159 --> 00:57:27.040
firstly, from an emissions perspective,

00:57:27.040 --> 00:57:28.998
when we look at the meta analyses

00:57:28.998 --> 00:57:31.243
on these farming systems,

00:57:31.243 --> 00:57:34.870
they're still producing net emissions.

00:57:34.870 --> 00:57:37.180
So the grazing animals are still producing emissions.

00:57:37.180 --> 00:57:39.953
And even in the most generous circumstances,

00:57:39.953 --> 00:57:43.720
we're looking at only a 20 to 60% of emissions being offset

00:57:43.720 --> 00:57:45.384
in these generative farming scenarios.

00:57:45.384 --> 00:57:47.910
But I think what's really important about this is,

00:57:47.910 --> 00:57:49.910
whilst soil is a carbon sink,

00:57:49.910 --> 00:57:52.320
it's not a limitless carbon sink.

00:57:52.320 --> 00:57:53.500
So after a period of time

00:57:53.500 --> 00:57:56.130
soils reach something called soil carbon equilibrium,

00:57:56.130 --> 00:57:57.310
which means the amount of carbon

00:57:57.310 --> 00:58:00.210
that can be stored in the soils is, is maximized.

00:58:00.210 --> 00:58:02.560
So at that point, even if grazing animals

00:58:02.560 --> 00:58:04.800
offset all of the emissions that they produce,

00:58:04.800 --> 00:58:06.200
which it doesn't, but even if it did,

00:58:06.200 --> 00:58:10.265
at some point, the soil reaches full saturation of carbon.

00:58:10.265 --> 00:58:12.315
Yeah, you saturate it and at that point

00:58:13.640 --> 00:58:16.340
it's net emissions just-
100% emissions.

00:58:16.340 --> 00:58:17.720
Not sequestration.

00:58:18.590 --> 00:58:20.733
It really puts to the test,

00:58:20.733 --> 00:58:24.490
the whole Allan Savory kind of philosophy around this.

00:58:24.490 --> 00:58:27.380
And it's a study or an article called

00:58:27.380 --> 00:58:28.630
like Grazed and Confused.

00:58:28.630 --> 00:58:30.050
That is a really great read

00:58:30.050 --> 00:58:32.550
that explains all of this in great detail.

00:58:32.550 --> 00:58:34.470
Well, Allan Savory released this Ted talk,

00:58:34.470 --> 00:58:35.990
which went super viral,

00:58:35.990 --> 00:58:37.440
where he made the claim, I believe,

00:58:37.440 --> 00:58:40.890
where it's something like, if just 50% of pasture lands

00:58:40.890 --> 00:58:43.240
could reverse climate change or something

00:58:43.240 --> 00:58:46.110
if there were grazed in the manner that he advocates for.

00:58:46.110 --> 00:58:49.220
Which is an unsubstantiated claim and graze and confuse

00:58:49.220 --> 00:58:51.906
is a great example, 'cause it looked at 300 sources

00:58:51.906 --> 00:58:54.780
and it looked at all these farming scenarios

00:58:54.780 --> 00:58:56.430
to try and work out whether there was any veracity

00:58:56.430 --> 00:58:58.730
to the claims being made and realized that,

00:58:58.730 --> 00:59:00.985
well, yes, it can sequester some carbon,

00:59:00.985 --> 00:59:04.590
it's nowhere near gonna offset the emissions.

00:59:04.590 --> 00:59:06.700
And with the soil-carbon equilibrium,

00:59:06.700 --> 00:59:08.890
you reach a point where you're not offsetting anything.

00:59:08.890 --> 00:59:12.120
So as a long term strategy, even if it did work,

00:59:12.120 --> 00:59:13.340
there's no legs to it.

00:59:13.340 --> 00:59:15.130
'cause at some point you either have to take the animals

00:59:15.130 --> 00:59:16.350
and farm them on more land,

00:59:16.350 --> 00:59:18.930
which means creating more agricultural land

00:59:18.930 --> 00:59:20.398
or you have to stop the animal farming.

00:59:20.398 --> 00:59:23.340
So when we talk about sequestering carbon,

00:59:23.340 --> 00:59:24.390
the question becomes well,

00:59:24.390 --> 00:59:25.293
is grazing animals the only way

00:59:25.293 --> 00:59:27.370
that we can put carbon in the soils.

00:59:27.370 --> 00:59:28.850
Because by having to offset

00:59:28.850 --> 00:59:30.180
the emissions the animals produce,

00:59:30.180 --> 00:59:32.810
we're limiting the capacity for our soils

00:59:32.810 --> 00:59:34.100
to sequester carbon.

00:59:34.100 --> 00:59:35.930
Why are we trying to offset these emissions

00:59:35.930 --> 00:59:37.757
when we can just not have these emissions to begin with?

00:59:37.757 --> 00:59:40.270
And if we weren't grazing cattle and lambs and such,

00:59:40.270 --> 00:59:42.740
we won't have to offset the emissions they produce.

00:59:42.740 --> 00:59:44.920
So again, when we look at sequestering carbon,

00:59:44.920 --> 00:59:46.130
the best thing that we can do

00:59:46.130 --> 00:59:49.920
is create forests and Woodlands and long grass Meadows

00:59:49.920 --> 00:59:50.850
and wild flower Meadows

00:59:50.850 --> 00:59:53.260
because that not only increases by diversity,

00:59:53.260 --> 00:59:54.740
but it also sequesters carbon

00:59:54.740 --> 00:59:56.450
and does so in a way about having to offset

00:59:56.450 --> 00:59:57.420
any of the negative emissions

00:59:57.420 --> 00:59:59.670
produced by the animals in the first place.

00:59:59.670 --> 01:00:02.280
And just to be clear where generative farming

01:00:02.280 --> 01:00:04.420
is far better than factory farming,

01:00:04.420 --> 01:00:08.333
it is a step in a positive direction to some extent,

01:00:09.810 --> 01:00:14.368
but it will never be able to scale the meat,

01:00:14.368 --> 01:00:15.770
current meat demand.

01:00:15.770 --> 01:00:18.670
It's not really a solution to any of these problems.

01:00:18.670 --> 01:00:22.670
And the argument that it is a valid participant

01:00:22.670 --> 01:00:24.170
in reversing climate change

01:00:24.170 --> 01:00:26.850
is really the error in all of this.

01:00:26.850 --> 01:00:28.509
It's dangerous.

01:00:28.509 --> 01:00:30.430
The graze and confused study

01:00:30.430 --> 01:00:32.782
said that only one gram protein per person per day

01:00:32.782 --> 01:00:35.850
on average globally comes from solely grass fed animals,

01:00:35.850 --> 01:00:37.000
just one gram.

01:00:37.000 --> 01:00:39.410
We need obviously a lot more than that.

01:00:39.410 --> 01:00:41.025
And when we look at animal products,

01:00:41.025 --> 01:00:43.570
I think is something like, 20, 22 grams or something

01:00:43.570 --> 01:00:45.560
is maybe coming from animal products right now.

01:00:45.560 --> 01:00:47.590
So if we want to get the protein

01:00:47.590 --> 01:00:49.390
that we get from animals right now,

01:00:49.390 --> 01:00:52.780
but only from systems of solely grass fed production,

01:00:52.780 --> 01:00:54.640
well, it's just not gonna be possible.

01:00:54.640 --> 01:00:58.000
The United Nation says that 26% of Earth's ice-free land

01:00:58.000 --> 01:00:59.490
is used for grazing animals,

01:00:59.490 --> 01:01:00.710
that that's currently what it is.

01:01:00.710 --> 01:01:02.810
So it has its benefits

01:01:02.810 --> 01:01:04.560
when you compare it to factory farming.

01:01:04.560 --> 01:01:07.390
Obviously these systems that these regenerative proponents

01:01:07.390 --> 01:01:09.660
are talking about are substantially better in many ways

01:01:09.660 --> 01:01:11.960
to the system that we currently have of mass feed production

01:01:11.960 --> 01:01:14.640
and factory farming and the huge manure lagoons

01:01:14.640 --> 01:01:17.340
that are obviously just terrible in so many ways.

01:01:17.340 --> 01:01:19.320
But the question is, is it the best thing that we can do?

01:01:19.320 --> 01:01:21.650
And does it genuinely solve the problems

01:01:21.650 --> 01:01:22.550
that we're trying to address?

01:01:22.550 --> 01:01:25.053
And when you view it against the alternatives,

01:01:26.610 --> 01:01:28.950
a plant-based food system that's more sustainable,

01:01:28.950 --> 01:01:31.470
but also reworded landscapes,

01:01:31.470 --> 01:01:34.360
those concepts and that system of agriculture

01:01:34.360 --> 01:01:37.140
and land management is substantially better

01:01:37.140 --> 01:01:40.150
in every single way than a system of regenerative farming.

01:01:40.150 --> 01:01:43.230
And often proponents of this way of thinking

01:01:43.230 --> 01:01:45.710
the regenerative animal agricultural way of thinking,

01:01:45.710 --> 01:01:47.090
we'll try and point the finger

01:01:47.090 --> 01:01:49.587
at the way the current plant-based food system is set up.

01:01:49.587 --> 01:01:51.320
And I see a lot of people-

01:01:51.320 --> 01:01:52.820
You're gonna (indistinct) this,

01:01:52.820 --> 01:01:55.413
but you're eating your monocropped,

01:01:55.413 --> 01:01:59.550
factory farmed plant foods.

01:01:59.550 --> 01:02:01.670
Well, firstly, if we swapped to a plant-based diet,

01:02:01.670 --> 01:02:04.820
we would reduce arable land by 20% anyway.

01:02:04.820 --> 01:02:06.530
So even just switching to a plant-based diet

01:02:06.530 --> 01:02:07.700
would still be preferable

01:02:07.700 --> 01:02:09.530
from an arable monocropping perspective,

01:02:09.530 --> 01:02:10.900
just straight off the bat.

01:02:10.900 --> 01:02:12.810
But I think what's disingenuous about that,

01:02:12.810 --> 01:02:13.680
is it's trying to say

01:02:13.680 --> 01:02:16.250
that there's no better way of producing plants.

01:02:16.250 --> 01:02:17.730
I recognize that the plant-based food system

01:02:17.730 --> 01:02:20.630
is far from perfect, it's significantly better,

01:02:20.630 --> 01:02:22.810
but it doesn't mean that it's as good as it could be.

01:02:22.810 --> 01:02:25.030
So by trying to say that regenerative animal farming

01:02:25.030 --> 01:02:27.270
isn't a viable solution,

01:02:27.270 --> 01:02:28.420
isn't saying that we shouldn't try

01:02:28.420 --> 01:02:29.483
and make what we currently do better

01:02:29.483 --> 01:02:31.170
when it comes to plant farming.

01:02:31.170 --> 01:02:32.400
And we should be adopting

01:02:32.400 --> 01:02:34.650
better forms of plant-based agriculture.

01:02:34.650 --> 01:02:37.360
Regenerative plant-based farms.

01:02:37.360 --> 01:02:39.230
Bingo.
Why not just do that.

01:02:39.230 --> 01:02:41.942
Exactly, and then we've got things like vertical farms,

01:02:41.942 --> 01:02:44.150
which an incredibly exciting prospect,

01:02:44.150 --> 01:02:45.750
even when it comes to lab grow meat,

01:02:45.750 --> 01:02:48.390
if you wanna look at producing meat sustainably,

01:02:48.390 --> 01:02:50.620
lab grown meat will be when it's scaled up

01:02:50.620 --> 01:02:52.820
and financially efficient enough

01:02:52.820 --> 01:02:55.580
will be more sustainable than regenerative farming anyway.

01:02:55.580 --> 01:02:59.440
So it's seen as kind of like this almost compromise,

01:02:59.440 --> 01:03:02.100
but effectively it's still causing huge amounts of damage

01:03:02.100 --> 01:03:02.933
to our environment

01:03:02.933 --> 01:03:05.940
and of course resulting in premature death of animals.

01:03:05.940 --> 01:03:07.340
And there's an alternative that exists

01:03:07.340 --> 01:03:09.500
and will continue to become more sustainable

01:03:09.500 --> 01:03:11.010
into the future as we engage

01:03:11.010 --> 01:03:12.570
with these more technological advancements

01:03:12.570 --> 01:03:14.840
within plant-based production as well.

01:03:14.840 --> 01:03:18.210
So that kind of covers the environmental arguments

01:03:18.210 --> 01:03:20.120
around regenerative agriculture.

01:03:20.120 --> 01:03:22.260
But another thing that comes into play

01:03:22.260 --> 01:03:24.522
when people are opting for grass fed beef,

01:03:24.522 --> 01:03:26.820
is this idea that these animals

01:03:26.820 --> 01:03:30.100
are living a much higher quality of life.

01:03:30.100 --> 01:03:31.420
I think there's a lot of confusion

01:03:31.420 --> 01:03:33.590
over the definition of grass fed

01:03:33.590 --> 01:03:38.570
and how that definition is sort of weaponized

01:03:38.570 --> 01:03:43.570
in a way that people, consumers who are trying to do better,

01:03:43.750 --> 01:03:45.550
might not be aware of.

01:03:45.550 --> 01:03:48.600
The idea behind grass feds,

01:03:48.600 --> 01:03:50.410
a lot of it is kind of a marketing term

01:03:50.410 --> 01:03:51.490
because grass fed animals

01:03:51.490 --> 01:03:54.390
can still be fated on feed lots at the end of their life.

01:03:54.390 --> 01:03:57.510
Like the last 15% of their life or something like that,

01:03:57.510 --> 01:03:59.210
where they go, and then they're just eating grain

01:03:59.210 --> 01:04:00.640
to get them as fat as possible.

01:04:00.640 --> 01:04:02.460
Exactly.
And it's still considered,

01:04:02.460 --> 01:04:04.420
it can be label grass fed.
That's right.

01:04:04.420 --> 01:04:06.890
And I think the percentages may be vary country to country.

01:04:06.890 --> 01:04:09.660
I think in Australia it might be as much as 25%

01:04:09.660 --> 01:04:11.010
of their life, the final quarter.

01:04:11.010 --> 01:04:13.140
So it doesn't mean that these animals

01:04:13.140 --> 01:04:14.660
are very solely in these pastures.

01:04:14.660 --> 01:04:15.493
And even on these pastures,

01:04:15.493 --> 01:04:18.197
they can be supplemented with different feeds anyway.

01:04:18.197 --> 01:04:20.450
And also there are still components

01:04:20.450 --> 01:04:21.810
of animal agriculture that exists

01:04:21.810 --> 01:04:24.077
such as the mutilations, the branding,

01:04:24.077 --> 01:04:25.330
these things still exist,

01:04:25.330 --> 01:04:27.500
even in grass fed farming productions.

01:04:27.500 --> 01:04:29.580
And they're still killed in slaughterhouses as well.

01:04:29.580 --> 01:04:33.500
So, people often say to me I'm against factory farming,

01:04:33.500 --> 01:04:35.930
everyone, even if they support it, says they are against it

01:04:35.930 --> 01:04:38.160
and likes to have this aspiration supporting

01:04:38.160 --> 01:04:40.470
these kind of grass fed systems,

01:04:40.470 --> 01:04:42.405
because again, it's seen compromise,

01:04:42.405 --> 01:04:45.570
but ultimately when we view this issue

01:04:46.930 --> 01:04:49.530
at its kind of most barest form,

01:04:49.530 --> 01:04:50.650
it's again about this notion

01:04:50.650 --> 01:04:52.720
of reducing exploitation and suffering and it's-

01:04:52.720 --> 01:04:54.370
The animal's still getting a bolt in the head

01:04:54.370 --> 01:04:55.720
and its throat cut, no matter what.

01:04:55.720 --> 01:04:57.760
So it may be better quote/unquote,

01:04:57.760 --> 01:05:00.018
but is it objectively the best thing that we can do?

01:05:00.018 --> 01:05:00.851
No.

01:05:00.851 --> 01:05:02.720
So we shouldn't be comparing this little scenario

01:05:02.720 --> 01:05:03.997
to the extreme of factory farming,

01:05:03.997 --> 01:05:06.550
we should be comparing it to the other side,

01:05:06.550 --> 01:05:09.110
which is, not exploiting these animals in the first place

01:05:09.110 --> 01:05:11.030
when it comes to the environment, when it comes to health

01:05:11.030 --> 01:05:12.510
and when it comes to the ethics,

01:05:12.510 --> 01:05:13.850
then not exploiting animals

01:05:13.850 --> 01:05:15.870
is better in all of those different ways,

01:05:15.870 --> 01:05:18.830
even compared to this middle kind of compromise if you like.

01:05:18.830 --> 01:05:21.910
That opens the door to a conversation around

01:05:21.910 --> 01:05:26.210
the sort of dark side of greenwashing or incremental change

01:05:26.210 --> 01:05:29.780
because these small changes and the marketing

01:05:29.780 --> 01:05:33.410
that kind of wraps around them

01:05:33.410 --> 01:05:36.490
makes people feel better about their choices

01:05:36.490 --> 01:05:40.530
and kind of ameliorates any type of activism

01:05:40.530 --> 01:05:43.430
because people are sort of sedated

01:05:43.430 --> 01:05:45.360
into believing that their actions

01:05:45.360 --> 01:05:48.756
are lining up with their values, when in fact they're not.

01:05:48.756 --> 01:05:51.547
We all like to be told good things about our bad habits

01:05:51.547 --> 01:05:56.130
and greenwashing and this kind of concept humane washing

01:05:56.130 --> 01:06:00.070
are really important tools that industries will use

01:06:00.070 --> 01:06:02.620
and companies will use to make us feel good

01:06:02.620 --> 01:06:03.540
about our bad habits.

01:06:03.540 --> 01:06:05.000
And so a lot of us are familiar

01:06:05.000 --> 01:06:06.200
with the concept of greenwashing

01:06:06.200 --> 01:06:08.520
or companies trying to overstate

01:06:08.520 --> 01:06:10.500
the sustainability impacts of their products,

01:06:10.500 --> 01:06:12.160
but humane washing is something similar.

01:06:12.160 --> 01:06:13.170
We're going to a supermarket,

01:06:13.170 --> 01:06:15.060
we see a box of free range X

01:06:15.060 --> 01:06:16.880
that's an example of humane washing.

01:06:16.880 --> 01:06:18.640
It makes us think we're buying a product

01:06:18.640 --> 01:06:21.120
that is quote/unquote humane.

01:06:21.120 --> 01:06:22.520
And the names of the farms

01:06:22.520 --> 01:06:24.730
and the picture of the red tractor

01:06:24.730 --> 01:06:27.778
and the beautiful looking fields and all of that.

01:06:27.778 --> 01:06:28.733
Exactly.

01:06:28.733 --> 01:06:30.180
Or the five step welfare program or whatever it might be.

01:06:30.180 --> 01:06:31.503
There are so many examples of it

01:06:31.503 --> 01:06:33.870
because fundamentally these industries

01:06:33.870 --> 01:06:37.010
and indeed the suppliers, the supermarkets

01:06:37.010 --> 01:06:39.230
know that we care about these issues,

01:06:39.230 --> 01:06:40.063
they understand that.

01:06:40.063 --> 01:06:44.950
And they know that if we had, or we saw a label that said,

01:06:44.950 --> 01:06:47.480
this piece of pig that you're about to buy

01:06:47.480 --> 01:06:50.270
was slaughtered in the gas chamber, using this method

01:06:50.270 --> 01:06:51.330
and they were raised in this way

01:06:51.330 --> 01:06:52.880
and here's a picture of the farm.

01:06:52.880 --> 01:06:54.600
We would probably go,

01:06:54.600 --> 01:06:56.824
that tin of chickpeas seems pretty good now.

01:06:56.824 --> 01:06:59.000
So they have to sell us an ideal

01:06:59.000 --> 01:07:00.600
and they have to sell us what we want to buy.

01:07:00.600 --> 01:07:02.820
And that's really dangerous because what we want to buy

01:07:02.820 --> 01:07:05.400
is at complete odds of what we're actually buying.

01:07:05.400 --> 01:07:07.125
So it's about being, I suppose,

01:07:07.125 --> 01:07:10.070
again, a bit conscious of these choices that we're making

01:07:10.070 --> 01:07:12.420
and the words humane is a really interesting word

01:07:12.420 --> 01:07:14.790
because we use it all the time

01:07:14.790 --> 01:07:16.850
to describe what we do to animals.

01:07:16.850 --> 01:07:18.220
It's acceptable to kill an animal

01:07:18.220 --> 01:07:19.630
if it's done in a humane way

01:07:19.630 --> 01:07:22.310
or, what happens in China is obviously terrible,

01:07:22.310 --> 01:07:23.700
but in the US or the UK,

01:07:23.700 --> 01:07:25.230
our slaughterhouse is a humane

01:07:25.230 --> 01:07:27.700
because they have these regulations.

01:07:27.700 --> 01:07:30.767
But the word humane, if we open up a thesaurus

01:07:30.767 --> 01:07:34.830
and we find synonyms means compassionate, benevolent, kind.

01:07:34.830 --> 01:07:37.160
And if you're a humane person, you're inherently,

01:07:37.160 --> 01:07:39.352
I guess, a good person, a kind person.

01:07:39.352 --> 01:07:42.690
But then how do we benevolently or compassionately

01:07:42.690 --> 01:07:45.670
exploit or kill someone against their need?

01:07:45.670 --> 01:07:48.160
And sometimes people say you can't call an animal a someone,

01:07:48.160 --> 01:07:50.738
I've heard this a lot recently, they're not a someone.

01:07:50.738 --> 01:07:52.240
And I say, "Well, if they're not as someone,

01:07:52.240 --> 01:07:53.720
what are they, something."

01:07:53.720 --> 01:07:56.208
They're either an individual or an object.

01:07:56.208 --> 01:07:59.260
When we use language in this way,

01:07:59.260 --> 01:08:02.510
again, reaffirms this notion that what we do to animals

01:08:02.510 --> 01:08:04.220
is a moral issue, when we recognize

01:08:04.220 --> 01:08:06.870
that they are someones and not some things,

01:08:06.870 --> 01:08:10.270
it makes the notion of doing something negatively to them,

01:08:10.270 --> 01:08:11.670
cutting their throat, for example,

01:08:11.670 --> 01:08:14.910
the idea that that's humane obviously becomes nonsensical,

01:08:14.910 --> 01:08:17.150
but we're constantly sold this idea

01:08:17.150 --> 01:08:20.010
of being high welfare, compassionate, nice for the animals,

01:08:20.010 --> 01:08:22.196
the animals are happy to be a part of the system

01:08:22.196 --> 01:08:25.930
when deep down, I think we all secretly realize

01:08:25.930 --> 01:08:28.000
that that's not necessarily true,

01:08:28.000 --> 01:08:29.563
but it's easier to believe the lies sometimes

01:08:29.563 --> 01:08:31.620
than is to accept the uncomfortableness

01:08:31.620 --> 01:08:34.223
of the truth in that scenario.

01:08:36.330 --> 01:08:37.720
Sorry to interrupt the flow.

