01:10:07.900 --> 01:10:10.303
All right, let's get back to the show.

01:10:11.320 --> 01:10:15.450
A lot of that is rooted in a lack of transparency,

01:10:15.450 --> 01:10:18.190
like this, inability to make a connection, obviously,

01:10:18.190 --> 01:10:21.520
and I watched this debate that you did on television

01:10:21.520 --> 01:10:23.687
with the dairy farmer who said,

01:10:23.687 --> 01:10:26.130
"Well, we are transparent and we have people come

01:10:26.130 --> 01:10:29.060
and they visit the farm and we show them everything."

01:10:29.060 --> 01:10:29.893
And you said,

01:10:29.893 --> 01:10:33.170
"Do you show them the part where you slit the throat?"

01:10:33.170 --> 01:10:34.967
And he said something like,

01:10:34.967 --> 01:10:37.539
"I'd like to think that we're transparent about that."

01:10:37.539 --> 01:10:40.527
And the host of the show to her credit said,

01:10:40.527 --> 01:10:42.150
"Well, it's a yes or no thing.

01:10:42.150 --> 01:10:45.230
Like, are you transparent about that or not?"

01:10:45.230 --> 01:10:47.170
Clearly he's not, but he's on television,

01:10:47.170 --> 01:10:50.107
he's trying to get through this experience.

01:10:50.107 --> 01:10:52.250
And I don't see a future

01:10:52.250 --> 01:10:54.889
in which there will be transparency around that.

01:10:54.889 --> 01:10:58.440
And that is very much at odds with your generation

01:10:58.440 --> 01:11:00.410
and the generation beneath you,

01:11:00.410 --> 01:11:02.670
that demands a level of transparency

01:11:02.670 --> 01:11:06.630
that my generation has sort of been myopic about.

01:11:06.630 --> 01:11:09.360
Like that there is a consciousness

01:11:09.360 --> 01:11:11.954
that is brought into consumer choice

01:11:11.954 --> 01:11:16.510
that younger people have where they expect transparency

01:11:16.510 --> 01:11:18.150
because they've acclimated to it,

01:11:18.150 --> 01:11:19.730
'cause they've grown up with it.

01:11:19.730 --> 01:11:22.920
And the idea that a corporation or an industry

01:11:22.920 --> 01:11:26.290
would lack that is anathema.

01:11:26.290 --> 01:11:28.550
Yeah, and I think there is also

01:11:28.550 --> 01:11:30.630
a heightened sense of scrutiny now

01:11:30.630 --> 01:11:33.030
where we don't just demand transparency

01:11:33.030 --> 01:11:35.570
but I guess we also recognize

01:11:35.570 --> 01:11:37.700
that these labels and these terms,

01:11:37.700 --> 01:11:39.620
and these adverts should be scrutinized

01:11:39.620 --> 01:11:41.530
and we shouldn't take everything on face value.

01:11:41.530 --> 01:11:44.640
And I think that is something that's kind of emerging in new

01:11:44.640 --> 01:11:49.640
and people are still maybe coming to grips with that.

01:11:49.670 --> 01:11:50.650
Because yeah, fundamentally

01:11:50.650 --> 01:11:52.160
these industries are not transparent

01:11:52.160 --> 01:11:53.900
and the dairy farmer is a good example of that,

01:11:53.900 --> 01:11:55.960
there is a perceived transparency,

01:11:55.960 --> 01:11:57.970
there's an illusion of transparency

01:11:57.970 --> 01:12:00.410
and that illusion of transparency can be perpetuated

01:12:00.410 --> 01:12:02.610
when we drive and we see animals grazing.

01:12:02.610 --> 01:12:03.880
In the UK, for example,

01:12:03.880 --> 01:12:07.105
it's very common to see animals grazing, lambs, cows.

01:12:07.105 --> 01:12:09.580
It's not an unusual thing to see.

01:12:09.580 --> 01:12:12.460
So that creates this illusion of, firstly transparency,

01:12:12.460 --> 01:12:15.300
but secondly, I guess this notion of being humane

01:12:15.300 --> 01:12:17.090
because we think, well, how bad can it be

01:12:17.090 --> 01:12:20.630
'cause I saw a dairy cow chewing on pasture earlier.

01:12:20.630 --> 01:12:22.330
So there is this constant perpetuation

01:12:22.330 --> 01:12:24.811
of this illusion of transparency.

01:12:24.811 --> 01:12:26.970
And I think farmers themselves,

01:12:26.970 --> 01:12:29.240
I think that they can be unconscious in that as well.

01:12:29.240 --> 01:12:31.510
I don't think that that dairy farmer, for example,

01:12:31.510 --> 01:12:33.300
knows that he's not being transparent

01:12:33.300 --> 01:12:34.880
or thinks that he's not being transparent.

01:12:34.880 --> 01:12:36.640
I think he thinks he's been very open.

01:12:36.640 --> 01:12:38.660
he does these open farm Sundays

01:12:38.660 --> 01:12:39.493
where members of the public

01:12:39.493 --> 01:12:40.929
can come and see his cows grazing.

01:12:40.929 --> 01:12:44.650
And I think he thinks that he is genuinely being

01:12:44.650 --> 01:12:48.040
this very open, nothing to hide kind of farmer,

01:12:48.040 --> 01:12:52.440
but farmers themselves don't go into the slaughterhouses.

01:12:52.440 --> 01:12:54.470
Don't necessarily walk onto the kill floor.

01:12:54.470 --> 01:12:56.360
they drop the animals off, drive away.

01:12:56.360 --> 01:13:00.000
So even they themselves can often be,

01:13:00.000 --> 01:13:03.890
I guess, oblivious or maybe willfully ignorant

01:13:03.890 --> 01:13:05.980
to what happens in some of these places.

01:13:05.980 --> 01:13:08.280
Of course they're aware, but even though themselves,

01:13:08.280 --> 01:13:10.725
maybe don't have the full picture of transparency

01:13:10.725 --> 01:13:13.520
that we would like them like them to have

01:13:13.520 --> 01:13:15.130
and indeed they should have.

01:13:15.130 --> 01:13:17.460
Because for a lot of farmers, it can be uncomfortable.

01:13:17.460 --> 01:13:18.490
I've spoken to many farmers

01:13:18.490 --> 01:13:21.410
who will say that dropping animals off at slaughterhouses

01:13:21.410 --> 01:13:24.560
is really hard and they form connections with these animals,

01:13:24.560 --> 01:13:26.188
but they kind of say,

01:13:26.188 --> 01:13:27.410
well, we have to do what we have to do.

01:13:27.410 --> 01:13:28.840
They've kind of found that justification

01:13:28.840 --> 01:13:31.570
that allows that difficult moment

01:13:31.570 --> 01:13:33.720
in this process of farming to occur,

01:13:33.720 --> 01:13:35.720
this disconnection, this justification of,

01:13:35.720 --> 01:13:37.497
well, we have to do it.

01:13:37.497 --> 01:13:40.000
And when we justify something by saying, we have to,

01:13:40.000 --> 01:13:42.220
it becomes easy to justify almost anything terrible

01:13:42.220 --> 01:13:44.840
if we create the mentality, that it's a necessity.

01:13:44.840 --> 01:13:45.850
And I think farmers do the same.

01:13:45.850 --> 01:13:47.560
They convince themselves it's a necessity.

01:13:47.560 --> 01:13:49.750
And therefore that makes dropping off these cows

01:13:49.750 --> 01:13:51.610
that they maybe misguidedly,

01:13:51.610 --> 01:13:53.600
but maybe themselves individually

01:13:53.600 --> 01:13:55.890
really believe they have a connection with.

01:13:55.890 --> 01:13:57.020
And I think that's hard.

01:13:57.020 --> 01:13:59.930
So I think there's a lack of transparency everywhere

01:13:59.930 --> 01:14:02.363
and a lack of, I guess, objectivity and honesty.

01:14:03.500 --> 01:14:05.750
And of course that lack of transparency

01:14:05.750 --> 01:14:08.780
makes it more difficult for us to understand

01:14:08.780 --> 01:14:10.500
the emotional experience of the people

01:14:10.500 --> 01:14:12.590
that actually work in these slaughterhouses

01:14:12.590 --> 01:14:15.563
and have to operate in the kill room,

01:14:17.550 --> 01:14:20.240
such that even if animal rights isn't your thing,

01:14:20.240 --> 01:14:22.580
or you have a hard time like connecting with that

01:14:22.580 --> 01:14:24.390
and you care about humans,

01:14:24.390 --> 01:14:26.010
this should be an issue that you care about

01:14:26.010 --> 01:14:29.230
because there are a lot of people that work in these places

01:14:29.230 --> 01:14:33.580
who have to endure sort of psychic toll day in, day out.

01:14:33.580 --> 01:14:36.550
And there's a lot of literature out there

01:14:36.550 --> 01:14:39.830
about the long term emotional implications

01:14:39.830 --> 01:14:43.770
of what it's like to be employed in that capacity.

01:14:43.770 --> 01:14:46.020
It's a terrible job that none of us want.

01:14:46.020 --> 01:14:49.100
There's a reason why slaughterhouses

01:14:49.100 --> 01:14:51.000
are in kind of rural areas.

01:14:51.000 --> 01:14:53.060
Often areas with little job opportunities

01:14:53.060 --> 01:14:54.100
and quite simply put,

01:14:54.100 --> 01:14:55.620
no one wants to work in slaughterhouse.

01:14:55.620 --> 01:14:57.060
No one goes to school,

01:14:57.060 --> 01:14:58.800
goes to college because that's what they want.

01:14:58.800 --> 01:15:02.291
They are victims of circumstance, victims of environment.

01:15:02.291 --> 01:15:04.910
There's incredibly high turnover rates in slaughterhouses

01:15:04.910 --> 01:15:06.820
because people take these jobs

01:15:06.820 --> 01:15:08.700
out of the feeling of necessity

01:15:08.700 --> 01:15:10.650
and then they can't handle it.

01:15:10.650 --> 01:15:13.860
And then those that do stay, find ways to handle it.

01:15:13.860 --> 01:15:15.970
That might be drug and alcohol abuse.

01:15:15.970 --> 01:15:18.820
It might be violence that perpetuates outside

01:15:18.820 --> 01:15:21.841
or permeates outside of the walls of the slaughterhouse.

01:15:21.841 --> 01:15:25.840
There are studies that show that in boomtown counties

01:15:25.840 --> 01:15:29.440
near large slaughterhouses, the rates of domestic violence,

01:15:29.440 --> 01:15:32.520
of rape, of assault, of violent crimes

01:15:32.520 --> 01:15:35.610
is exponentially higher, exponentially increases.

01:15:35.610 --> 01:15:37.490
And even when they look at the variables

01:15:37.490 --> 01:15:41.370
and they look at other kind of maybe working class jobs,

01:15:41.370 --> 01:15:43.730
such as, steel production car production,

01:15:43.730 --> 01:15:46.390
those rates of violent crime increases aren't seen,

01:15:46.390 --> 01:15:48.532
it's something very unique to slaughterhouse work.

01:15:48.532 --> 01:15:51.196
And it's very easy to understand why that is.

01:15:51.196 --> 01:15:53.319
most of us would never want to kill one animal.

01:15:53.319 --> 01:15:55.980
Now I'm imagining slaughterhouse where eight hours a day,

01:15:55.980 --> 01:15:57.990
five, six days a week, or killing hundreds,

01:15:57.990 --> 01:15:59.230
thousands maybe of animals.

01:15:59.230 --> 01:16:01.590
That your job is to endlessly pull a knife

01:16:01.590 --> 01:16:03.420
across someone else's throat.

01:16:03.420 --> 01:16:05.220
How do you reconcile with that?

01:16:05.220 --> 01:16:06.990
How do you leave at the end of the day

01:16:06.990 --> 01:16:08.830
and not bring that home of you?

01:16:08.830 --> 01:16:10.090
We expect slaughterhouses workers

01:16:10.090 --> 01:16:12.393
to hang up the blood soaked aprons

01:16:12.393 --> 01:16:14.250
and the then just be these

01:16:14.250 --> 01:16:16.730
completely mentally competent people at the end of the day

01:16:16.730 --> 01:16:19.630
and that's an unfair thing for us to place on these people.

01:16:19.630 --> 01:16:20.463
And then on top of that,

01:16:20.463 --> 01:16:23.150
not only are they having the pressure of the job,

01:16:23.150 --> 01:16:25.730
but they have the pressure of being low incomes

01:16:25.730 --> 01:16:28.970
being in deprived areas where they feel completely trapped

01:16:28.970 --> 01:16:30.520
because there isn't the availability

01:16:30.520 --> 01:16:32.640
of working in a different place.

01:16:32.640 --> 01:16:33.490
That's the job,

01:16:33.490 --> 01:16:34.815
that's the job.
And if

01:16:34.815 --> 01:16:35.648
you don't have that job,

01:16:35.648 --> 01:16:36.690
then maybe you can't afford to live.

01:16:36.690 --> 01:16:37.570
Maybe you'll be homeless.

01:16:37.570 --> 01:16:38.720
Maybe you can't feed your family,

01:16:38.720 --> 01:16:40.270
and you've got kids to look after.

01:16:40.270 --> 01:16:42.210
There are all of these extra pressures and burdens

01:16:42.210 --> 01:16:43.610
that can lead people to doing things

01:16:43.610 --> 01:16:45.377
that they wouldn't want to do,

01:16:45.377 --> 01:16:47.110
but they feel they have no choice to,

01:16:47.110 --> 01:16:49.520
and we're fulfilling them

01:16:49.520 --> 01:16:51.340
and creating that scenario for our purchases.

01:16:51.340 --> 01:16:55.300
And it's a terrible industry in every a re conceivable way,

01:16:55.300 --> 01:16:57.690
even for the humans in that scenario.

01:16:57.690 --> 01:16:59.550
That doesn't detract from the fact that what they're doing

01:16:59.550 --> 01:17:01.640
is obviously morally wrong,

01:17:01.640 --> 01:17:05.170
but they are merely fulfilling the expectations

01:17:05.170 --> 01:17:06.420
of the people who are paying for it.

01:17:06.420 --> 01:17:08.830
The blood is on their hands literally,

01:17:08.830 --> 01:17:11.720
but we're the ones who are really have the blooded hands,

01:17:11.720 --> 01:17:12.553
'cause they're the ones paying

01:17:12.553 --> 01:17:14.530
for these things to exist in the first place.

01:17:14.530 --> 01:17:16.630
And I've talked about this before,

01:17:16.630 --> 01:17:20.410
but a version of that scenario scales up

01:17:20.410 --> 01:17:23.850
as you go up the chain of command at these farms

01:17:23.850 --> 01:17:25.850
because most of them are

01:17:28.390 --> 01:17:30.070
controlled through debt structures

01:17:30.070 --> 01:17:32.110
by these large food companies

01:17:32.110 --> 01:17:34.600
like Tyson and Cargill, et cetera,

01:17:34.600 --> 01:17:37.820
who basically underwrite the construction

01:17:37.820 --> 01:17:42.420
of these massive farms and create a system

01:17:42.420 --> 01:17:47.420
in which it's very difficult to meet your debt demands,

01:17:49.080 --> 01:17:52.110
creating a certain type of indentured servitude

01:17:52.110 --> 01:17:55.980
with these farmers that they can never really scale out of.

01:17:55.980 --> 01:18:00.210
They have to continue to grow or perish essentially.

01:18:00.210 --> 01:18:03.760
And that's not what, a lot of these people

01:18:03.760 --> 01:18:07.010
who farming has been in their family for generations

01:18:07.010 --> 01:18:08.460
kind of signed up for.

01:18:08.460 --> 01:18:09.300
Certainly not.

01:18:09.300 --> 01:18:12.700
I mean, they would is a huge boom in these corporations

01:18:12.700 --> 01:18:16.226
in 1980s with Smithfields, Tyson, JBS of course,

01:18:16.226 --> 01:18:18.860
and they have a complete monopoly of the market.

01:18:18.860 --> 01:18:21.900
In the UK, we have a similar thing with like Moy Park,

01:18:21.900 --> 01:18:24.030
which is a big chicken producer, 2 Sisters,

01:18:24.030 --> 01:18:26.240
So it's a similar thing across many countries,

01:18:26.240 --> 01:18:27.180
not just in the US,

01:18:27.180 --> 01:18:30.170
but the US is a uniquely terrible problem with it,

01:18:30.170 --> 01:18:31.070
which is just,

01:18:31.070 --> 01:18:32.260
these huge companies,

01:18:32.260 --> 01:18:34.320
they sign contracts of all these producers

01:18:34.320 --> 01:18:36.463
and it's mainly poultry producers and pig producers.

01:18:36.463 --> 01:18:38.900
And those farmers

01:18:38.900 --> 01:18:41.900
who maybe don't want to sign these contracts

01:18:41.900 --> 01:18:43.350
are faced with a problem,

01:18:43.350 --> 01:18:45.890
which is that these companies

01:18:45.890 --> 01:18:48.460
with the farmers who are contracted to them

01:18:48.460 --> 01:18:50.658
are constantly driving down the cost of production.

01:18:50.658 --> 01:18:53.200
And driving down the cost of production,

01:18:53.200 --> 01:18:55.350
they're producing cheaper and cheaper food,

01:18:55.350 --> 01:18:58.080
which means that these kind of more independent farmers,

01:18:58.080 --> 01:19:00.550
they can't financially operate in the strains of the market

01:19:00.550 --> 01:19:02.750
because it's costing them so much more to produce

01:19:02.750 --> 01:19:04.400
the food they produce.

01:19:04.400 --> 01:19:07.210
And no one's gonna buy it.

01:19:07.210 --> 01:19:08.360
The suppliers and distributors,

01:19:08.360 --> 01:19:09.650
aren't gonna pay the same amount of money

01:19:09.650 --> 01:19:10.840
for it to cover the costs.

01:19:10.840 --> 01:19:13.380
And so a lot of these farmers end up their hands tied

01:19:13.380 --> 01:19:16.310
because they have to sell into these corporations.

01:19:16.310 --> 01:19:18.560
And as you say, they then have all these debts

01:19:18.560 --> 01:19:20.060
and the contracts might stipulate

01:19:20.060 --> 01:19:22.720
that they have to update their machinery every so often,

01:19:22.720 --> 01:19:25.226
have to update the methods of farming.

01:19:25.226 --> 01:19:26.820
And so those are more debts, more loans,

01:19:26.820 --> 01:19:28.430
more financial problems.

01:19:28.430 --> 01:19:29.960
And so they become trapped in the system

01:19:29.960 --> 01:19:32.011
where they can't really get out

01:19:32.011 --> 01:19:34.740
by incurring huge financial problems,

01:19:34.740 --> 01:19:36.060
which again could make them homeless,

01:19:36.060 --> 01:19:38.040
could mean they have to leave their communities,

01:19:38.040 --> 01:19:39.117
not be able to support their families.

01:19:39.117 --> 01:19:42.210
And so there is this external pressure

01:19:42.210 --> 01:19:44.380
which we often don't realize,

01:19:44.380 --> 01:19:46.830
which is constantly fueling the continuation

01:19:46.830 --> 01:19:47.730
of these industries.

01:19:47.730 --> 01:19:50.930
And many farmers may wake up one day

01:19:50.930 --> 01:19:52.920
and not want to continue farming

01:19:52.920 --> 01:19:53.940
for whatever reason that might be,

01:19:53.940 --> 01:19:55.649
maybe an ethical realization,

01:19:55.649 --> 01:19:59.480
but then have no choice based in the financial strains

01:19:59.480 --> 01:20:00.313
that have been incurred

01:20:00.313 --> 01:20:02.490
due to these corporations and companies

01:20:02.490 --> 01:20:04.890
and the monopolization they have on the markets.

01:20:06.520 --> 01:20:09.300
The other big argument that I want to talk about

01:20:09.300 --> 01:20:12.158
is this idea that,

01:20:12.158 --> 01:20:16.710
if you wanna kill the maximum number of animals,

01:20:16.710 --> 01:20:19.070
the best way to do that is to go vegan.

01:20:19.070 --> 01:20:22.167
So this is an argument that was promulgated

01:20:22.167 --> 01:20:24.200
by the great Ted Nugent

01:20:24.200 --> 01:20:26.098
on the Joe Rogan experience recently,

01:20:26.098 --> 01:20:30.950
and I've heard this one fielded before many times,

01:20:30.950 --> 01:20:32.800
and we can kinda get into that a little bit,

01:20:32.800 --> 01:20:36.700
but explain the basis for this argument

01:20:36.700 --> 01:20:39.781
and then let's try to deconstruct it.

01:20:39.781 --> 01:20:42.540
The basis for the argument

01:20:42.540 --> 01:20:44.490
stems from something which is absolutely true,

01:20:44.490 --> 01:20:45.920
which is that plant-based farming

01:20:45.920 --> 01:20:48.090
still results in animal death,

01:20:48.090 --> 01:20:51.480
crop production, harvesting results in animals being killed.

01:20:51.480 --> 01:20:53.090
We can't get around that for the time being.

01:20:53.090 --> 01:20:54.820
So it's always important

01:20:54.820 --> 01:20:57.710
that when we advocate for veganism myself,

01:20:57.710 --> 01:21:00.506
I have to make the important consideration,

01:21:00.506 --> 01:21:02.040
which is that veganism isn't perfect.

01:21:02.040 --> 01:21:04.300
It's not gonna eliminate all exploitation.

01:21:04.300 --> 01:21:06.000
It's gonna not gonna eliminate all death.

01:21:06.000 --> 01:21:08.160
It's really a reduction of these things,

01:21:08.160 --> 01:21:11.020
a huge reduction, but a reduction, nonetheless.

01:21:11.020 --> 01:21:12.950
So it comes from an important point to recognize,

01:21:12.950 --> 01:21:14.910
which is that veganism isn't perfect.

01:21:14.910 --> 01:21:19.480
And we're not devoid of harm by choosing plant-based foods.

01:21:19.480 --> 01:21:21.103
But the problem is it is kind of extrapolated

01:21:21.103 --> 01:21:24.840
it's up into this really overexaggerated thing

01:21:24.840 --> 01:21:26.871
where vegans are killing all these mice

01:21:26.871 --> 01:21:29.127
and these rodents and all these birds

01:21:29.127 --> 01:21:31.630
and all these of other animals at the same time.

01:21:31.630 --> 01:21:33.995
And well, first that's not strictly true.

01:21:33.995 --> 01:21:37.530
when we think about rodents and we think about birds,

01:21:37.530 --> 01:21:39.960
it's very hard to, to catch a mouse,

01:21:39.960 --> 01:21:42.120
let alone run them over with a combine harvester.

01:21:42.120 --> 01:21:45.660
They've put like radio trackers on these animals before,

01:21:45.660 --> 01:21:47.550
harvested fields and then found

01:21:47.550 --> 01:21:50.270
that the population just haven't really been decimated.

01:21:50.270 --> 01:21:52.400
They just kind of go leave the fields.

01:21:52.400 --> 01:21:54.180
You've got big combine harvester making all this noise,

01:21:54.180 --> 01:21:55.390
noise vibrations in the ground,

01:21:55.390 --> 01:21:56.920
the animals don't stick around.

01:21:56.920 --> 01:21:58.410
But insects of course are killed

01:21:58.410 --> 01:22:01.330
and will be killed on massive in crop production.

01:22:01.330 --> 01:22:03.910
And the idea is, I suppose,

01:22:03.910 --> 01:22:06.250
to try and create a sense of hypocrisy

01:22:06.250 --> 01:22:07.840
that vegans are causing death,

01:22:07.840 --> 01:22:10.020
so therefore there's no point being vegan,

01:22:10.020 --> 01:22:12.957
but of course it's about reducing the harm that we cause.

01:22:12.957 --> 01:22:16.030
And when we look at crop production for animals,

01:22:16.030 --> 01:22:18.500
we look at the mass production of feed in south America

01:22:18.500 --> 01:22:20.210
for animals,

01:22:20.210 --> 01:22:24.150
Even if we just take the system that we have now,

01:22:24.150 --> 01:22:25.390
significantly more animals are gonna be killed

01:22:25.390 --> 01:22:26.830
in the production of animal foods,

01:22:26.830 --> 01:22:29.170
because not only do you have 80 billion land animals

01:22:29.170 --> 01:22:31.600
and 0.8, 2.3 trillion marine animals,

01:22:31.600 --> 01:22:32.820
but then you also have all the animals

01:22:32.820 --> 01:22:34.640
that are killed in crop production as well

01:22:34.640 --> 01:22:36.010
to feed the animals who we farm,

01:22:36.010 --> 01:22:37.450
including the farmed fish

01:22:37.450 --> 01:22:39.740
who are fed things like soy and stuff as well.

01:22:39.740 --> 01:22:42.380
So it's a problem that exists for plant-based farming,

01:22:42.380 --> 01:22:43.770
but the problem that exists there,

01:22:43.770 --> 01:22:46.330
exists in all of the animal agriculture space

01:22:46.330 --> 01:22:47.454
and on top of that,

01:22:47.454 --> 01:22:48.680
you have all the violence that's committed to the animals

01:22:48.680 --> 01:22:50.267
who we eat directly as well.

01:22:50.267 --> 01:22:53.610
So it's kind of a non-starter in that sense.

01:22:53.610 --> 01:22:55.950
A couple things, first of all, the mice thing,

