01:31:38.220 --> 01:31:40.330
But actually when you look at something,

01:31:40.330 --> 01:31:42.410
I suppose, a little bit more logical,

01:31:42.410 --> 01:31:43.243
it makes more sense

01:31:43.243 --> 01:31:45.950
that it was the consumption of carbohydrate dense foods,

01:31:45.950 --> 01:31:48.710
tubers, potatoes, wheat, rice

01:31:48.710 --> 01:31:50.650
that allowed our brains to grow.

01:31:50.650 --> 01:31:54.191
Because our brain's primary fuel is glucose.

01:31:54.191 --> 01:31:57.117
And so when we think about carbohydrate dense foods,

01:31:57.117 --> 01:32:01.250
these are the foods that provide us with the most glucose.

01:32:01.250 --> 01:32:02.690
So when we started cooking foods

01:32:02.690 --> 01:32:05.400
and we started to being able to digest things like potatoes

01:32:05.400 --> 01:32:07.800
and wheat and rice and such,

01:32:07.800 --> 01:32:10.350
it allowed our body to consume more glucose.

01:32:10.350 --> 01:32:12.903
And as a consequence of that, would presumably allow,

01:32:12.903 --> 01:32:14.920
I mean, our brains could grow bigger.

01:32:14.920 --> 01:32:17.253
And even when they look at sort of DNA

01:32:17.253 --> 01:32:20.350
and they look at in a fossilized remains of people,

01:32:20.350 --> 01:32:23.110
they identify enzymes in our mouths

01:32:23.110 --> 01:32:25.900
that were there to help us break down starches

01:32:25.900 --> 01:32:27.567
and to break down carbohydrates and digest them.

01:32:27.567 --> 01:32:29.777
And so all the evidence points

01:32:29.777 --> 01:32:30.610
to the fact that carbohydrates

01:32:30.610 --> 01:32:32.570
were not only an essential part of our diet,

01:32:32.570 --> 01:32:35.090
but were the reason that we were our brains grew

01:32:35.090 --> 01:32:36.180
because we were providing our brains

01:32:36.180 --> 01:32:40.193
to the fuel source that it needs to work most optimum.

01:32:41.070 --> 01:32:46.070
But even if the meat brain argument were to be true,

01:32:46.740 --> 01:32:50.020
we live in a society now

01:32:50.020 --> 01:32:54.410
where we don't have to be obligate omnivores,

01:32:54.410 --> 01:32:56.260
we can choose to be herbivores

01:32:56.260 --> 01:32:58.320
and the science is pretty clear

01:32:58.320 --> 01:33:01.760
that you can thrive on a vegan diet at any age,

01:33:01.760 --> 01:33:04.830
whether you're pregnant or a small child,

01:33:04.830 --> 01:33:07.000
we're both living examples of that.

01:33:07.000 --> 01:33:10.423
So again, it goes back to choice.

01:33:10.423 --> 01:33:12.020
Absolutely.
Like you can spend

01:33:12.020 --> 01:33:13.820
all your time making those arguments

01:33:13.820 --> 01:33:15.612
about whether we're herbivores or omnivores

01:33:15.612 --> 01:33:17.850
and looking at our teeth and all of that,

01:33:17.850 --> 01:33:20.490
but to me, like none of that really matters.

01:33:20.490 --> 01:33:22.330
It's like here we are living now

01:33:22.330 --> 01:33:26.810
and you look at the many harmful

01:33:26.810 --> 01:33:30.680
implications of the way that we consume meat

01:33:30.680 --> 01:33:31.920
and treat our animals.

01:33:31.920 --> 01:33:35.510
And it doesn't make sense.
No.

01:33:35.510 --> 01:33:37.070
And again, yeah, you are obviously right.

01:33:37.070 --> 01:33:40.664
We can get bogged down with a forward facing eyes.

01:33:40.664 --> 01:33:43.520
I hear that a lot, or-
Well, I haven't heard

01:33:43.520 --> 01:33:44.540
that one, what's that one?

01:33:44.540 --> 01:33:47.057
Forward facing eyes are the signs of a predator.

01:33:47.057 --> 01:33:49.120
Pray have eyes that can see all around

01:33:49.120 --> 01:33:52.212
'cause it allows them to see predators more easily.

01:33:52.212 --> 01:33:54.720
Because we have forward facing eyes, the canine teeth,

01:33:54.720 --> 01:33:57.510
even though our canine are really quite rubbish.

01:33:57.510 --> 01:33:59.963
And the lion thing, it always goes back to the lion.

01:33:59.963 --> 01:34:01.453
What is that?
I have no idea.

01:34:01.453 --> 01:34:03.150
It's so baffling, I don't think it's like-

01:34:03.150 --> 01:34:04.063
Like lions do this.

01:34:04.063 --> 01:34:05.610
Like all these guys walking around

01:34:05.610 --> 01:34:07.700
thinking that they're lions.
It's bizarre, isn't it?

01:34:07.700 --> 01:34:09.410
Yeah.
On the one hand

01:34:09.410 --> 01:34:10.243
I'll have conversations

01:34:10.243 --> 01:34:11.100
and the same person

01:34:11.100 --> 01:34:13.340
will tell me that humans are different to animals

01:34:13.340 --> 01:34:14.400
'cause we're more intelligent.

01:34:14.400 --> 01:34:16.020
And then later in the conversation they'll go,

01:34:16.020 --> 01:34:18.360
well humans are animals and so are lions.

01:34:18.360 --> 01:34:20.240
And so it's this irony.
Which one is it?

01:34:20.240 --> 01:34:21.707
Which one is it? Exactly.

01:34:21.707 --> 01:34:23.850
But we do like to compare ourselves to other animals

01:34:23.850 --> 01:34:25.760
because again, I think we're trying to constantly appeal

01:34:25.760 --> 01:34:28.410
to the idea that it's natural and normal

01:34:28.410 --> 01:34:30.000
and that it's acceptable for us to do it

01:34:30.000 --> 01:34:31.775
because there's a historical precedence,

01:34:31.775 --> 01:34:33.410
a historical precedent, sorry,

01:34:33.410 --> 01:34:35.780
and there's like this kind of naturalistic precedent

01:34:35.780 --> 01:34:38.310
that exists with whatever animals do.

01:34:38.310 --> 01:34:40.180
But again, we can get bogged down on that.

01:34:40.180 --> 01:34:44.200
But the point remains now where we are,

01:34:44.200 --> 01:34:45.560
with the information that we have,

01:34:45.560 --> 01:34:47.260
with the food that we have available to us,

01:34:47.260 --> 01:34:51.210
with the knowledge that we have, we don't have to.

01:34:51.210 --> 01:34:52.663
The fact that we have forward face eyes

01:34:52.663 --> 01:34:54.280
doesn't mean that we're now justified

01:34:54.280 --> 01:34:55.700
to cause so much suffering to animals

01:34:55.700 --> 01:34:58.095
when we could just eat the plants instead.

01:34:58.095 --> 01:34:59.210
That's a very good point.

01:34:59.210 --> 01:35:01.690
In all of these interactions and conversations

01:35:01.690 --> 01:35:04.710
that you've had on college campuses with young people,

01:35:04.710 --> 01:35:08.190
have you ever met an argument that you couldn't manage?

01:35:08.190 --> 01:35:11.310
Like what do you think is the strongest argument

01:35:11.310 --> 01:35:14.343
that you've had to field against veganism?

01:35:14.343 --> 01:35:18.108
I think it's, people's personal health situations.

01:35:18.108 --> 01:35:21.220
sometimes someone might say something to me about

01:35:21.220 --> 01:35:22.850
the health of a family member

01:35:22.850 --> 01:35:25.170
and I'm not necessarily sure how to respond to that,

01:35:25.170 --> 01:35:27.370
I don't know the nuances and intricacies

01:35:27.370 --> 01:35:29.130
of that individual scenario.

01:35:29.130 --> 01:35:30.453
When it comes to like the moral issues,

01:35:30.453 --> 01:35:32.360
when it comes to the environmental issues,

01:35:32.360 --> 01:35:35.400
I've never heard anything that's made go, oh

01:35:35.400 --> 01:35:38.280
that's something I'm not sure how to respond to,

01:35:38.280 --> 01:35:40.700
or, oh that's a point I'm not considered.

01:35:40.700 --> 01:35:41.930
But I think that people can

01:35:41.930 --> 01:35:44.270
come up with these health arguments that-

01:35:44.270 --> 01:35:45.868
They need this or I tried it

01:35:45.868 --> 01:35:47.506
and this is what happened to me.

01:35:47.506 --> 01:35:49.300
And it could be hard to argue against that,

01:35:49.300 --> 01:35:51.040
because without knowing the personal situations,

01:35:51.040 --> 01:35:52.340
what people were eating,

01:35:52.340 --> 01:35:53.920
if they were supplementing how they were gonna about it,

01:35:53.920 --> 01:35:55.640
it's hard to necessarily tell

01:35:55.640 --> 01:35:57.570
that their experience was wrong in that scenario.

01:35:57.570 --> 01:35:58.410
It's just more of a case

01:35:58.410 --> 01:35:59.350
of trying to explain to people

01:35:59.350 --> 01:36:01.270
that perhaps there was a deficiency

01:36:01.270 --> 01:36:02.460
or something you could have done differently

01:36:02.460 --> 01:36:04.055
to try and negate those problems.

01:36:04.055 --> 01:36:06.430
But I think more broadly than that,

01:36:06.430 --> 01:36:08.770
something that I really empathize with

01:36:08.770 --> 01:36:12.150
is when people talk about family scenarios,

01:36:12.150 --> 01:36:13.920
social situations.

01:36:13.920 --> 01:36:15.410
That's and like the huge limiter.

01:36:15.410 --> 01:36:16.243
It is.

01:36:16.243 --> 01:36:19.100
And I think that's one of the biggest drivers for people.

01:36:19.100 --> 01:36:21.170
It is just those social environments,

01:36:21.170 --> 01:36:22.003
whether it's with family,

01:36:22.003 --> 01:36:23.410
whether it's with friends.
100%

01:36:23.410 --> 01:36:24.890
And that's the thing-
They could be

01:36:24.890 --> 01:36:28.120
completely convinced that this is the right thing to do

01:36:28.120 --> 01:36:31.410
and they could have a deep rooted desire to do it,

01:36:31.410 --> 01:36:34.700
but their environment and their surroundings prevent it

01:36:34.700 --> 01:36:37.020
because the social ostracization,

01:36:37.020 --> 01:36:41.670
or just the drain of having to contend with that

01:36:41.670 --> 01:36:44.130
is too burdensome for a lot of people.

01:36:44.130 --> 01:36:46.030
And use that word ostracization,

01:36:46.030 --> 01:36:48.540
which is a really important word to use

01:36:48.540 --> 01:36:51.330
because humans we're social animals,

01:36:51.330 --> 01:36:55.020
we thrive being in communities, being with others

01:36:55.020 --> 01:36:55.990
and throughout history,

01:36:55.990 --> 01:37:00.460
our survivalist depended on participation of others.

01:37:00.460 --> 01:37:02.380
Having these communities where we had these roles

01:37:02.380 --> 01:37:05.130
where we could integrate together

01:37:05.130 --> 01:37:07.590
and being outside of that,

01:37:07.590 --> 01:37:09.800
not having that sense of community,

01:37:09.800 --> 01:37:13.190
being ostracized was catastrophic.

01:37:13.190 --> 01:37:15.050
So now when we think of veganism,

01:37:15.050 --> 01:37:17.960
it can be perceived as kind of a form of self ostracization.

01:37:17.960 --> 01:37:20.601
We're removing ourself from the safe of the community,

01:37:20.601 --> 01:37:23.860
the safety of the tribe, the safety of our social groups.

01:37:23.860 --> 01:37:24.930
And so it can be very daunting

01:37:24.930 --> 01:37:26.000
because it seems like we're stepping

01:37:26.000 --> 01:37:27.600
outside of the comfort zone

01:37:27.600 --> 01:37:30.900
and there's a historical kind of primal fear of doing that

01:37:30.900 --> 01:37:34.090
because of the reliance that we had on others for survival.

01:37:34.090 --> 01:37:36.310
It's different now, of course, but that primal fear,

01:37:36.310 --> 01:37:37.240
I still think remains.

01:37:37.240 --> 01:37:40.000
And so one of the most important things about being vegan

01:37:40.000 --> 01:37:42.070
is that it normalizes being vegan

01:37:42.070 --> 01:37:44.260
and it makes it more accessible for others.

01:37:44.260 --> 01:37:45.940
And it's kind of like a snowball effect

01:37:45.940 --> 01:37:47.670
where the more people who go vegan,

01:37:47.670 --> 01:37:48.700
more people will go vegan

01:37:48.700 --> 01:37:50.440
simply because more people are going vegan

01:37:50.440 --> 01:37:53.100
and it becomes more normal and more accepted.

01:37:53.100 --> 01:37:54.910
And that's how we can hopefully help people

01:37:54.910 --> 01:37:56.020
in those environments.

01:37:56.020 --> 01:37:57.670
But, I mean, I totally hear people

01:37:57.670 --> 01:37:59.970
when they say that because it is hard

01:37:59.970 --> 01:38:01.180
to look a family member

01:38:01.180 --> 01:38:04.410
or go into a meal or a social situation

01:38:04.410 --> 01:38:06.650
where everyone's doing one thing and you used to do that

01:38:06.650 --> 01:38:08.817
and they're expecting you to continue doing it

01:38:08.817 --> 01:38:10.640
and then tell them you're not.

01:38:10.640 --> 01:38:12.520
And then explain their reasons why you're not.

01:38:12.520 --> 01:38:14.270
And also be aware that they're gonna give you

01:38:14.270 --> 01:38:16.690
loads of excuses to why you are wrong.

01:38:16.690 --> 01:38:18.960
And you are gonna have to feel those excuses

01:38:18.960 --> 01:38:20.280
while still trying to enjoy yourself

01:38:20.280 --> 01:38:22.410
and not treading anyone's toes and upset anyone

01:38:22.410 --> 01:38:23.760
or cause an argument.

01:38:23.760 --> 01:38:25.130
That's exceptionally daunting.

01:38:25.130 --> 01:38:29.380
So, whilst it doesn't morally justify what we do,

01:38:29.380 --> 01:38:31.750
I totally empathize with people in those environments.

01:38:31.750 --> 01:38:35.410
And I think it's about creating a sense of normality

01:38:35.410 --> 01:38:37.790
around being vegan so that it becomes more accepted,

01:38:37.790 --> 01:38:39.740
becomes more mainstream,

01:38:39.740 --> 01:38:42.960
becomes something that people don't feel is intimidated by.

01:38:42.960 --> 01:38:45.960
With family, I think especially my family, aren't vegan,

01:38:45.960 --> 01:38:49.287
they're perfectly supportive of what I do,

01:38:49.287 --> 01:38:52.500
but they're not necessarily open minded

01:38:52.500 --> 01:38:55.520
to hearing why I do what I do.

01:38:55.520 --> 01:38:56.980
And when it comes to family,

01:38:56.980 --> 01:38:58.930
I think what's particularly challenging

01:38:58.930 --> 01:39:02.910
is our parents hopefully try and raise us

01:39:02.910 --> 01:39:06.120
to be kind of good people with good morals, good values.

01:39:06.120 --> 01:39:08.310
They want us to integrate into an everyday society

01:39:08.310 --> 01:39:09.510
and be good citizens.

01:39:09.510 --> 01:39:11.090
Hopefully that's the idea.

01:39:11.090 --> 01:39:12.810
And so when we turn around to our parents

01:39:12.810 --> 01:39:14.480
and we say, look, I've gone vegan.

01:39:14.480 --> 01:39:16.350
And in my situation, it was

01:39:16.350 --> 01:39:17.183
"Look, I've gone vegan

01:39:17.183 --> 01:39:19.360
because I think what we do to animals is wrong."

01:39:19.360 --> 01:39:21.400
I'm kind of criticizing their parenting.

01:39:21.400 --> 01:39:23.510
I'm saying that they raised me with bad morals,

01:39:23.510 --> 01:39:25.430
that they raised me with bad values.

01:39:25.430 --> 01:39:26.907
And for a parent to hear that,

01:39:26.907 --> 01:39:27.740
and in the case of my parents,

01:39:27.740 --> 01:39:30.540
I know they try to raise me to be hopefully a good person,

01:39:30.540 --> 01:39:32.797
to hear that, even though I'm not explicitly stating that,

01:39:32.797 --> 01:39:34.950
but I'm implicitly stating it,

01:39:34.950 --> 01:39:36.117
I think it's hard for a parent to hear.

01:39:36.117 --> 01:39:40.160
And so there are these dynamics, social, familial

01:39:40.160 --> 01:39:43.650
that are very challenging for us to acknowledge and address

01:39:43.650 --> 01:39:45.090
and can be very intimidating.

01:39:45.090 --> 01:39:46.450
There's these social structures,

01:39:46.450 --> 01:39:49.250
these hierarchies that exist within families

01:39:49.250 --> 01:39:51.840
and for a child to go to their parent and say,

01:39:51.840 --> 01:39:54.150
something like that, I think can be daunting

01:39:54.150 --> 01:39:56.037
and challenging for a parent to hear.

01:39:56.037 --> 01:39:58.370
And I understand that can then make it difficult

01:39:58.370 --> 01:40:00.061
for people to want to make that change.

01:40:00.061 --> 01:40:02.300
I'm very compassionate about that.

01:40:02.300 --> 01:40:04.310
And we should just acknowledge the fact

01:40:04.310 --> 01:40:06.880
that we're having this conversation in Los Angeles

01:40:06.880 --> 01:40:09.030
and you hail from London,

01:40:09.030 --> 01:40:13.273
like these are incredibly vegan, friendly environments.

01:40:16.730 --> 01:40:18.707
And most people don't live in places like that, right?

01:40:18.707 --> 01:40:23.707
And I think that speaks to another criticism or argument

01:40:23.960 --> 01:40:28.530
against veganism, which is this idea that it is elitist.

01:40:28.530 --> 01:40:30.889
And I think there is a strain of this movement

01:40:30.889 --> 01:40:35.753
that has been co-opted by a kind of elitist notion

01:40:37.740 --> 01:40:41.680
because in this burgeoning wellness industry

01:40:41.680 --> 01:40:45.520
where high priced items are associated with

01:40:45.520 --> 01:40:47.620
kind of living your best life,

01:40:47.620 --> 01:40:49.060
going to the fancy markets

01:40:49.060 --> 01:40:51.830
and getting the latest superfood and all of that,

01:40:51.830 --> 01:40:54.905
veganism gets kind of wrapped up into that narrative

01:40:54.905 --> 01:40:59.905
and creates an aspirational lifestyle perhaps,

01:41:00.400 --> 01:41:03.660
but an inaccesible one for a lot of people

01:41:03.660 --> 01:41:05.363
that turn them off to,

01:41:09.775 --> 01:41:10.700
the stronger arguments

01:41:10.700 --> 01:41:14.350
that all route back to this idea of moral philosophy.

01:41:14.350 --> 01:41:16.940
I think part of the problem is use the word wellness.

01:41:16.940 --> 01:41:19.390
I think that planet diets and veganism

01:41:20.570 --> 01:41:23.980
have become somewhat synonymous

01:41:23.980 --> 01:41:26.190
with this idea of individual wellness.

01:41:26.190 --> 01:41:28.600
And that's why I always try and emphasize

01:41:28.600 --> 01:41:30.070
that veganism is a moral issue

01:41:30.070 --> 01:41:31.850
where obviously it has-

01:41:31.850 --> 01:41:33.912
Well it's also about collect wellness.

01:41:33.912 --> 01:41:35.400
Collective wellness is true.

01:41:35.400 --> 01:41:36.910
But I suppose when we're talking about

01:41:36.910 --> 01:41:41.100
the pricier things that we can buy,

01:41:41.100 --> 01:41:43.270
there is this idea that it's kind of fulfilling

01:41:43.270 --> 01:41:45.710
that stereotype of maybe an LA person

01:41:45.710 --> 01:41:48.490
who's into those kind of those pursuits.

01:41:48.490 --> 01:41:51.060
And so I think I try and acknowledge that veganism

01:41:51.060 --> 01:41:53.026
is a moral philosophy

01:41:53.026 --> 01:41:57.860
and there are people who eat vegan on a budget.

01:41:57.860 --> 01:42:00.810
And of course you can be an expensive vegan.

01:42:00.810 --> 01:42:02.600
You can go to Whole Foods or Erewhon

01:42:02.600 --> 01:42:04.347
or whatever it may be and spend a lot of money

01:42:04.347 --> 01:42:06.770
buying meat substitutes and stuff,

01:42:06.770 --> 01:42:08.360
but that's not what veganism is.

01:42:08.360 --> 01:42:10.600
Veganism is about trying to reduce the harm we cause.

01:42:10.600 --> 01:42:11.690
And there was a study

01:42:11.690 --> 01:42:13.260
that just came out from University of Oxford

01:42:13.260 --> 01:42:15.560
by a professor called Marco Springmann

01:42:15.560 --> 01:42:17.750
and he was looking at food prices

01:42:17.750 --> 01:42:20.090
and he said in the UK, at least,

01:42:20.090 --> 01:42:21.890
shopping and buying whole foods.

01:42:21.890 --> 01:42:23.920
So the healthier plant-based foods

01:42:23.920 --> 01:42:25.823
reduces your food bill by about 1/3.

01:42:27.020 --> 01:42:29.060
So you can save money doing it that way.

01:42:29.060 --> 01:42:30.860
But there is this connotation of bean vegan

01:42:30.860 --> 01:42:32.630
means eating beyond burgers and impossible burgers

01:42:32.630 --> 01:42:36.370
and and kind of like superfood powders and stuff.

01:42:36.370 --> 01:42:37.940
And that's not what it is.

01:42:37.940 --> 01:42:39.050
And it's great if you wanna do that.

01:42:39.050 --> 01:42:41.370
I mean that, all power to you, if that's what you wanna do,

01:42:41.370 --> 01:42:43.063
but it doesn't have to be like that.

01:42:43.063 --> 01:42:44.920
And I think just trying to help people understand

01:42:44.920 --> 01:42:47.620
that veganism doesn't mean having to shop in these places,

01:42:47.620 --> 01:42:50.020
but can mean shopping and buying whole foods,

01:42:50.020 --> 01:42:52.160
legumes, whole grains, potatoes,

01:42:52.160 --> 01:42:54.710
all these foods that should make up the majority

01:42:54.710 --> 01:42:55.873
of our diet anyway.

01:42:57.079 --> 01:42:59.190
It is a really important way of making sure

01:42:59.190 --> 01:43:02.103
that we can break through that elitist stereotype.

01:43:02.950 --> 01:43:05.500
Now it is important to recognize

01:43:05.500 --> 01:43:07.730
that there are people who live across the world,

01:43:07.730 --> 01:43:09.840
but even in the US that don't have the means

01:43:09.840 --> 01:43:12.260
to be able to live as comfortably as you and I do.

01:43:12.260 --> 01:43:14.300
Of course now that's fairly obvious

01:43:14.300 --> 01:43:18.320
and the way this socio-economic structure is set up,

01:43:18.320 --> 01:43:20.850
there are situations of food disparity

01:43:20.850 --> 01:43:23.550
where people don't have the accessibility

01:43:23.550 --> 01:43:25.670
that we're used to where we live.

01:43:25.670 --> 01:43:27.830
And we have to look at how we can challenge that

01:43:27.830 --> 01:43:29.440
and change that.

01:43:29.440 --> 01:43:32.060
Now there is something fundamentally wrong

01:43:32.060 --> 01:43:34.770
when buying a beef burger from McDonald's

01:43:34.770 --> 01:43:36.650
is cheaper than buying a pun a blueberries

01:43:36.650 --> 01:43:39.180
from a supermarket, there's obviously something wrong

01:43:39.180 --> 01:43:40.990
because the production of animal products

01:43:40.990 --> 01:43:42.460
costs a lot more than the production

01:43:42.460 --> 01:43:44.520
of fruits and vegetables and whole grains.

01:43:44.520 --> 01:43:46.400
So it shouldn't be that way.

01:43:46.400 --> 01:43:48.220
But the way that the system is set up now

01:43:48.220 --> 01:43:51.860
is that huge amounts of subsidies are given to agriculture

01:43:51.860 --> 01:43:54.750
and animal agriculture receives the most amount of subsidies

01:43:54.750 --> 01:43:56.540
to drive down production costs

01:43:56.540 --> 01:43:58.390
and make it cheaper for consumers.

01:43:58.390 --> 01:43:59.870
At the same time as doing that,

01:43:59.870 --> 01:44:01.520
we have the system of factory farming,

01:44:01.520 --> 01:44:03.990
which drives down production costs even further.

01:44:03.990 --> 01:44:05.560
So we're just constantly finding ways

01:44:05.560 --> 01:44:07.260
to bring down the cost of these foods.

01:44:07.260 --> 01:44:09.980
And then these fast food chains are opening up in areas

01:44:09.980 --> 01:44:12.100
where people don't have the money

01:44:12.100 --> 01:44:14.500
to be able to maybe travel to some supermarkets.

01:44:14.500 --> 01:44:15.333
They don't have the money

01:44:15.333 --> 01:44:17.700
to be able to rent a car to pick up groceries

01:44:17.700 --> 01:44:19.750
and who don't have supermarkets in their area

01:44:19.750 --> 01:44:21.440
and the availability and accessibility

01:44:21.440 --> 01:44:22.750
that a lot of us have.

01:44:22.750 --> 01:44:24.400
And so are forced out of that situation

01:44:24.400 --> 01:44:26.240
to feed their families on a budget,

01:44:26.240 --> 01:44:27.230
which can often mean buying

01:44:27.230 --> 01:44:30.550
this subsidized cheaply, produced meat, dairy, and eggs.

01:44:30.550 --> 01:44:33.320
So we have to challenge that system.

01:44:33.320 --> 01:44:35.670
And the best way we can do that is firstly

01:44:35.670 --> 01:44:36.810
being vegan ourselves

01:44:36.810 --> 01:44:39.280
and asking for a different food system in general.

01:44:39.280 --> 01:44:40.570
And then through doing that,

01:44:40.570 --> 01:44:42.980
we can start to ask for a change in subsidy policy.

01:44:42.980 --> 01:44:45.700
For example, according to Bloomberg last year,

01:44:45.700 --> 01:44:47.831
the agriculture industry in the US

01:44:47.831 --> 01:44:51.090
had its third most profitable year in half a century.

01:44:51.090 --> 01:44:52.320
But how does that make sense

01:44:52.320 --> 01:44:53.960
because it was during the pandemic.

01:44:53.960 --> 01:44:57.240
Restaurants were shut down, there was food problems.

01:44:57.240 --> 01:44:59.460
And the reason why, was because there was so much money

01:44:59.460 --> 01:45:01.610
given in bailouts and subsidies.

01:45:01.610 --> 01:45:04.200
So the animal farming industry had a very profitable year

01:45:04.200 --> 01:45:05.700
because of the government handouts

01:45:05.700 --> 01:45:07.410
that were granted to them.

01:45:07.410 --> 01:45:09.320
So if we can reverse that situation

01:45:09.320 --> 01:45:11.820
where we can stop giving subsidies to harmful industries

01:45:11.820 --> 01:45:13.210
and instead use these subsidies

01:45:13.210 --> 01:45:15.670
to drive down production costs for plant foods,

01:45:15.670 --> 01:45:18.310
to incentivize technological advancements

01:45:18.310 --> 01:45:20.240
in the plant-based food space,

01:45:20.240 --> 01:45:22.970
we can alter the price of these products,

01:45:22.970 --> 01:45:24.940
make them more accessible and available.

01:45:24.940 --> 01:45:25.900
And by being vegan,

01:45:25.900 --> 01:45:27.640
we're changing the supply and demand scenario,

01:45:27.640 --> 01:45:29.667
which means that beyond meat and Oatly

01:45:29.667 --> 01:45:32.580
and all these plant-based companies can scale at production,

01:45:32.580 --> 01:45:34.590
can reduce costs over time as well.

01:45:34.590 --> 01:45:37.230
And what we can do is we can reverse the current paradigm,

01:45:37.230 --> 01:45:40.080
which is that meat, dairy, and eggs are generally cheaper

01:45:40.080 --> 01:45:42.310
and plant-based alternative are generally more expensive

01:45:42.310 --> 01:45:45.600
and make not only whole foods even more inexpensive,

01:45:45.600 --> 01:45:48.660
but also meet people's demand for the alternatives as well

01:45:48.660 --> 01:45:50.970
in a way that is more financially viable.

01:45:50.970 --> 01:45:52.260
It's a broken system.

01:45:52.260 --> 01:45:53.420
Yeah, it really is.

01:45:53.420 --> 01:45:57.990
And I despair of the lack of political will

01:45:57.990 --> 01:46:00.850
to untie this knot around subsidies

01:46:00.850 --> 01:46:03.390
that's driving so many of these problems.

01:46:03.390 --> 01:46:06.830
It's one thing to speak about the grassroots movement

01:46:06.830 --> 01:46:11.830
and shifting demand through aggregating consumer habits

01:46:12.490 --> 01:46:14.240
around healthier choices,

01:46:14.240 --> 01:46:17.740
but the change also has to come from the top down

01:46:17.740 --> 01:46:20.923
in terms of government getting its head around this.

01:46:22.520 --> 01:46:26.100
When you see this sort of musical chairs that occurs

01:46:26.100 --> 01:46:31.100
between governing bodies and regulatory bodies,

01:46:31.370 --> 01:46:35.280
and C-suites of these giant food companies,

01:46:35.280 --> 01:46:39.300
it's easy to like lose my ability

01:46:39.300 --> 01:46:43.160
to be enthusiastic about a potential good future

01:46:43.160 --> 01:46:46.650
in terms of fixing this problem.

01:46:46.650 --> 01:46:50.780
Yeah, it's daunting and it may sound slight depressing,

01:46:50.780 --> 01:46:54.360
but even right now, the us US Secretary of Agriculture

01:46:54.360 --> 01:46:55.680
is a guy called Tom Vilsack

01:46:55.680 --> 01:46:58.410
and before he was the current secretary of agriculture,

01:46:58.410 --> 01:47:00.560
he was the CEO of the Dairy Export Council.

01:47:00.560 --> 01:47:02.930
And this is normal operating procedure.

01:47:02.930 --> 01:47:04.020
It's just a revolving door.

01:47:04.020 --> 01:47:05.230
So it is troubling.

01:47:05.230 --> 01:47:07.480
And I think this is why we as consumers

01:47:07.480 --> 01:47:11.290
have to feel more because our elected officials

01:47:11.290 --> 01:47:12.700
currently aren't gonna change anything.

01:47:12.700 --> 01:47:14.250
Yeah, no one's coming to save us.

01:47:14.250 --> 01:47:15.240
No.
So I guess there is,

01:47:15.240 --> 01:47:17.220
yeah, there is an empowering,

01:47:17.220 --> 01:47:21.270
that's something that can provide us with a sense of agency.

01:47:21.270 --> 01:47:22.103
Definitely.

01:47:22.103 --> 01:47:24.180
Even think about climate change and environmental policies

01:47:24.180 --> 01:47:25.090
and the green new deal

01:47:25.090 --> 01:47:27.490
and these issues that have been spoken about now,

01:47:27.490 --> 01:47:29.640
when Al Gore released an on being in truth,

01:47:29.640 --> 01:47:31.020
they weren't being spoken about.

01:47:31.020 --> 01:47:32.310
So something's changed

01:47:32.310 --> 01:47:34.120
and I think that's because there is now an appetite

01:47:34.120 --> 01:47:36.993
within society for these to become political issues.

01:47:36.993 --> 01:47:40.040
Now, politicians just generally follow the trend

01:47:40.040 --> 01:47:44.240
of what is seen as a viable voting concept.

01:47:44.240 --> 01:47:46.090
And right now changing subsidies

01:47:46.090 --> 01:47:47.860
from animal agriculture to plant-based farming

01:47:47.860 --> 01:47:49.930
isn't a viable voting concept.

01:47:49.930 --> 01:47:52.360
It's not gonna win votes, it'll probably lose votes.

01:47:52.360 --> 01:47:54.490
So we have to make these issues

01:47:54.490 --> 01:47:56.680
something that politicians will stand on,

01:47:56.680 --> 01:47:57.840
because I'm sure there are politicians

01:47:57.840 --> 01:48:00.640
that would be more outspoken who maybe would agree,

01:48:00.640 --> 01:48:03.647
but they don't want to risk jeopardizing the votes

01:48:03.647 --> 01:48:05.690
that they have and the constituents they represent.

01:48:05.690 --> 01:48:08.750
So we as consumers have to make that choice first.

01:48:08.750 --> 01:48:10.230
And then we have to demand

01:48:10.230 --> 01:48:11.830
that there is legislative change

01:48:12.746 --> 01:48:14.660
that is implement as a consequence

01:48:14.660 --> 01:48:16.170
of us wanting it to happen.

01:48:16.170 --> 01:48:17.003
'Cause it won't happen

01:48:17.003 --> 01:48:19.630
if we keep perpetuating the same systems we currently do.

01:48:19.630 --> 01:48:21.150
Yeah, even at COP26,

01:48:21.150 --> 01:48:24.080
animal agriculture wasn't really on the agenda

01:48:24.080 --> 01:48:27.760
to be discussed, which is just infuriating,

01:48:27.760 --> 01:48:29.200
it's unbelievable.
It's crazy, isn't it?

01:48:29.200 --> 01:48:31.710
Even at COP20, this Tom Vilsack,

01:48:31.710 --> 01:48:34.140
the secretary of agriculture here in the US,

01:48:34.140 --> 01:48:35.640
whilst everyone was debating

01:48:35.640 --> 01:48:38.342
about reducing MEFA emissions by 30%, by 2030,

01:48:38.342 --> 01:48:41.370
he was saying to the press that in the US,

01:48:41.370 --> 01:48:44.340
there is no need to reduce the amount of meats or livestock

01:48:44.340 --> 01:48:47.010
that have been produced, during COP26 said that.

01:48:47.010 --> 01:48:49.370
And it's just contradicting all of the sciences coming out,

01:48:49.370 --> 01:48:52.210
even from the United Nations who are telling us,

01:48:52.210 --> 01:48:55.300
we need to switch to plant-based food systems,

01:48:55.300 --> 01:48:56.930
we need to transition in that direction.

01:48:56.930 --> 01:48:59.730
While they're breaking for lunch and going and eating,

01:49:00.770 --> 01:49:01.823
cheeseburgers and stuff like that.

01:49:01.823 --> 01:49:05.343
It's so frustrating.
It's just like unbelievable.

01:49:06.750 --> 01:49:09.310
You did a podcast with Jack Harris a while back

01:49:09.310 --> 01:49:13.243
where you guys discussed this under this rubric of like,

01:49:15.070 --> 01:49:17.187
can you be an environmentalist if you're not vegan,

01:49:17.187 --> 01:49:19.360
and it was kind of a broader conversation

01:49:19.360 --> 01:49:23.410
around what advocacy means and how we define it.

01:49:23.410 --> 01:49:26.640
But certainly in the wake of COP26,

01:49:26.640 --> 01:49:30.140
it's like, guys, we need to sit down and talk.

01:49:30.140 --> 01:49:32.703
Seriously.
Like, what are we we doing?

01:49:34.190 --> 01:49:35.260
It's very frustrating

01:49:35.260 --> 01:49:37.490
that there has been such little conversation about this.

01:49:37.490 --> 01:49:38.610
And I think I mentioned in the book

01:49:38.610 --> 01:49:41.220
that there have been some kind of studies

01:49:41.220 --> 01:49:44.180
that have shown that when you look at articles

01:49:44.180 --> 01:49:45.420
about climate change,

01:49:45.420 --> 01:49:46.330
I don't know, like a few percent of them

01:49:46.330 --> 01:49:49.550
even mention a agriculture, let alone make that a focus.

01:49:49.550 --> 01:49:51.630
So even within these,

01:49:51.630 --> 01:49:55.540
I suppose what you'd consider to be friendly, media outlets,

01:49:55.540 --> 01:49:57.810
places like The New York Times or The Guardian

01:49:57.810 --> 01:49:58.643
or wherever it might be,

01:49:58.643 --> 01:50:00.170
where we would like to think that they would be the ones

01:50:00.170 --> 01:50:01.980
to maybe talk about this issue.

01:50:01.980 --> 01:50:04.040
There is still a reluctance to mention it.

01:50:04.040 --> 01:50:04.980
There is still a reluctance

01:50:04.980 --> 01:50:05.913
to tell people about these problems.

01:50:05.913 --> 01:50:09.159
Even with George Monbiot just like going nuts

01:50:09.159 --> 01:50:10.780
in The Guardian all the time.

01:50:10.780 --> 01:50:12.140
I mean, thank goodness that he exists

01:50:12.140 --> 01:50:13.963
and that he's our voice in The Guardian,

01:50:13.963 --> 01:50:14.870
'cause if it wasn't for him,

01:50:14.870 --> 01:50:17.300
it would feel like we had no one backing us up

01:50:17.300 --> 01:50:18.420
in that media space.

01:50:18.420 --> 01:50:19.800
And it's very frustrating

01:50:19.800 --> 01:50:22.940
because people need that normalization of these ideas

01:50:22.940 --> 01:50:24.920
in the media, they consume,

01:50:24.920 --> 01:50:26.730
there's something called the elusory truth effect,

01:50:26.730 --> 01:50:30.080
which that basically the more you hear something,

01:50:30.080 --> 01:50:31.780
the more you are likely to believe that it's true

01:50:31.780 --> 01:50:32.950
even if it isn't.

01:50:32.950 --> 01:50:34.360
And so if we're constantly hearing

01:50:34.360 --> 01:50:37.090
that consuming meat, dairy eggs is fine, it's not a concern.

01:50:37.090 --> 01:50:38.468
it's moral, it's humane.

01:50:38.468 --> 01:50:40.810
We're gonna believe that even if that's not the case.

01:50:40.810 --> 01:50:43.500
And so we need this proliferation of content

01:50:43.500 --> 01:50:45.770
telling people why that's not true

01:50:45.770 --> 01:50:48.240
so that we can challenge this elusive truth effects,

01:50:48.240 --> 01:50:51.540
so that we can challenge this current paradigm of the media

01:50:51.540 --> 01:50:52.920
and people's perceptions of veganism

01:50:52.920 --> 01:50:54.200
being something that's bad

01:50:54.200 --> 01:50:57.450
when in effect it's incredibly good in so many ways.

01:50:57.450 --> 01:50:59.050
Well, it's a good news, bad news thing.

01:50:59.050 --> 01:51:01.790
The bad news is mainstream media

01:51:01.790 --> 01:51:04.558
isn't gonna cut onto that narrative anytime soon,

01:51:04.558 --> 01:51:08.120
but there's stuff like this and what you do

01:51:08.120 --> 01:51:09.626
that's becoming increasingly

01:51:09.626 --> 01:51:11.880
more and more salient and powerful

01:51:11.880 --> 01:51:16.880
in terms of shifting citizen ideas

01:51:16.940 --> 01:51:19.070
around these important topics.

01:51:19.070 --> 01:51:20.850
Certainly that's the important thing

01:51:20.850 --> 01:51:22.321
about social media, isn't it?

01:51:22.321 --> 01:51:24.640
And I think the important thing about people realizing

01:51:24.640 --> 01:51:27.240
that their voice is valuable and important

01:51:27.240 --> 01:51:29.250
and that we have hour to influence

01:51:29.250 --> 01:51:31.680
our immediate social circles.

01:51:31.680 --> 01:51:32.950
There's a consequence of that.

01:51:32.950 --> 01:51:35.900
There's a ripple effect, where we influence those people,

01:51:35.900 --> 01:51:37.930
they can influence the people in their social circles

01:51:37.930 --> 01:51:41.510
and we, as individuals can achieve a lot

01:51:41.510 --> 01:51:43.260
and it can be disheartening of course,

01:51:43.260 --> 01:51:46.200
to see that there are these subsidy policies

01:51:46.200 --> 01:51:49.800
and there are these corporations, monopolizing markets

01:51:49.800 --> 01:51:52.560
and there is this proliferation of misinformation,

01:51:52.560 --> 01:51:53.820
that is demoralizing,

01:51:53.820 --> 01:51:55.570
it can feel like we're up against this

01:51:55.570 --> 01:51:58.280
almost unassailable beast of a problem,

01:51:58.280 --> 01:52:00.020
but ultimately there are happening

01:52:00.020 --> 01:52:01.450
and there are cracks forming

01:52:01.450 --> 01:52:06.450
in the the, otherwise sturdy armor of these industries,

01:52:06.450 --> 01:52:09.490
the cracks are forming and we are starting

01:52:09.490 --> 01:52:11.510
to get the message heard by people

01:52:11.510 --> 01:52:13.430
who maybe wouldn't have heard it otherwise.

01:52:13.430 --> 01:52:15.880
I think it's just really important to recognize

01:52:15.880 --> 01:52:19.040
that change comes from individual action,

01:52:19.040 --> 01:52:21.380
forming a mass movement, a collective action,

01:52:21.380 --> 01:52:23.503
and we can all be a part of that. So it's about working out

01:52:23.503 --> 01:52:25.120
what we want the future to look like.

01:52:25.120 --> 01:52:26.704
Do we want this future to be a future

01:52:26.704 --> 01:52:28.760
which does seek to reduce suffering

01:52:28.760 --> 01:52:33.760
that does seek to create fairer more equal food systems.

01:52:34.100 --> 01:52:35.820
That means that people can have options,

01:52:35.820 --> 01:52:37.020
can have accessibility

01:52:37.020 --> 01:52:39.460
and availability of healthy plant-based foods.

01:52:39.460 --> 01:52:41.130
Do we want people to be more empowered

01:52:41.130 --> 01:52:42.530
to make individual choices?

01:52:42.530 --> 01:52:44.910
Do we want people to feel empowered to recognize

01:52:44.910 --> 01:52:48.211
that they themselves are a part of this huge planet,

01:52:48.211 --> 01:52:50.050
but also a very small planet

01:52:50.050 --> 01:52:52.060
where we have the power to influence change

01:52:52.060 --> 01:52:53.240
for the choices that we make.

01:52:53.240 --> 01:52:56.100
And veganism is a stepping stone, I think,

01:52:56.100 --> 01:52:58.550
to recognizing the broader impact

01:52:58.550 --> 01:53:01.070
that we all have collectively, but also individually.

01:53:01.070 --> 01:53:04.170
And it's by no means the only step we need to need to take,

01:53:04.170 --> 01:53:06.526
but it a huge step in the right direction

01:53:06.526 --> 01:53:10.497
in fixing, tackling, addressing, acknowledging

01:53:10.497 --> 01:53:12.370
so many different issues

01:53:12.370 --> 01:53:13.460
that up until this point,

01:53:13.460 --> 01:53:16.493
we have often turned blind eye too, sadly.

01:53:18.210 --> 01:53:21.010
Ordinarily, I would say that would be a great place

01:53:21.010 --> 01:53:22.160
to like put a pin in it,

01:53:22.160 --> 01:53:25.265
'cause it was like this super compelling monologue

01:53:25.265 --> 01:53:27.650
that was delivered.

01:53:27.650 --> 01:53:28.690
But there's a couple more things

01:53:28.690 --> 01:53:29.990
I wanna talk to you about.

01:53:31.720 --> 01:53:33.870
I wanna learn a little bit about Surge,

01:53:33.870 --> 01:53:37.180
which is your nonprofit organization

01:53:37.180 --> 01:53:40.420
that you founded or co-founded several years ago

01:53:40.420 --> 01:53:44.100
that also has a media arm and now you have a sanctuary,

01:53:44.100 --> 01:53:46.910
like how do you make all of the, these things work?

01:53:46.910 --> 01:53:49.460
And I wanna talk about the restaurants too.

01:53:49.460 --> 01:53:53.220
I'm super lucky to have a supportive community around me

01:53:53.220 --> 01:53:56.230
of people who work in the sanctuary,

01:53:56.230 --> 01:53:57.540
who do the day to day stuff there,

01:53:57.540 --> 01:54:00.840
looking after these animals, we have over 130 animals now.

01:54:00.840 --> 01:54:02.760
So, there's a wonderful group of people

01:54:02.760 --> 01:54:04.810
that are doing the day to day work there,

01:54:06.320 --> 01:54:09.944
So yeah, my partner and I, we founded it five years ago now

01:54:09.944 --> 01:54:13.570
and we've just recently been growing the team.

01:54:13.570 --> 01:54:16.910
We've got editors, animators, writers.

01:54:16.910 --> 01:54:18.760
And so it's just been a really wonderful process

01:54:18.760 --> 01:54:22.550
of us having this idea of what we want to achieve and do,

01:54:22.550 --> 01:54:24.670
but then finding other people that are mission aligned

01:54:24.670 --> 01:54:26.330
and incredibly talented people

01:54:26.330 --> 01:54:28.860
and having them part of this process

01:54:28.860 --> 01:54:30.480
as well has been really rewarding.

01:54:30.480 --> 01:54:33.010
The animators and the filmmakers and the editors

01:54:33.010 --> 01:54:34.500
that you have are unbelievable.

01:54:34.500 --> 01:54:37.020
Like the quality of the content that you're putting out

01:54:37.020 --> 01:54:39.240
is so elevated

01:54:39.240 --> 01:54:42.750
in comparison to everything else that's out there.

01:54:42.750 --> 01:54:44.730
So I would encourage you to keep going.

01:54:44.730 --> 01:54:47.230
I certainly will.
And part of Surge

01:54:47.230 --> 01:54:49.750
is funded by Unity Diner, right?

01:54:49.750 --> 01:54:51.220
So you opened this restaurant.

01:54:51.220 --> 01:54:53.870
That's also ostensibly a nonprofit

01:54:53.870 --> 01:54:57.870
because the funds are channeled right back into Surge.

01:54:57.870 --> 01:54:58.703
That's right.

01:54:58.703 --> 01:55:00.356
So we had this idea a few years ago

01:55:00.356 --> 01:55:02.440
that we wanted to do something

01:55:02.440 --> 01:55:04.500
that kind of gave back the community,

01:55:04.500 --> 01:55:07.550
but also could be used as a method of raising funds

01:55:07.550 --> 01:55:09.440
to allow us to do some of things that we wanted to do,

01:55:09.440 --> 01:55:12.543
primarily open the animal sanctuary that we currently have.

01:55:13.557 --> 01:55:15.243
So we came up this idea of like a restaurant

01:55:15.243 --> 01:55:18.420
where it would be operating on a nonprofit basis,

01:55:18.420 --> 01:55:19.253
where as you say,

01:55:19.253 --> 01:55:21.629
the money would go into funding Surge projects.

01:55:21.629 --> 01:55:24.780
So we've done like two ads advertising around London

01:55:24.780 --> 01:55:27.660
and the money's gone towards opening the animal sanctuary

01:55:27.660 --> 01:55:31.270
that we now have have in the kind of middle of England.

01:55:31.270 --> 01:55:33.320
And so, yeah, it was just an idea that we had

01:55:33.320 --> 01:55:36.240
and it kind of snowballed very quickly.

01:55:36.240 --> 01:55:37.330
And then we were like, oh my goodness,

01:55:37.330 --> 01:55:38.899
we have this restaurant,

01:55:38.899 --> 01:55:39.732
none of us know what we're actually doing.

01:55:39.732 --> 01:55:41.360
So fingers crossed we can work it out.

01:55:41.360 --> 01:55:46.060
And it's been amazing, amazing to meet so many people.

01:55:46.060 --> 01:55:47.410
And it's given me a newfound awareness

01:55:47.410 --> 01:55:49.023
of the importance of food.

01:55:49.023 --> 01:55:51.150
I think that this thing about veganism

01:55:51.150 --> 01:55:53.410
is kind of like a jewel process

01:55:53.410 --> 01:55:55.810
where we need to give people the reason why,

01:55:55.810 --> 01:55:57.880
but we also have to give people the how,

01:55:57.880 --> 01:56:01.090
and without the how the food the delicious alternatives,

01:56:01.090 --> 01:56:02.540
the recipes that exist.

01:56:02.540 --> 01:56:04.700
We can talk about the why as much as we like,

01:56:04.700 --> 01:56:06.160
but people won't make that change.

01:56:06.160 --> 01:56:09.990
And having non-vegans come in and try plant-based food,

01:56:09.990 --> 01:56:11.863
or at least try some of these plant-based foods

01:56:11.863 --> 01:56:13.260
for the first time, the burgers, hot dogs,

01:56:13.260 --> 01:56:14.690
whatever it might be.

01:56:14.690 --> 01:56:17.984
And hearing them say about how they never realized

01:56:17.984 --> 01:56:19.150
that plant-based food could taste like this,

01:56:19.150 --> 01:56:22.352
or they never realized they could have fish and chips

01:56:22.352 --> 01:56:25.420
that's vegan, for example, is a really rewarding thing.

01:56:25.420 --> 01:56:27.820
That's the good, it's the no catch company

01:56:27.820 --> 01:56:28.810
that you just started.

01:56:28.810 --> 01:56:30.120
How many of those do you have now?

01:56:30.120 --> 01:56:31.200
We have one currently,

01:56:31.200 --> 01:56:33.582
which was just opened-
Brighton.

01:56:33.582 --> 01:56:35.220
In Brighton.

01:56:35.220 --> 01:56:37.790
So it's a completely vegan fish and chip shop,

01:56:37.790 --> 01:56:40.134
and fish and chips in the UK is a big thing,

01:56:40.134 --> 01:56:41.030
it's like a huge industry.

01:56:41.030 --> 01:56:42.350
And I was raised on fish and chips,

01:56:42.350 --> 01:56:44.430
like so many British people were,

01:56:44.430 --> 01:56:46.801
and it's a really damaging industry.

01:56:46.801 --> 01:56:48.710
And plant-based seafood options

01:56:48.710 --> 01:56:50.380
are kind of few and far between,

01:56:50.380 --> 01:56:54.300
it's still like a very new niche emerging market.

01:56:54.300 --> 01:56:56.460
And we have this tow fish product

01:56:56.460 --> 01:56:58.100
that we sell at Unity Diner which people love,

01:56:58.100 --> 01:56:59.398
it's my favorite thing.

01:56:59.398 --> 01:57:00.780
And we were like, why don't we do something

01:57:00.780 --> 01:57:03.620
that try and takes on the fish and chip shop industry

01:57:03.620 --> 01:57:05.450
that shows is that we don't have to keep

01:57:05.450 --> 01:57:06.470
pillaging our oceans

01:57:06.470 --> 01:57:09.457
and killing trillions of marine animals all the time,

01:57:09.457 --> 01:57:10.900
but we can do it different way.

01:57:10.900 --> 01:57:12.520
And so we open up the shop in Brighton,

01:57:12.520 --> 01:57:14.790
which has been really well received so far

01:57:14.790 --> 01:57:17.010
and our plan is to open up more,

01:57:17.010 --> 01:57:18.490
hopefully around the UK

01:57:18.490 --> 01:57:20.440
and kind of start to make a little bit of the dent

01:57:20.440 --> 01:57:22.853
in this fish and chip shop industry.

01:57:24.201 --> 01:57:27.400
Well, you said food is a powerful form of advocacy

01:57:27.400 --> 01:57:30.200
and I think that's a good reminder

01:57:30.200 --> 01:57:31.750
to pivot back to this conversation

01:57:31.750 --> 01:57:32.730
that I wanted to have with you

01:57:32.730 --> 01:57:35.720
around what constitutes effective advocacy.

01:57:35.720 --> 01:57:38.410
And we need all different kinds of advocates.

01:57:38.410 --> 01:57:40.180
We need extinction rebellion

01:57:40.180 --> 01:57:43.140
and we need Peter and we need people like yourself,

01:57:43.140 --> 01:57:47.980
like whatever its you and whatever feels correct

01:57:47.980 --> 01:57:50.290
in your blueprint or your constitution

01:57:52.156 --> 01:57:54.370
is needed in all forms, I think.

01:57:54.370 --> 01:57:56.310
But I think there are core principles

01:57:56.310 --> 01:57:59.040
around things at work and things that don't work.

01:57:59.040 --> 01:58:00.610
And as somebody who's like out in the field,

01:58:00.610 --> 01:58:01.710
talking to people all the time,

01:58:01.710 --> 01:58:03.500
I mean, you alluded to it

01:58:03.500 --> 01:58:05.020
and we've talked a little bit about it,

01:58:05.020 --> 01:58:09.850
but not everybody has your level of skill and argumentation

01:58:09.850 --> 01:58:12.290
or your linguistic abilities.

01:58:12.290 --> 01:58:14.100
So for people who are trying to figure out

01:58:14.100 --> 01:58:16.800
how to be effective advocates in their community,

01:58:16.800 --> 01:58:18.610
whether it's veganism or environmentalism

01:58:18.610 --> 01:58:21.095
or whatever it is that you're passionate about,

01:58:21.095 --> 01:58:24.630
how do you counsel that person

01:58:24.630 --> 01:58:27.340
so that they can be maximally effective

01:58:27.340 --> 01:58:28.740
in what they're trying to do.

01:58:28.740 --> 01:58:30.635
Yeah. It's a really good question.

01:58:30.635 --> 01:58:32.173
You are absolutely right.

01:58:32.173 --> 01:58:35.720
That we need so many different types of activism,

01:58:35.720 --> 01:58:38.330
types of advocacy, because we're up against this,

01:58:38.330 --> 01:58:39.850
I like to think of it as like a hydra,

01:58:39.850 --> 01:58:40.950
like a multi-headed beast

01:58:40.950 --> 01:58:42.980
and we need different types of advocacy

01:58:42.980 --> 01:58:45.012
to take off all the different heads of this hydra.

01:58:45.012 --> 01:58:47.300
And so it's important to not feel

01:58:47.300 --> 01:58:49.960
like you have to do activism in a certain way,

01:58:49.960 --> 01:58:51.110
but just find the voice that you have.

01:58:51.110 --> 01:58:52.940
And I think apply the skills that you have

01:58:52.940 --> 01:58:55.680
and things that you already enjoy into your advocacy,

01:58:55.680 --> 01:58:59.230
because it's really important to be sustainable as well

01:58:59.230 --> 01:59:03.250
and to not feel burn out by doing things that are grueling

01:59:03.250 --> 01:59:06.940
and maybe contradict our passions in life.

01:59:06.940 --> 01:59:08.760
So if you're an artist, you're a filmmaker,

01:59:08.760 --> 01:59:10.140
you are a, a songwriter.

01:59:10.140 --> 01:59:12.410
I think combining those passions with advocacy

01:59:12.410 --> 01:59:13.650
is a really important thing.

01:59:13.650 --> 01:59:15.710
But for me, I always say that the primary thing

01:59:15.710 --> 01:59:18.490
that we should all do is take the time

01:59:18.490 --> 01:59:20.700
to educate ourselves as vegans,

01:59:20.700 --> 01:59:23.850
to the time to learn what people's oppositions are.

01:59:23.850 --> 01:59:24.790
And we don't have to go too deep.

01:59:24.790 --> 01:59:26.560
I mean, I have probably gone deeper

01:59:26.560 --> 01:59:27.890
into these arguments than most people do,

01:59:27.890 --> 01:59:29.600
and most people need to,

01:59:29.600 --> 01:59:30.660
basically, I guess,

01:59:30.660 --> 01:59:32.600
because I'm talking to so many different people

01:59:32.600 --> 01:59:34.220
from different walks of life,

01:59:34.220 --> 01:59:35.080
but I think primarily

01:59:35.080 --> 01:59:36.620
you just need to find the main objections,

01:59:36.620 --> 01:59:38.290
the main reasons that people have

01:59:38.290 --> 01:59:40.810
and work out how to respond to those.

01:59:40.810 --> 01:59:44.330
And I think that there are definitely ways of communicating

01:59:44.330 --> 01:59:45.970
that are more effective than others.

01:59:45.970 --> 01:59:47.690
And so it's about maybe taking the time

01:59:47.690 --> 01:59:50.290
to reflect on before you were vegan,

01:59:50.290 --> 01:59:51.487
how would you have responded?

01:59:51.487 --> 01:59:53.025
And I always think this,

01:59:53.025 --> 01:59:55.430
and I would love to sit down with myself seven years ago,

01:59:55.430 --> 01:59:58.040
eight years ago, and how the conversations I have now,

01:59:58.040 --> 01:59:59.610
but with myself back then,

01:59:59.610 --> 02:00:01.570
and to see how I would've reacted.

02:00:01.570 --> 02:00:02.740
I don't know how I would've reacted.

02:00:02.740 --> 02:00:05.704
But what I do know is that communicating

02:00:05.704 --> 02:00:07.110
in the way that I try to now

02:00:07.110 --> 02:00:07.943
would've been more effective

02:00:07.943 --> 02:00:09.310
than communicating in a more aggressive,

02:00:09.310 --> 02:00:10.580
more judgemental way.

02:00:10.580 --> 02:00:11.677
So I think trying to put yourself in that position

02:00:11.677 --> 02:00:14.700
of the person you're speaking to and trying to reflect on

02:00:14.700 --> 02:00:15.770
what would've worked for you,

02:00:15.770 --> 02:00:17.670
and can that be translated into what will work for them

02:00:17.670 --> 02:00:19.400
is a really important thing to do.

02:00:19.400 --> 02:00:20.890
But the cornerstone to it is education

02:00:20.890 --> 02:00:23.083
because education builds confidence.

02:00:25.263 --> 02:00:27.510
I felt deeply uncomfortable at the beginning

02:00:27.510 --> 02:00:29.150
because I was always worried

02:00:29.150 --> 02:00:30.343
that someone was gonna say something

02:00:30.343 --> 02:00:31.957
I didn't know how to respond to.

02:00:31.957 --> 02:00:33.920
And I think a lot of my frustration

02:00:33.920 --> 02:00:38.180
and my maybe anger at the beginning when I was advocating,

02:00:38.180 --> 02:00:39.860
came, not from the person I was speaking to,

02:00:39.860 --> 02:00:40.870
but from myself,

02:00:40.870 --> 02:00:43.140
a frustration that I didn't know what to say,

02:00:43.140 --> 02:00:45.330
a frustration that I didn't know how to respond,

02:00:45.330 --> 02:00:48.040
that I was angry at myself for letting the animals down

02:00:48.040 --> 02:00:50.920
because I didn't have this great rebuttal

02:00:50.920 --> 02:00:52.636
to this one argument the person was using.

02:00:52.636 --> 02:00:55.300
So the more I took the time to research,

02:00:55.300 --> 02:00:56.510
the more to the time to learn

02:00:56.510 --> 02:00:58.316
and the more practice I had,

02:00:58.316 --> 02:01:00.880
the easier it got to be hopefully more level headed,

02:01:00.880 --> 02:01:02.190
to be more empathetic,

02:01:02.190 --> 02:01:05.400
to be more understanding of the personal viewpoint

02:01:05.400 --> 02:01:07.710
the person has that I'm speaking to,

02:01:07.710 --> 02:01:10.040
because I felt confident enough for my capabilities

02:01:10.040 --> 02:01:11.533
to be able to address what they were saying.

02:01:11.533 --> 02:01:14.670
I didn't feel worried about letting the animals down

02:01:14.670 --> 02:01:15.910
or the movement down.

02:01:15.910 --> 02:01:18.540
So I think it is about education and then practice.

02:01:18.540 --> 02:01:19.910
Get used to having these conversations.

02:01:19.910 --> 02:01:21.530
You could, even, if you have a vegan friend,

02:01:21.530 --> 02:01:24.050
just have dummy conversations with them, practice,

02:01:24.050 --> 02:01:25.027
I kind of what we did earlier.

02:01:25.027 --> 02:01:26.730
You said, but Ed, what about this?

02:01:26.730 --> 02:01:28.430
And I hopefully gave a good response.

02:01:28.430 --> 02:01:30.320
And I think doing that's a really important thing to do

02:01:30.320 --> 02:01:32.810
because it gets used to vocalizing your feelings,

02:01:32.810 --> 02:01:34.760
vocalizing your beliefs.

02:01:34.760 --> 02:01:36.820
Because it's one thing, knowing what you want to say,

02:01:36.820 --> 02:01:38.750
and it's a different thing actually saying it.

02:01:38.750 --> 02:01:42.390
So I think just practicing having that external vocalization

02:01:42.390 --> 02:01:43.960
is also very important.

02:01:43.960 --> 02:01:46.800
It's also the energy that you bring to the experience.

02:01:46.800 --> 02:01:49.920
I mean, I think empathy and listening super important,

02:01:49.920 --> 02:01:53.470
and just take your videos for example,

02:01:53.470 --> 02:01:55.470
where you're in these conversations with people,

02:01:55.470 --> 02:01:58.657
I think there's sort of an internet sensibility,

02:01:58.657 --> 02:02:00.470
the like, oh, I'm gonna watch this person

02:02:00.470 --> 02:02:02.840
own this other person or win this argument

02:02:02.840 --> 02:02:04.217
or beat this person,

02:02:04.217 --> 02:02:07.020
but that's not what's happening

02:02:07.020 --> 02:02:08.850
if you're watching what you're doing closely,

02:02:08.850 --> 02:02:10.796
like you're engaging this person

02:02:10.796 --> 02:02:14.220
on their terms at their level, you're listening,

02:02:14.220 --> 02:02:15.640
you're not interrupting.

02:02:15.640 --> 02:02:17.780
You're trying to understand their perspective.

02:02:17.780 --> 02:02:18.990
You're acknowledging them.

02:02:18.990 --> 02:02:23.150
All of these things are critical, not just in advocacy,

02:02:23.150 --> 02:02:25.493
but in human relations in general.

02:02:26.370 --> 02:02:28.276
And I think we're also quick,

02:02:28.276 --> 02:02:29.990
if there's frustrations

02:02:29.990 --> 02:02:32.410
around that process of argumentation,

02:02:32.410 --> 02:02:34.470
it's like I'm trying to win.

02:02:34.470 --> 02:02:36.590
And it's like, if you can just let go of that

02:02:36.590 --> 02:02:38.770
and be present for that other person

02:02:38.770 --> 02:02:41.320
and try understand where they're coming from.

02:02:41.320 --> 02:02:46.320
Not only will it sharpen your own ability to form arguments,

02:02:48.830 --> 02:02:51.780
it will stress test your ideas and you'll get better.

02:02:51.780 --> 02:02:55.750
But I think it's about letting go of the outcome of it

02:02:55.750 --> 02:02:58.280
and just being available for the experience

02:02:58.280 --> 02:03:00.160
that is super important.
It is.

02:03:00.160 --> 02:03:03.180
I think fundamentally we all just want to be heard.

02:03:03.180 --> 02:03:04.640
We all just want to want to listen to us

02:03:04.640 --> 02:03:06.380
and take us seriously.

02:03:06.380 --> 02:03:09.553
And there is this division,

02:03:10.570 --> 02:03:13.570
this kind of binary that is more prevalent now

02:03:13.570 --> 02:03:15.940
than probably it ever has been

02:03:15.940 --> 02:03:17.490
where I think people just don't feel heard

02:03:17.490 --> 02:03:19.740
where they feel maybe caricatured, stereotyped

02:03:21.340 --> 02:03:22.450
and it's really damaging.

02:03:22.450 --> 02:03:24.330
And so when I have these conversations,

02:03:24.330 --> 02:03:26.700
I guess the first thing I want to do

02:03:26.700 --> 02:03:28.140
is just make sure that the person knows

02:03:28.140 --> 02:03:30.060
that I'm not trying to own them.

02:03:30.060 --> 02:03:33.075
I'm not gonna put this video up vegan destroys-

02:03:33.075 --> 02:03:35.700
(indistinct)

02:03:35.700 --> 02:03:36.660
Exactly.

02:03:36.660 --> 02:03:39.280
Which is again, why I take those first few minutes

02:03:39.280 --> 02:03:40.980
at the beginning to build up a bit of a rapport.

02:03:40.980 --> 02:03:42.220
So just let someone feel comfortable

02:03:42.220 --> 02:03:44.540
knowing that's not my intentions.

02:03:44.540 --> 02:03:46.160
And sometimes I'll say don't worry

02:03:46.160 --> 02:03:47.460
that's not what we're here to do,

02:03:47.460 --> 02:03:49.120
I just wanna polite conversation

02:03:49.120 --> 02:03:51.000
because I think it's really important that people know that

02:03:51.000 --> 02:03:52.640
so they can just let their defenses down.

02:03:52.640 --> 02:03:56.040
And when I upload them, these are long format conversations.

02:03:56.040 --> 02:03:57.543
It's not like some of the stuff you see on YouTube,

02:03:57.543 --> 02:03:58.980
it's this chopped up stuff

02:03:58.980 --> 02:04:00.630
where someone's made to look silly

02:04:00.630 --> 02:04:01.890
and I'm not trying to you that.

02:04:01.890 --> 02:04:03.570
I want people to be able to express themselves.

02:04:03.570 --> 02:04:05.320
I want their arguments to be heard.

02:04:05.320 --> 02:04:07.790
And I want my arguments in response to be heard.

02:04:07.790 --> 02:04:10.490
And the powerful thing about making a video

02:04:10.490 --> 02:04:13.090
is I'm thinking about the audience as well.

02:04:13.090 --> 02:04:15.460
And having just two people sit down

02:04:15.460 --> 02:04:17.040
and just rationally have a conversation

02:04:17.040 --> 02:04:18.410
where they can shake hands at the end

02:04:18.410 --> 02:04:21.420
and maybe agree to disagree, but still have that ending

02:04:21.420 --> 02:04:22.760
means that the view who's watching

02:04:22.760 --> 02:04:23.593
has been taken along

02:04:23.593 --> 02:04:25.500
and they can create their own opinion

02:04:25.500 --> 02:04:27.670
based on two opposing arguments.

02:04:27.670 --> 02:04:28.503
How do they align?

02:04:28.503 --> 02:04:29.423
What do they think?

02:04:30.580 --> 02:04:32.170
I'm not under any false pretense

02:04:32.170 --> 02:04:34.397
that the person who sits down with me is gonna leave saying,

02:04:34.397 --> 02:04:35.930
"I'm gonna be vegan now."

02:04:35.930 --> 02:04:38.218
It has happened. But more often than not,

02:04:38.218 --> 02:04:39.510
that doesn't happen.

02:04:39.510 --> 02:04:41.713
And I try and make the point to people.

02:04:41.713 --> 02:04:42.600
And I've been saying it more recently

02:04:42.600 --> 02:04:45.010
in the conversations I've just had which I'll be uploading

02:04:45.010 --> 02:04:48.310
for the near future, I try and say to people at the end,

02:04:48.310 --> 02:04:51.790
the reason that I'm here isn't to make you leave the table

02:04:51.790 --> 02:04:52.770
saying you're gonna be vegan.

02:04:52.770 --> 02:04:54.900
I'd love for that to be the case but I understand it's not.

02:04:54.900 --> 02:04:56.350
It's for you to hopefully leave the table

02:04:56.350 --> 02:04:58.160
with an more positive impression of veganism

02:04:58.160 --> 02:04:59.760
than you had when you sat down.

02:04:59.760 --> 02:05:01.660
And that's why I think optics are really important

02:05:01.660 --> 02:05:05.830
because people don't often remember what they've heard,

02:05:05.830 --> 02:05:07.700
they remember how that person made them feel.

02:05:07.700 --> 02:05:09.520
We have arguments with people

02:05:09.520 --> 02:05:12.210
and we can sometimes forget what the argument was about

02:05:12.210 --> 02:05:15.420
or what the actual points were.

02:05:15.420 --> 02:05:16.840
We can get bogged down and all that,

02:05:16.840 --> 02:05:18.730
but we remember how they made us feel.

02:05:18.730 --> 02:05:20.500
And so I want someone to leave that conversation,

02:05:20.500 --> 02:05:22.250
having a positive feeling about it,

02:05:22.250 --> 02:05:23.657
because they might then go

02:05:23.657 --> 02:05:25.200
and think about these issues a bit more on their own,

02:05:25.200 --> 02:05:27.330
because they feel comfortable and confident doing that

02:05:27.330 --> 02:05:29.210
because I've made hopefully veganism

02:05:29.210 --> 02:05:30.753
something they feel positively about.

02:05:30.753 --> 02:05:32.990
That it isn't something that is attacking them,

02:05:32.990 --> 02:05:35.000
that isn't something that's there to make them feel bad

02:05:35.000 --> 02:05:37.210
to call them a murder or an abuser,

02:05:37.210 --> 02:05:39.420
but it's instead just an interesting concept

02:05:39.420 --> 02:05:41.190
that they actually probably align with.

02:05:41.190 --> 02:05:44.090
And I think that feeling is often just as important

02:05:44.090 --> 02:05:46.360
than the actual points that I made.

02:05:46.360 --> 02:05:48.700
And that's why I try and construct

02:05:48.700 --> 02:05:50.170
the conversations around that.

02:05:50.170 --> 02:05:53.370
It's not about not holding people account either,

02:05:53.370 --> 02:05:54.320
I think that's important.

02:05:54.320 --> 02:05:56.950
You can hold people accountable and question them.

02:05:56.950 --> 02:05:59.770
And in fact, the that by asking people questions

02:05:59.770 --> 02:06:01.490
and by having a more respectful dialogue,

02:06:01.490 --> 02:06:03.260
it allows you to ask questions

02:06:03.260 --> 02:06:04.640
that you may not be able to ask.

02:06:04.640 --> 02:06:05.953
It allows me to say things to people

02:06:05.953 --> 02:06:07.680
that I might not have felt comfortable saying,

02:06:07.680 --> 02:06:09.940
because we've built up an element of trust.

02:06:09.940 --> 02:06:12.395
And they know that my intentions for saying those things

02:06:12.395 --> 02:06:15.140
are not to try and get a click bait title

02:06:15.140 --> 02:06:16.510
and not to try and make them seem silly,

02:06:16.510 --> 02:06:18.270
that my intentions are because I think

02:06:18.270 --> 02:06:21.030
this is an important point and I've built up enough trust,

02:06:21.030 --> 02:06:22.693
hopefully that they don't feel attacked and threatened

02:06:22.693 --> 02:06:25.240
when I say, "Do you think that's hypocritical,

02:06:25.240 --> 02:06:28.150
or do you think that's in misalignment with your morals?

02:06:28.150 --> 02:06:30.000
Or do you think that that's, immoral?"

02:06:30.000 --> 02:06:33.620
These are a big questions and to try and to ask someone

02:06:33.620 --> 02:06:36.790
if they're acting in an immoral way

02:06:36.790 --> 02:06:38.570
is a big question to ask someone.

02:06:38.570 --> 02:06:39.610
But if we can do so in a way

02:06:39.610 --> 02:06:43.140
that is kind of created out of this respectful dialogue,

02:06:43.140 --> 02:06:44.890
it makes that question a lot more palatable

02:06:44.890 --> 02:06:47.150
for someone to hear and to actually go,

02:06:47.150 --> 02:06:48.270
well, actually, yes,

02:06:48.270 --> 02:06:50.300
I think it is immoral on what we do to animals.

02:06:50.300 --> 02:06:52.680
But the important distinction being,

02:06:52.680 --> 02:06:54.030
you're asking questions

02:06:54.030 --> 02:06:57.620
rather than levying judgmental statements

02:06:57.620 --> 02:06:58.940
at the other person.

02:06:58.940 --> 02:07:02.310
And I think the more you engender that trust

02:07:02.310 --> 02:07:05.480
and the manner in which the question is not only asked,

02:07:05.480 --> 02:07:09.270
but formed so that you're not necessarily

02:07:09.270 --> 02:07:10.510
coming at them directly,

02:07:10.510 --> 02:07:12.970
like, do you think you're an immoral person,

02:07:12.970 --> 02:07:16.130
but rather like, how does that decision make you feel

02:07:16.130 --> 02:07:18.150
when you see this other thing or something.

02:07:18.150 --> 02:07:19.960
There's ways of like getting around it

02:07:19.960 --> 02:07:23.540
to have people stress test their own ideas

02:07:25.014 --> 02:07:28.090
without coming at somebody from a judgemental stance.

02:07:28.090 --> 02:07:30.540
And language is such an important part of that.

02:07:31.470 --> 02:07:32.880
Sometimes I'll use words like you,

02:07:32.880 --> 02:07:35.283
but I try and use words like we and us.

02:07:37.599 --> 02:07:39.080
How do we feel about this?

02:07:39.080 --> 02:07:40.380
Or do you think that it's wrong

02:07:40.380 --> 02:07:42.150
when we do these things to animals?

02:07:42.150 --> 02:07:44.300
I mean, I know that I'm not speaking about myself,

02:07:44.300 --> 02:07:46.050
I'm not paying for some of these things to happen

02:07:46.050 --> 02:07:46.883
in the same way,

02:07:46.883 --> 02:07:50.640
but I think using we and us creates a sense of comradery.

02:07:50.640 --> 02:07:53.530
It creates a sense of joint responsibility.

02:07:53.530 --> 02:07:56.620
It doesn't make it about this individual person being bad.

02:07:56.620 --> 02:07:59.670
It's about we as a collective are engaging in bad behaviors

02:07:59.670 --> 02:08:02.480
and we as a collective have responsibility to work together.

02:08:02.480 --> 02:08:04.529
So language is very important

02:08:04.529 --> 02:08:07.874
and using different language techniques

02:08:07.874 --> 02:08:10.000
and using questioning

02:08:10.000 --> 02:08:12.450
and using different methods of communication

02:08:12.450 --> 02:08:15.050
means that we can talk about really important issues

02:08:15.050 --> 02:08:17.090
and really deep philosophical issues

02:08:17.090 --> 02:08:19.167
and what could be very divisive issues,

02:08:19.167 --> 02:08:20.900
but in a way that's actually constructive

02:08:20.900 --> 02:08:24.030
and hopefully that happens.

02:08:24.030 --> 02:08:24.960
I can't sit here and tell you

02:08:24.960 --> 02:08:26.705
that it always works perfectly.

02:08:26.705 --> 02:08:28.060
I can't sit here and tell you

02:08:28.060 --> 02:08:30.780
that I've had people who become upset,

02:08:30.780 --> 02:08:32.590
angry that maybe don't leave feeling

02:08:32.590 --> 02:08:34.890
as positive as I'd like them to, it happens,

02:08:34.890 --> 02:08:37.440
but it's about minimizing how often that happens

02:08:37.440 --> 02:08:38.680
and then reflecting on why it happened.

02:08:38.680 --> 02:08:40.631
What did I say that caused that?

02:08:40.631 --> 02:08:41.870
Could I have said something differently.

02:08:41.870 --> 02:08:45.280
And everything is a kind of a process of trial and error.

02:08:45.280 --> 02:08:46.310
'Cause we'll never be perfect.

02:08:46.310 --> 02:08:47.820
But what we can do is I guess,

02:08:47.820 --> 02:08:49.670
just strive to learn from our mistakes

02:08:49.670 --> 02:08:51.649
and constantly better ourselves.

02:08:51.649 --> 02:08:53.550
You seem like somebody

02:08:53.550 --> 02:08:56.730
who knows how to set healthy boundaries.

02:08:56.730 --> 02:08:58.710
You don't personalize any of this.

02:08:58.710 --> 02:09:02.140
Like you're not letting it penetrate your psyche

02:09:02.140 --> 02:09:03.840
if someone's disagreeing with you

02:09:03.840 --> 02:09:06.640
and I think that's part of why you're good

02:09:06.640 --> 02:09:09.520
at not getting too heated or reactive in these situations.

02:09:09.520 --> 02:09:11.560
And I feel like a lot of people

02:09:11.560 --> 02:09:14.130
who are sensitive, compassionate, people

02:09:14.130 --> 02:09:15.260
who feel strongly about

02:09:15.260 --> 02:09:16.999
whether it's veganism or some issue,

02:09:16.999 --> 02:09:19.670
it's such an emotionally charged thing

02:09:19.670 --> 02:09:22.530
and they can't create that distance

02:09:22.530 --> 02:09:26.690
or that boundary between their emotional sensibility

02:09:26.690 --> 02:09:29.580
and the advocacy, so it spills into it

02:09:29.580 --> 02:09:32.330
and it actually ends up kind of corrupting

02:09:32.330 --> 02:09:34.810
what they're trying to achieve.

02:09:34.810 --> 02:09:36.900
I think what you've said is so important

02:09:36.900 --> 02:09:39.190
and you're right.

02:09:39.190 --> 02:09:41.890
I really try to detach myself

02:09:41.890 --> 02:09:43.630
from what people are justifying.

02:09:43.630 --> 02:09:45.044
If I was to sit there and think,

02:09:45.044 --> 02:09:47.608
oh, this person's saying that plants feel pain,

02:09:47.608 --> 02:09:51.950
therefore, their-
Yeah, we didn't talk about

02:09:51.950 --> 02:09:53.190
the plants feel pain argument.

02:09:53.190 --> 02:09:54.976
I mean, there's like 20 more arguments.

02:09:54.976 --> 02:09:56.489
We could be here for like eight hours.

02:09:56.489 --> 02:10:00.384
We could be. I heard the other day actually,

02:10:00.384 --> 02:10:01.890
something very interesting, someone was saying-

02:10:01.890 --> 02:10:03.380
Plant consciousness.
Yeah.

02:10:03.380 --> 02:10:06.760
They said, what about thyme leaves and rosemary.

02:10:06.760 --> 02:10:10.513
Now that's so it seems so arbitrary

02:10:10.513 --> 02:10:11.510
that the line of conversation-

02:10:11.510 --> 02:10:13.406
It's very specific.
So specific.

02:10:13.406 --> 02:10:14.355
I think the point

02:10:14.355 --> 02:10:17.227
was that we don't need to flavor our foods

02:10:17.227 --> 02:10:20.835
and so rosemary, is it necessary for us to be healthy?

02:10:20.835 --> 02:10:23.450
Exactly, so if rosemary can feel, then we should,

02:10:23.450 --> 02:10:24.540
anyway, we've gone off this weird tangent,

02:10:24.540 --> 02:10:26.790
as you can clearly tell from that point.

02:10:26.790 --> 02:10:28.240
Well, so many of the arguments

02:10:28.240 --> 02:10:32.830
live so far out on the margins as a way to justify behaviors

02:10:32.830 --> 02:10:35.250
because when you hit the bullseye of it,

02:10:35.250 --> 02:10:36.250
it doesn't make sense.

02:10:36.250 --> 02:10:41.090
So we have to live in these weird artificial examples

02:10:42.070 --> 02:10:45.200
that aren't really real life situations.

02:10:45.200 --> 02:10:46.270
That's true. That's true.

02:10:46.270 --> 02:10:48.080
And I have to say to people,

02:10:48.080 --> 02:10:49.150
when they say about plants for pain,

02:10:49.150 --> 02:10:51.880
I say, are you saying that cutting the stalk of a broccoli

02:10:51.880 --> 02:10:53.030
is morally only the same

02:10:53.030 --> 02:10:55.250
as cutting the throat of a pig or a cow.

02:10:55.250 --> 02:10:57.440
Most people realize that that's not the same.

02:10:57.440 --> 02:10:59.670
Some people will try and take me a little bit further

02:10:59.670 --> 02:11:01.110
and they'll go, yeah, that's the same.

02:11:01.110 --> 02:11:03.791
And if I was to think about that

02:11:03.791 --> 02:11:06.110
and to think about what they're justifying,

02:11:06.110 --> 02:11:07.630
it would then be a lot easier,

02:11:07.630 --> 02:11:09.410
as you were saying just before,

02:11:09.410 --> 02:11:12.000
to have that more emotional response.

02:11:12.000 --> 02:11:13.730
And so there is this kind and I guess,

02:11:13.730 --> 02:11:15.300
personal boundary setting,

02:11:15.300 --> 02:11:17.710
there is this kind of detachment that I have

02:11:17.710 --> 02:11:19.775
from thinking too deeply about it,

02:11:19.775 --> 02:11:22.830
because it allows me to, again,

02:11:22.830 --> 02:11:24.650
distance the person from the action.

02:11:24.650 --> 02:11:26.030
Good people can do bad things

02:11:26.030 --> 02:11:28.810
so I need to make sure that I'm creating that distinction.

02:11:28.810 --> 02:11:30.230
They're justifying a bad thing,

02:11:30.230 --> 02:11:31.960
but that doesn't make them a bad person.

02:11:31.960 --> 02:11:33.980
These are all the reasons why they're justifying it.

02:11:33.980 --> 02:11:37.620
Yes, their excuses may seem arbitrary, they are arbitrary.

02:11:37.620 --> 02:11:40.030
Yes, they may seem nonsensical because they are

02:11:40.030 --> 02:11:40.910
but at the same time,

02:11:40.910 --> 02:11:43.620
I can understand that there is this mechanism

02:11:43.620 --> 02:11:45.230
behind their thought process.

02:11:45.230 --> 02:11:49.410
There is this social, cultural, personal experience

02:11:49.410 --> 02:11:51.000
that is leading them to this moment

02:11:51.000 --> 02:11:52.430
where they're now using this excuse.

02:11:52.430 --> 02:11:54.700
And it's not because they're this tech terrible person

02:11:54.700 --> 02:11:56.560
who relishes in animal violence.

02:11:56.560 --> 02:11:58.220
It's, there's all of this baggage

02:11:58.220 --> 02:11:59.350
that has brought us to that point

02:11:59.350 --> 02:12:03.400
and having that understanding helps me just,

02:12:03.400 --> 02:12:05.940
keep that distance from the truth

02:12:05.940 --> 02:12:07.070
of what we're talking about.

02:12:07.070 --> 02:12:09.610
To view it a little bit more intellectually,

02:12:09.610 --> 02:12:12.250
to view it a little bit more as an abstraction

02:12:12.250 --> 02:12:13.900
rather than to think about

02:12:13.900 --> 02:12:15.057
this is what's happening right now

02:12:15.057 --> 02:12:17.360
and this is what they're trying to justify.

02:12:17.360 --> 02:12:18.583
That definitely helps.

02:12:20.140 --> 02:12:22.797
So the book, "This Is Vegan Propaganda"

02:12:22.797 --> 02:12:24.650
you did an amazing job this book.

02:12:24.650 --> 02:12:25.910
Oh, thank you.
This is gonna be

02:12:25.910 --> 02:12:27.630
a perennial bestseller.

02:12:27.630 --> 02:12:28.623
You really,

02:12:31.080 --> 02:12:33.360
knocked it out of the park, I think.

02:12:33.360 --> 02:12:36.730
It follows somewhat the trajectory

02:12:36.730 --> 02:12:38.380
that we spoke about today.

02:12:38.380 --> 02:12:42.470
I mean, essentially you talk about your personal on-ramp

02:12:42.470 --> 02:12:43.540
into this movement

02:12:43.540 --> 02:12:47.230
and you address your general philosophy around veganism

02:12:47.230 --> 02:12:50.019
and then you kind of take (indistinct)

02:12:50.019 --> 02:12:52.420
us through all of the different implications

02:12:52.420 --> 02:12:55.606
of animal agriculture from the philosophical

02:12:55.606 --> 02:12:58.480
and compassionate arguments

02:12:58.480 --> 02:13:00.310
to the environmental implications.

02:13:00.310 --> 02:13:05.310
There's a whole section on pandemics and health, et cetera.

02:13:05.430 --> 02:13:08.723
So I highly recommend everybody check it out.

02:13:10.390 --> 02:13:13.390
It's extremely well crafted and I'm excited for you.

02:13:16.521 --> 02:13:17.731
I'm nervous.

02:13:17.731 --> 02:13:22.031
I wanted to write a book that people would feel confident

02:13:22.031 --> 02:13:24.003
giving to like a non-vegan,

02:13:24.003 --> 02:13:25.631
that kind of like touched on every argument

02:13:25.631 --> 02:13:27.541
and hopefully made a good case

02:13:27.541 --> 02:13:30.031
for all of the different reasons to be vegan.

02:13:30.031 --> 02:13:32.346
But I also wanted to have those personal aspects to it.

02:13:32.346 --> 02:13:37.346
But I'm nervous I think 'cause it is just me,

02:13:37.501 --> 02:13:40.471
it's just my thoughts, my feelings,

02:13:40.471 --> 02:13:41.344
it's such a personal thing to write,

02:13:41.344 --> 02:13:44.211
because you of course will well know yourself

02:13:44.211 --> 02:13:46.801
and making these Surge videos, it's collaboration,

02:13:46.801 --> 02:13:48.871
having a restaurants collaboration, it's all collaboration.

02:13:48.871 --> 02:13:49.861
And of course there's so many people

02:13:49.861 --> 02:13:50.861
that have helped with the book

02:13:50.861 --> 02:13:52.761
from the publishing house and such,

02:13:52.761 --> 02:13:55.071
but having that real personal thing

02:13:55.071 --> 02:13:56.641
that's gonna be out there for people to buy

02:13:56.641 --> 02:13:58.281
and review and judge,

02:13:58.281 --> 02:14:00.841
and I guess also importantly formulate their opinion

02:14:00.841 --> 02:14:04.841
about veganism over, it's quite quite nerve-wracking.

02:14:04.841 --> 02:14:06.261
I'm trying to remember

02:14:06.261 --> 02:14:10.151
if there has been a book about veganism

02:14:10.151 --> 02:14:14.221
that has approached it in this kind of comprehensive way

02:14:14.221 --> 02:14:15.441
in recent memory.

02:14:15.441 --> 02:14:18.101
I mean, it really is, when I gave you a blurb,

02:14:18.101 --> 02:14:19.871
I made this comparison to Peter Singer,

02:14:19.871 --> 02:14:20.941
I don't know how you feel about that,

02:14:20.941 --> 02:14:22.461
but I do think there are parallels

02:14:22.461 --> 02:14:25.741
like you are your generation's version of that.

02:14:25.741 --> 02:14:28.661
Like you have decided to shoulder this mantle,

02:14:28.661 --> 02:14:31.781
this responsibility and this book is a product

02:14:31.781 --> 02:14:34.161
of everything that you've been talking about

02:14:34.161 --> 02:14:35.401
in your videos for some time.

02:14:35.401 --> 02:14:38.554
And it's done in a way where I think,

02:14:38.554 --> 02:14:41.171
obviously it's gonna appeal to people who are vegan,

02:14:41.171 --> 02:14:43.791
but it really is written with this sensibility

02:14:43.791 --> 02:14:46.391
that it's not necessarily intended for those people.

02:14:46.391 --> 02:14:49.261
It's really intended for the vegan person

02:14:49.261 --> 02:14:50.788
who gives it to their friend or says,

02:14:50.788 --> 02:14:52.251
"Maybe you should check this out."

02:14:52.251 --> 02:14:55.841
And it it's very welcoming in that kind of tone and tenor.

02:14:55.841 --> 02:14:57.831
Oh good. That's exactly what I wanted.

02:14:57.831 --> 02:15:00.961
I wanted a book that was a book that vegans could read

02:15:00.961 --> 02:15:02.491
and they would probably learn things

02:15:02.491 --> 02:15:03.461
maybe they didn't already know about.

02:15:03.461 --> 02:15:06.638
So it was a good tool for them to become better advocates,

02:15:06.638 --> 02:15:08.611
but I always wanted it to be a book

02:15:08.611 --> 02:15:10.378
that was for a non-vegan to read,

02:15:10.378 --> 02:15:12.591
that was for that reluctant family member,

02:15:12.591 --> 02:15:14.551
that skeptical friend,

02:15:14.551 --> 02:15:16.801
or even just the curious person in the bookshop

02:15:16.801 --> 02:15:20.311
and the point of the title I suppose, is to be grabbing,

02:15:20.311 --> 02:15:22.292
to kind of be a little bit tongue and cheek.

02:15:22.292 --> 02:15:24.091
So people kind of raise an eyebrow,

02:15:24.091 --> 02:15:26.951
the cover it's quite bright, it's a bit different.

02:15:26.951 --> 02:15:29.171
It has this kind of like kind of Soviet era.

02:15:29.171 --> 02:15:30.671
Soviet era.
Yeah, exactly.

02:15:30.671 --> 02:15:32.261
It's all propaganda.
All propaganda.

02:15:32.261 --> 02:15:33.875
The whole thing.
Yeah, exactly.

02:15:33.875 --> 02:15:35.761
Toss it in the garbage.

02:15:35.761 --> 02:15:38.243
Exactly, it's not worth the paper its printed on.

02:15:38.243 --> 02:15:40.758
But we really wanted to play with the idea

02:15:40.758 --> 02:15:41.981
of it being this kind of propaganda piece

02:15:41.981 --> 02:15:44.118
so that people could read it and go,

02:15:44.118 --> 02:15:46.841
"Oh, this, these industries are calling propaganda.

02:15:46.841 --> 02:15:50.101
Oh, okay, now I understand why they call it propaganda."

02:15:50.101 --> 02:15:52.891
And everything's extensively referenced,

02:15:52.891 --> 02:15:53.821
there are citations.

02:15:53.821 --> 02:15:56.811
And I just wanted to kind of draw people's awareness

02:15:56.811 --> 02:15:59.931
to the fact that when farmers are accusing

02:15:59.931 --> 02:16:01.831
or when the industry are accusing vegans

02:16:01.831 --> 02:16:03.271
of spreading propaganda,

02:16:03.271 --> 02:16:04.641
they're not attacking vegans,

02:16:04.641 --> 02:16:06.611
they're attacking the scientific literature,

02:16:06.611 --> 02:16:08.581
they're attacking our moral compasses.

02:16:08.581 --> 02:16:10.541
They're attacking something more objective

02:16:10.541 --> 02:16:12.451
than this vegan bias

02:16:12.451 --> 02:16:15.181
that can sometimes be perceived as existing.

02:16:15.181 --> 02:16:19.541
And it's really an attack on so much more than just vegans.

02:16:19.541 --> 02:16:21.201
And I think I want people to read the book

02:16:21.201 --> 02:16:23.128
and hopefully reach the end and go,

02:16:23.128 --> 02:16:24.621
"Okay, I understand that now,

02:16:24.621 --> 02:16:26.291
I understand why they call it propaganda.

02:16:26.291 --> 02:16:30.051
And potentially there is another side to this story."

02:16:30.051 --> 02:16:32.561
And the way the book is structured,

02:16:32.561 --> 02:16:34.911
having the first section about ethics and morals

